defmodule AlchemGuideData do
  # Generated by scripts/generate-guide-data.py from what the pinned
  # command line prints; edit nothing here.

  def commands(): string do
    "build\u{1F}alchem build [options] <entry.alc>\u{1F}Compile a program to a native executable. The entry may be a file of a package, written as an import writes it.\u{1F}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured compile diagnostics\u{1D}locked\u{1C}\u{1C}fail instead of updating alchem.lock\u{1D}max-call-depth\u{1C}100000\u{1C}nested Alchem calls allowed per process\u{1D}o\u{1C}\u{1C}output executable\u{1D}race\u{1C}\u{1C}enable Go race detection in generated executable\u{1D}shutdown-timeout\u{1C}2s\u{1C}runtime shutdown deadline\u{1D}target\u{1C}\u{1C}target GOOS/GOARCH\u{1D}timeout\u{1C}2m0s\u{1C}build deadline\u{1D}trace\u{1C}true\u{1C}include Alchem runtime frames\u{1F}Usage of build:\n  -json\n    \temit structured compile diagnostics\n  -locked\n    \tfail instead of updating alchem.lock\n  -max-call-depth int\n    \tnested Alchem calls allowed per process (default 100000)\n  -o string\n    \toutput executable\n  -race\n    \tenable Go race detection in generated executable\n  -shutdown-timeout duration\n    \truntime shutdown deadline (default 2s)\n  -target string\n    \ttarget GOOS/GOARCH\n  -timeout duration\n    \tbuild deadline (default 2m0s)\n  -trace\n    \tinclude Alchem runtime frames (default true)\n\u{1E}run\u{1F}alchem run [options] <entry.alc> [-- arguments]\u{1F}Build a program and run it with the arguments that follow. The entry may be a file of a package: host/owner/repo@v1.2.3/tools/name.alc.\u{1F}locked\u{1C}\u{1C}fail instead of updating alchem.lock\u{1D}max-call-depth\u{1C}100000\u{1C}nested Alchem calls allowed per process\u{1D}race\u{1C}\u{1C}enable Go race detection in the program\u{1D}shutdown-timeout\u{1C}2s\u{1C}runtime shutdown deadline\u{1D}timeout\u{1C}2m0s\u{1C}build deadline\u{1F}Usage of run:\n  -locked\n    \tfail instead of updating alchem.lock\n  -max-call-depth int\n    \tnested Alchem calls allowed per process (default 100000)\n  -race\n    \tenable Go race detection in the program\n  -shutdown-timeout duration\n    \truntime shutdown deadline (default 2s)\n  -timeout duration\n    \tbuild deadline (default 2m0s)\n\u{1E}alx\u{1F}alchem alx\u{1F}alx, the Alchem Language Executor: an interactive shell where each input is compiled and run with the definitions and statements before it.\u{1F}\u{1F}Usage of alx:\n\u{1E}lsp\u{1F}alchem lsp\u{1F}A language server over standard input and output: diagnostics, hover documentation, completion, definitions, symbols and formatting.\u{1F}\u{1F}Usage of lsp:\n\u{1E}new\u{1F}alchem new <directory>\u{1F}Create a project: a library module, its test and a main program.\u{1F}\u{1F}Usage of new:\n\u{1E}check\u{1F}alchem check [options] <entry.alc>\u{1F}Report compile errors without building.\u{1F}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured diagnostics\u{1D}locked\u{1C}\u{1C}fail instead of updating alchem.lock\u{1F}Usage of check:\n  -json\n    \temit structured diagnostics\n  -locked\n    \tfail instead of updating alchem.lock\n\u{1E}fmt\u{1F}alchem fmt [--check] <path>...\u{1F}Format .alc files in place.\u{1F}check\u{1C}\u{1C}check formatting without writing\u{1F}Usage of fmt:\n  -check\n    \tcheck formatting without writing\n\u{1E}test\u{1F}alchem test [options] <path>...\u{1F}Run *_test.alc files, with seeds, tags and coverage.\u{1F}cover\u{1C}\u{1C}report line coverage of the code under test\u{1D}cover-min\u{1C}\u{1C}fail if total coverage is below this percentage\u{1D}exclude\u{1C}\u{1C}leave out tests carrying one of these comma-separated tags\u{1D}jobs\u{1C}\u{1C}test files compiled and run at once; 0 means one per processor\u{1D}max-failures\u{1C}\u{1C}stop a test file after this many failures\u{1D}only\u{1C}\u{1C}run only tests whose name or module contains this text\u{1D}receive-timeout\u{1C}100ms\u{1C}how long assert_receive and refute_receive wait by default\u{1D}seed\u{1C}\u{1C}seed for test order and random values; 0 picks one\u{1D}tag\u{1C}\u{1C}run only tests carrying one of these comma-separated tags\u{1D}timeout\u{1C}30s\u{1C}deadline for one fixture, or one test\u{1D}trace\u{1C}\u{1C}print each test with its duration\u{1F}Usage of test:\n  -cover\n    \treport line coverage of the code under test\n  -cover-min float\n    \tfail if total coverage is below this percentage\n  -exclude string\n    \tleave out tests carrying one of these comma-separated tags\n  -jobs int\n    \ttest files compiled and run at once; 0 means one per processor\n  -max-failures int\n    \tstop a test file after this many failures\n  -only string\n    \trun only tests whose name or module contains this text\n  -receive-timeout duration\n    \thow long assert_receive and refute_receive wait by default (default 100ms)\n  -seed uint\n    \tseed for test order and random values; 0 picks one\n  -tag string\n    \trun only tests carrying one of these comma-separated tags\n  -timeout duration\n    \tdeadline for one fixture, or one test (default 30s)\n  -trace\n    \tprint each test with its duration\n\u{1E}syntax\u{1F}alchem syntax [--all] [--group name] [--json] [topic]\u{1F}Explain a language construct, with a working example; without a topic, list them.\u{1F}all\u{1C}\u{1C}print every topic\u{1D}group\u{1C}\u{1C}list the topics of one group, such as data or control-flow\u{1D}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured output\u{1F}Usage of syntax:\n  -all\n    \tprint every topic\n  -group string\n    \tlist the topics of one group, such as data or control-flow\n  -json\n    \temit structured output\n\u{1E}doc\u{1F}alchem doc [--all] [--group name] [--search text] [--json] [Module | Module.function | file.alc]\u{1F}Show library documentation, or the documentation written in a file, in a file of a package (host/owner/repo@v1.2.3/lib/name.alc), or in a module of the project in the current directory and the packages it imports.\u{1F}all\u{1C}\u{1C}print every function of the module in full\u{1D}group\u{1C}\u{1C}show the modules of one group, such as text or files-and-system\u{1D}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured output\u{1D}search\u{1C}\u{1C}find functions, modules and topics containing this text\u{1F}Usage of doc:\n  -all\n    \tprint every function of the module in full\n  -group string\n    \tshow the modules of one group, such as text or files-and-system\n  -json\n    \temit structured output\n  -search string\n    \tfind functions, modules and topics containing this text\n\u{1E}agent-guide\u{1F}alchem agent-guide [--json]\u{1F}Print the guide for coding agents: commands, topics, modules and how to learn more.\u{1F}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured output\u{1F}Usage of agent-guide:\n  -json\n    \temit structured output\n\u{1E}agent-install\u{1F}alchem agent-install [--scope project|user] [--dir path] [--force] [--json] <agent> | --list\u{1F}Install a short instruction file that points a coding agent at agent-guide.\u{1F}dir\u{1C}\u{1C}directory to install into instead of the one the scope implies\u{1D}force\u{1C}\u{1C}replace an existing file that differs\u{1D}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured output\u{1D}list\u{1C}\u{1C}list the supported agents\u{1D}scope\u{1C}project\u{1C}install for this project, or for the user\u{1F}Usage of agent-install:\n  -dir string\n    \tdirectory to install into instead of the one the scope implies\n  -force\n    \treplace an existing file that differs\n  -json\n    \temit structured output\n  -list\n    \tlist the supported agents\n  -scope string\n    \tinstall for this project, or for the user (default \"project\")\n\u{1E}docs\u{1F}alchem docs [--check] [--dir path]\u{1F}Write the generated reference files; --check fails if they are out of date.\u{1F}check\u{1C}\u{1C}fail if a generated file is out of date, without writing\u{1D}dir\u{1C}docs\u{1C}directory the reference files are written to\u{1F}Usage of docs:\n  -check\n    \tfail if a generated file is out of date, without writing\n  -dir string\n    \tdirectory the reference files are written to (default \"docs\")\n\u{1E}version\u{1F}alchem version [--json]\u{1F}Print the compiler's version and the Go toolchain builds use.\u{1F}json\u{1C}\u{1C}emit structured output\u{1F}Usage of version:\n  -json\n    \temit structured output\n"
  end

  def differences(): string do
    "Statically typed: parameters carry types (`def add(a: integer, b: integer): integer do`), checked before anything runs.\u{1E}No `nil`, and `{:ok, value}` is not the convention: use `option[T]` (`some(x)` / `none`) and `result[T, E]` (`ok(x)` / `error(reason)`). Atoms exist as the type `atom`; a fixed set of alternatives is better as a sum type (`alchem syntax sum-types`).\u{1E}Library functions return `option` or `result` where Elixir returns `nil` or `{:ok, _}` tuples: `Map.get(map, key)` is an option, `Integer.parse(text)` a result.\u{1E}Bindings are immutable and cannot be rebound: `x = 1` then `x = 2` is an error; choose a new name.\u{1E}One module per file; other files are brought in with `import \"file.alc\"`, not `alias`, `use` or `require`.\u{1E}No behaviours, charlists, binaries pattern matching or `receive do` blocks. Protocols are resolved when the program is compiled (`alchem syntax protocols`), and macros are templates that substitute their arguments (`alchem syntax macros`): no code runs inside the compiler.\u{1E}Integers are 64-bit and overflow is an error; `1 + 1.5` is a type error; integer `/` truncates.\u{1E}Failures carry a string message: `raise(\"message\")`, rescued by matching the text. There are no exception structs.\u{1E}Map keys are integers, strings, booleans or atoms, and a map's keys share one type, as do its values. Use structs for records.\u{1E}Processes have typed mailboxes (`pid[T]`); `Server`, `Task` and `Agent` cover what GenServer, Task and Agent do.\u{1E}Nodes connect with `Node.start` and `Node.connect`, and messages between them are copied (`alchem syntax nodes`); a process is reached by a registered name, not by an Erlang-style pid from another system.\u{1E}Hex packages do not work. Go packages do, through `extern`."
  end

  def topics(): string do
    "program\u{1F}Basics\u{1F}A file is one module; the entry module has a main function.\u{1F}A file holds exactly one defmodule with an uppercase name.\u{1D}The entry module has exactly one main/0; imported modules have none.\u{1D}alchem build main.alc compiles to a native executable; alchem check only reports errors.\u{1D}The whole file is type-checked before anything runs, including unused functions.\u{1F}functions\u{1D}modules\u{1D}tests\u{1F}a complete program\u{1C}defmodule Hello do\n  def greeting(name: string): string do\n    \"hello, \#{name}\"\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    IO.puts(greeting(\"world\"))\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}values\u{1F}Basics\u{1F}Integers, floats, booleans and strings.\u{1F}integer is signed 64-bit and overflow fails at run time; float is 64-bit.\u{1D}A float literal needs digits on both sides of the point: 1.0, not 1.\u{1D}There is no implicit conversion: 1 + 1.5 is a type error. Use Float.from_integer, round, trunc.\u{1D}A float in interpolation or to_string is its shortest text, in exponent form when that is shorter: 1000.0 is \"1.0e3\" and 1250.0 is \"1250.0\". inspect keeps plain digits.\u{1D}There is no nil: use option and result. Atoms are a type of their own (see atoms).\u{1F}operators\u{1D}strings\u{1D}option-result\u{1F}literals\u{1C}count = 42\nprice = 9.5\nready = true\nname = \"ada\"\nassert(count + 1 == 43)\nassert(price * 2.0 == 19.0)\nassert(ready and name == \"ada\")\nassert(Float.from_integer(count) + 0.5 == 42.5)\n\u{1E}bindings\u{1F}Basics\u{1F}name = expression binds a name once; bindings are immutable.\u{1F}A name cannot be rebound or shadowed in the same function: pick a new name.\u{1D}An annotation gives the type where the value alone does not: xs: list[integer] = [].\u{1D}Bindings made inside a branch do not escape it.\u{1D}The left side may be a pattern (see patterns).\u{1F}patterns\u{1D}values\u{1F}bindings and annotations\u{1C}total = 2 + 3 * 4\ndoubled = total * 2\nempty: list[string] = []\nmissing: option[integer] = none\nassert(doubled == 28)\nassert(length(empty) == 0)\nassert(missing == none)\n\u{1E}operators\u{1F}Basics\u{1F}Arithmetic, comparison, boolean, string and list operators.\u{1F}Arithmetic: + - * / on two integers or two floats; integer / truncates; % and rem are integer remainder; ** is power.\u{1D}Comparison: == != < <= > >=. == compares lists, tuples, maps, structs and sum types structurally.\u{1D}Boolean: and, or, not (also && || !); operands must be booleans.\u{1D}Strings: <> concatenates; text =~ ~r/pattern/ tests a regular expression.\u{1D}Lists: ++ concatenates, -- removes, value in enumerable tests membership.\u{1D}first..last is an inclusive range; first..last//step sets the step.\u{1D}|> passes the left value as the first argument of the call on the right.\u{1F}pipes\u{1D}ranges\u{1D}strings\u{1F}operators\u{1C}assert(7 / 2 == 3 and 7 % 2 == 1)\nassert(2 ** 10 == 1024)\nassert(7.0 / 2.0 == 3.5)\nassert(\"al\" <> \"chem\" == \"alchem\")\nassert([1, 2] ++ [3] == [1, 2, 3])\nassert([1, 2, 3, 2] -- [2] == [1, 3, 2])\nassert(2 in [1, 2, 3] and not (5 in 1..3))\nassert(\"order 42\" =~ ~r/\\d+/)\nassert({1, \"a\"} == {1, \"a\"})\n\u{1E}strings\u{1F}Basics\u{1F}UTF-8 text with interpolation, heredocs and the String module.\u{1F}\"\#{expression}\" interpolates a string, integer, float or boolean; write \\\#{ for the literal characters.\u{1D}Escapes: \\n, \\t, \\r, \\e (escape), \\s (space), \\\\, \\\", \\xHH for a byte, and \\uHHHH, \\UHHHHHHHH or \\u{H...} for a Unicode code point, as in \"caf\\u{e9}\".\u{1D}A heredoc starts with \"\"\" at the end of a line and ends at the next line that begins with \"\"\"; the indentation of the closing quotes is removed from every line. What follows the closing quotes continues the expression, so a heredoc can be an argument, closed with \"\"\").\u{1D}A heredoc has escapes and \#{...} interpolation, as a string does; ~S\"\"\" starts one that is taken literally, as documentation with examples usually is.\u{1D}inspect(value) renders any value as text; to_string renders the four primitive types.\u{1F}String\u{1D}Regex\u{1D}sigils\u{1F}interpolation and concatenation\u{1C}name = \"ada\"\nage = 36\nline = \"\#{name} is \#{age}\"\nassert(line == \"ada is 36\")\nassert(String.upcase(name) <> \"!\" == \"ADA!\")\nassert(inspect([1, 2]) == \"[1, 2]\")\n\u{1D}heredoc\u{1C}text = \"\"\"\n  first line\n    indented\n  \"\"\"\nassert(text == \"first line\\n  indented\\n\")\n\u{1E}comments\u{1F}Basics\u{1F}\# comments, and @moduledoc, @doc and @typedoc documentation.\u{1F}\# starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.\u{1D}@moduledoc documents the module, @doc the next function, @typedoc the next struct or type. Each takes a string or heredoc.\u{1D}alx> lines inside @doc are examples; a test file runs them with doctest Module (see doctests).\u{1D}alchem doc file.alc prints a file's documentation.\u{1F}doctests\u{1D}strings\u{1F}a documented module\u{1C}defmodule Temperature do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Temperature conversion.\n  \"\"\"\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Converts Celsius to Fahrenheit.\n\n      alx> Temperature.to_fahrenheit(100.0)\n      212.0\n  \"\"\"\n  def to_fahrenheit(celsius: float): float do\n    \# nine fifths, plus the offset\n    celsius * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(to_fahrenheit(0.0) == 32.0)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}functions\u{1F}Functions\u{1F}def name(parameter: type): result do ... end.\u{1F}Parameters carry types; the result type follows the parentheses and may be left out when a first clause or the body fixes it.\u{1D}The last expression is the result; return value leaves early.\u{1D}defp defines a function private to its module.\u{1D}A function that returns nothing omits the result type.\u{1D}Names may end in ? or !. Recursion is the loop; Process.loop and Enum cover the rest.\u{1D}A function may be named put, get, has_key, length, map, filter, reduce, prepend or concat, as library functions are. In the module that defines it a bare call then means that function, and the builtin is written Map.put, Enum.map and so on; in other modules it is called Module.put and the bare name keeps its meaning.\u{1D}&name refers to a named function as a value.\u{1D}name: type \\\\ value gives a trailing parameter a default, which a call may leave out. The value is a constant: literals, lists, tuples and maps of them, none, some(...), ok(...) or error(...).\u{1D}Definitions of one name with different numbers of parameters are different functions: size/1 and size/2. A call takes the one its number of arguments fits.\u{1D}A function may also be defined for arguments of different types: def show(n: integer) and def show(text: string) are two forms of show/1, each with its own body and result type, and a call takes the first form its arguments fit. A reference, &show/1, is the form the expected function type names.\u{1F}clauses\u{1D}anonymous-functions\u{1D}generics\u{1F}definitions and calls\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def area(width: float, height: float): float do\n    width * height\n  end\n\n  def sign(n: integer): string do\n    if n < 0 do\n      return \"negative\"\n    end\n    \"non-negative\"\n  end\n\n  defp double(n: integer): integer do\n    n * 2\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(area(2.0, 3.0) == 6.0)\n    assert(sign(-1) == \"negative\")\n    assert(Enum.map([1, 2], &double) == [2, 4])\n  end\nend\n\u{1D}default arguments\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def greet(name: string, greeting: string \\\\ \"hello\", marks: integer \\\\ 1): string do\n    \"\#{greeting}, \#{name}\" <> String.duplicate(\"!\", marks)\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(greet(\"ada\") == \"hello, ada!\")\n    assert(greet(\"ada\", \"hi\") == \"hi, ada!\")\n    assert(greet(\"ada\", \"hi\", 3) == \"hi, ada!!!\")\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}clauses\u{1F}Functions\u{1F}Several definitions of one function, chosen by pattern and guard.\u{1F}Clauses are tried top to bottom; parameters may be patterns and literals.\u{1D}The first clause gives the types; later clauses may leave them out.\u{1D}when adds a guard, a boolean expression over the parameters.\u{1D}Clauses of one function must be adjacent and have the same number of parameters; a definition with another number of parameters is another function, as in Elixir: area/1 and area/2.\u{1D}Together the clauses must cover every input: end with a catch-all when guards leave gaps.\u{1F}patterns\u{1D}functions\u{1F}pattern-matching clauses\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def fact(0): integer do\n    1\n  end\n\n  def fact(n: integer) when n > 0 do\n    n * fact(n - 1)\n  end\n\n  def fact(_) do\n    raise(\"fact needs a non-negative number\")\n  end\n\n  def sum([]: list[integer]): integer do\n    0\n  end\n\n  def sum([head | tail]) do\n    head + sum(tail)\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(fact(5) == 120)\n    assert(sum([1, 2, 3]) == 6)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}anonymous-functions\u{1F}Functions\u{1F}fn x -> body end, captures with &, and closures.\u{1F}fn x, y -> body end takes its types from where it is used: an argument, an annotated binding or a return.\u{1D}Where nothing gives the types, annotate: fn(x: integer): integer do x + 1 end.\u{1D}&(&1 + &2) and &String.length(&1) are short forms; &name refers to a named function.\u{1D}A parameter may be a pattern: fn {key, value} -> ... end.\u{1D}With clauses on the lines after fn, each pattern -> body with an optional guard, the function takes one argument and tries them in order; match a tuple for several values.\u{1D}A function value is called like a function, f(1), or as in Elixir, f.(1). Its type is fn[argument, ..., result].\u{1D}Functions capture the bindings around them, which never change.\u{1F}functions\u{1D}Enum\u{1F}function values\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def adder(offset: integer): fn[integer, integer] do\n    fn value -> value + offset end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    add_ten = adder(10)\n    assert(add_ten(5) == 15)\n    assert(Enum.map([1, 2, 3], fn n -> n * n end) == [1, 4, 9])\n    assert(Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, &(&1 + &2)) == 6)\n    assert(Enum.map([\"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1)) == [1, 2])\n    square = fn(n: integer): integer do n * n end\n    assert(square(4) == 16)\n    pairs = Enum.map(%{\"a\" => 1}, fn {key, value} -> \"\#{key}:\#{value}\" end)\n    assert(pairs == [\"a:1\"])\n  end\nend\n\u{1D}clauses\u{1C}labels = Enum.map([0, -3, 7], fn\n  0 -> \"zero\"\n  n when n < 0 -> \"negative\"\n  _ -> \"positive\"\nend)\nassert(labels == [\"zero\", \"negative\", \"positive\"])\nareas = Enum.map([{2, 3}, {4, 0}], fn\n  {_, 0} -> 0\n  {width, height} -> width * height\nend)\nassert(areas == [6, 0])\n\u{1E}generics\u{1F}Functions\u{1F}Lowercase type names are type variables.\u{1F}A function is generic in the type variables of its parameters; each call picks the types.\u{1D}Inside the function a type variable's values can be passed, stored, returned and compared, but not used in arithmetic.\u{1D}Structs and sum types take parameters in brackets: struct Pair[a, b], type Tree[t].\u{1D}A misspelled type name is therefore read as a type variable and reported where the value is first used.\u{1F}functions\u{1D}structs\u{1D}sum-types\u{1F}a generic function and type\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  struct Pair[a, b] do\n    left: a\n    right: b\n  end\n\n  def swap(pair: Pair[a, b]): Pair[b, a] do\n    %Pair{left: pair.right, right: pair.left}\n  end\n\n  def first_or(items: list[t], fallback: t): t do\n    case items do\n      [] -> fallback\n      [head | _] -> head\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    swapped = swap(%Pair{left: 1, right: \"one\"})\n    assert(swapped.left == \"one\" and swapped.right == 1)\n    assert(first_or([\"a\"], \"z\") == \"a\")\n    assert(first_or(Enum.take([1], 0), 9) == 9)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}if\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}if condition do ... else ... end, as a statement or a value.\u{1F}The condition must be a boolean; there is no truthiness.\u{1D}As a value both branches must end in the same type, and else is required.\u{1D}There is no else if: nest, or use cond.\u{1D}unless condition do ... end runs its body when the condition is false; it may have an else and be used as a value.\u{1D}On one line: if condition, do: value, else: value, and the same for unless.\u{1F}cond\u{1D}case\u{1F}statement and value\u{1C}count = 3\nlabel = if count > 0 do \"some\" else \"none\" end\nassert(label == \"some\")\nif count > 2 do\n  IO.puts(\"many\")\nelse\n  IO.puts(\"few\")\nend\n\u{1D}unless\u{1C}items = [1, 2]\nunless items == [] do\n  IO.puts(\"has items\")\nend\nstate = unless length(items) > 5 do \"short\" else \"long\" end\nassert(state == \"short\")\n\u{1D}one line\u{1C}n = 1\nnoun = if n == 1, do: \"item\", else: \"items\"\nassert(noun == \"item\")\nif n > 0, do: IO.puts(\"positive\")\n\u{1E}case\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}Matches a value against patterns, with optional guards.\u{1F}Arms are pattern -> body, tried in order; _ matches anything.\u{1D}when adds a guard to an arm.\u{1D}A case over a sum type, option or result must cover every variant or end in a catch-all.\u{1D}As a value every arm must end in the same type.\u{1F}patterns\u{1D}sum-types\u{1D}option-result\u{1F}matching lists, tuples and results\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def describe(items: list[integer]): string do\n    case items do\n      [] -> \"empty\"\n      [only] -> \"one: \#{only}\"\n      [first | _] when first < 0 -> \"starts negative\"\n      _ -> \"many\"\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(describe([7]) == \"one: 7\")\n    assert(describe([-1, 2]) == \"starts negative\")\n    parsed = case Integer.parse(\"42\") do\n      ok(n) -> n\n      error(_) -> 0\n    end\n    assert(parsed == 42)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}cond\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}Runs the first branch whose condition is true.\u{1F}Arms are condition -> body; end with true -> for the default.\u{1D}Without a default, a cond in which nothing is true fails with no cond clause matched.\u{1F}if\u{1D}case\u{1F}cond as a value\u{1C}n = 0\nlabel = cond do\n  n < 0 -> \"negative\"\n  n == 0 -> \"zero\"\n  true -> \"positive\"\nend\nassert(label == \"zero\")\n\u{1E}patterns\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}Destructuring in bindings, case arms, function parameters, fn parameters, with and for.\u{1F}Tuples {a, b}; lists [], [a, b], [head | tail]; maps %{\"key\" => value}; structs %Name{field: value}; sum variants Shape.circle(r); some(x), none, ok(x), error(e).\u{1D}Literals match themselves; _ ignores; ^name matches the value of an existing binding instead of binding.\u{1D}A map or struct pattern matches when the listed keys or fields match; others are ignored.\u{1D}A binding whose pattern does not match fails with pattern match failed.\u{1F}case\u{1D}clauses\u{1D}with\u{1D}for\u{1F}destructuring\u{1C}{count, label} = {3, \"ok\"}\n[first | rest] = [1, 2, 3]\nassert(count == 3 and label == \"ok\")\nassert(first == 1 and rest == [2, 3])\nexpected = 3\nmatched = case {3, \"x\"} do\n  {^expected, text} -> text\n  _ -> \"other\"\nend\nassert(matched == \"x\")\nfound = case %{\"count\" => 4} do\n  %{\"count\" => n} when n > 0 -> n\n  _ -> 0\nend\nassert(found == 4)\n\u{1E}pipes\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}value |> function(arguments) passes the value as the first argument.\u{1F}Each step is a call; the piped value becomes its first argument.\u{1D}then(value, fn) pipes into a function value; tap(value, fn) runs an effect and passes the value on.\u{1F}Enum\u{1D}Stream\u{1F}a pipeline\u{1C}total = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n  |> Enum.filter(&(rem(&1, 2) == 0))\n  |> Enum.map(&(&1 * &1))\n  |> Enum.sum()\nassert(total == 56)\nassert((\"  alchem  \" |> String.trim() |> String.upcase()) == \"ALCHEM\")\n\u{1E}with\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}Chains pattern matches, stopping at the first that fails.\u{1F}Each clause is pattern <- expression; bindings are visible to later clauses and the body.\u{1D}Without else, the first value that does not match becomes the result, so error(reason) and none pass straight through.\u{1D}With else, that value is matched against the else arms.\u{1F}option-result\u{1D}patterns\u{1D}try\u{1F}chaining results\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def parse_point(text: string): result[tuple[integer, integer], string] do\n    with [left, right] <- String.split(text, \",\"),\n         ok(x) <- Integer.parse(left),\n         ok(y) <- Integer.parse(right) do\n      ok({x, y})\n    else\n      error(reason) -> error(\"bad number: \#{reason}\")\n      parts -> error(\"expected 2 parts, got \#{length(parts)}\")\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(parse_point(\"3,4\") == ok({3, 4}))\n    assert(parse_point(\"3\") == error(\"expected 2 parts, got 1\"))\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}for\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}Comprehensions: generators and filters producing a list.\u{1F}for pattern <- enumerable, filter, do: expression — or a do ... end block.\u{1D}Several generators nest; later ones see earlier bindings.\u{1D}Elements that do not match a generator's pattern are skipped.\u{1D}Without an option the result is a list. A body that ends in IO.puts or another call with no value gives {} for each element, as Elixir's gives :ok. One option may follow the generators and filters.\u{1D}into: collectable adds the values to a map (from {key, value} tuples), a set, a list or a string; an empty %{} or [] takes its type from the values.\u{1D}uniq: true drops values already produced.\u{1D}reduce: initial takes a do block of accumulator -> body arms; the body's value is the next accumulator and the last one is the result. An empty initial value such as %{} needs a type from an annotation.\u{1F}Enum\u{1D}ranges\u{1D}patterns\u{1F}generators and filters\u{1C}squares = for n <- 1..5, do: n * n\nassert(squares == [1, 4, 9, 16, 25])\npairs = for x <- [1, 2, 3], y <- [1, 2, 3], x < y do\n  {x, y}\nend\nassert(pairs == [{1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}])\npresent = for some(n) <- [some(1), none, some(3)], do: n\nassert(present == [1, 3])\n\u{1D}into, uniq and reduce\u{1C}squares = for n <- 1..3, into: %{}, do: {n, n * n}\nassert(squares == %{1 => 1, 2 => 4, 3 => 9})\ninitials = for word <- [\"bee\", \"ant\", \"bat\"], uniq: true, do: String.slice(word, 0, 1)\nassert(initials == [\"b\", \"a\"])\ntotal = for x <- [1, 2, 3], x != 2, reduce: 0 do\n  acc -> acc + x\nend\nassert(total == 4)\ncounts: map[string, integer] = for word <- [\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"], reduce: %{} do\n  seen -> Map.update(seen, word, 1, &(&1 + 1))\nend\nassert(counts == %{\"a\" => 2, \"b\" => 1})\n\u{1E}try\u{1F}Control flow\u{1F}try / rescue / catch / else / after, raise, throw and defexception.\u{1F}raise(message) fails the current process with a message; every runtime failure has one.\u{1D}defexception Name do message: string ... end declares an exception, a struct with a message field; raise(%Name{...}) raises it.\u{1D}rescue arms match a failure: e in Name binds the exception, %Name{field: x} matches its fields, and a string literal or a name matches the message of any failure.\u{1D}throw(value) unwinds to the nearest catch arm that takes the value's type; the type comes from the throws in the body, from the arm's pattern, or from value: Type ->.\u{1D}else arms match the body's value when nothing was raised or thrown; a failure in an else arm is not rescued by the same try.\u{1D}after runs on every way out; its value is discarded. return cannot be used inside try.\u{1D}Prefer result and option for expected failures; try is for the unexpected, and throw for leaving a loop early.\u{1F}option-result\u{1D}with\u{1F}rescuing a failure\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def safe_div(a: integer, b: integer): result[integer, string] do\n    try do\n      ok(a / b)\n    rescue\n      \"division by zero\" -> error(\"cannot divide by zero\")\n      message -> error(message)\n    after\n      IO.puts(\"divided\")\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(safe_div(6, 3) == ok(2))\n    assert(safe_div(1, 0) == error(\"cannot divide by zero\"))\n    message = try do\n      raise(\"boom\")\n    rescue\n      text -> text\n    end\n    assert(message == \"boom\")\n  end\nend\n\u{1D}exceptions, throw and else\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  defexception NotFound do\n    message: string\n    id: integer\n  end\n\n  def fetch(id: integer): string do\n    if id > 9 do\n      raise(%NotFound{message: \"no such item\", id: id})\n    end\n    \"item\"\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    missing = try do\n      fetch(42)\n    rescue\n      e in NotFound -> \"missing \" <> to_string(e.id)\n    end\n    assert(missing == \"missing 42\")\n    first = try do\n      Enum.each([1, 7, 9], fn n -> if n > 5 do throw(n) end end)\n      0\n    catch\n      n -> n\n    end\n    assert(first == 7)\n    parsed = try do\n      Integer.parse(\"12\")\n    rescue\n      _ -> 0\n    else\n      ok(n) -> n\n      error(_) -> -1\n    end\n    assert(parsed == 12)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}lists\u{1F}Data\u{1F}list[T]: immutable, written [1, 2, 3].\u{1F}An empty list needs a type from an annotation or its use: xs: list[integer] = [].\u{1D}[head | tail] is a pattern; build with prepend(list, value), ++ or Enum.\u{1D}list[index] reads by zero-based position and fails out of range; Enum.at returns an option.\u{1D}length(list), hd(list), tl(list). The Enum module works on every enumerable; List has positional updates.\u{1F}Enum\u{1D}List\u{1D}for\u{1D}patterns\u{1F}building and reading lists\u{1C}numbers = [3, 1, 2]\nassert(numbers[0] == 3 and length(numbers) == 3)\nassert(prepend(numbers, 0) == [0, 3, 1, 2])\nassert(Enum.sort(numbers) == [1, 2, 3])\nassert(Enum.at(numbers, 9) == none)\nnested: list[list[integer]] = [[], [1, 2]]\nassert(nested[1][0] == 1)\n\u{1E}tuples\u{1F}Data\u{1F}tuple[A, B]: a fixed group of values, written {a, b}.\u{1F}Positions have their own types; read with tuple[index] using a literal index, or destructure.\u{1D}{} is the empty tuple, the value of a function that returns nothing useful.\u{1F}patterns\u{1D}structs\u{1F}tuples\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def min_max(items: list[integer]): tuple[integer, integer] do\n    {Enum.min(items), Enum.max(items)}\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    {low, high} = min_max([3, 9, 2])\n    assert(low == 2 and high == 9)\n    pair = {\"a\", 1}\n    assert(pair[0] == \"a\" and pair[1] == 1)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}maps\u{1F}Data\u{1F}map[K, V]: immutable, written %{key => value}.\u{1F}Keys are integers, strings or booleans; all keys share one type and all values another.\u{1D}map[key] fails when the key is absent; Map.get returns an option, or a default with a third argument.\u{1D}Map.put, Map.delete, Map.update and friends return a new map; the original is unchanged.\u{1D}Maps enumerate as {key, value} tuples in ascending key order.\u{1D}An empty map needs a type: counts: map[string, integer] = %{}.\u{1F}Map\u{1D}MapSet\u{1D}structs\u{1F}reading and updating maps\u{1C}stock = %{\"apple\" => 3, \"pear\" => 0}\nmore = Map.put(stock, \"fig\", 7)\nassert(stock[\"apple\"] == 3 and map_size(more) == 3)\nassert(Map.get(stock, \"kiwi\") == none)\nassert(Map.get(stock, \"kiwi\", 0) == 0)\nassert(Map.update(stock, \"apple\", 1, &(&1 + 1))[\"apple\"] == 4)\nnames = Enum.map(stock, fn {name, _} -> name end)\nassert(names == [\"apple\", \"pear\"])\ncounts: map[string, integer] = %{}\nassert(map_size(counts) == 0)\n\u{1E}structs\u{1F}Data\u{1F}struct Name do field: type ... end: a named record.\u{1F}Declared inside a module. A field may have a default, field: type = value, which %Name{...} fills in when it leaves the field out; every other field must be given, and %Name{} gives every field its default.\u{1D}value.field reads; %Name{value | field: new} copies with fields replaced.\u{1D}%Name{field: pattern} is a pattern; unlisted fields are ignored.\u{1D}From another module the type is Module.Name and the literal %Module.Name{...}.\u{1F}sum-types\u{1D}generics\u{1D}patterns\u{1F}declare, build, update, match\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  struct Account do\n    owner: string\n    balance: integer = 0\n  end\n\n  def deposit(account: Account, amount: integer): Account do\n    %Account{account | balance: account.balance + amount}\n  end\n\n  def owner_of(%Account{owner: name}: Account): string do\n    name\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(%Account{owner: \"bo\"}.balance == 0)\n    account = %Account{owner: \"ada\", balance: 10}\n    richer = deposit(account, 15)\n    assert(richer.balance == 25 and account.balance == 10)\n    assert(owner_of(richer) == \"ada\")\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}sum-types\u{1F}Data\u{1F}type Name do variant(fields) ... end: a value that is one of several shapes.\u{1F}Build with Name.variant(arguments), or Name.variant for one without fields; the same forms are patterns.\u{1D}A case or set of clauses over a sum type must cover every variant or include a catch-all.\u{1D}Sum types may be recursive and take type parameters: type Tree[t].\u{1D}Use a sum type where Elixir would use atoms or tagged tuples.\u{1F}case\u{1D}structs\u{1D}option-result\u{1F}variants and matching\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  type Shape do\n    circle(radius: float)\n    rect(width: float, height: float)\n    empty\n  end\n\n  def area(shape: Shape): float do\n    case shape do\n      Shape.circle(radius) -> 3.14159 * radius * radius\n      Shape.rect(width, height) -> width * height\n      Shape.empty -> 0.0\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(area(Shape.rect(2.0, 3.0)) == 6.0)\n    assert(area(Shape.empty) == 0.0)\n    shapes = [Shape.circle(1.0), Shape.empty]\n    assert(length(shapes) == 2)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}option-result\u{1F}Data\u{1F}option[T] is some(value) or none; result[T, E] is ok(value) or error(reason).\u{1F}These replace nil and {:ok, value} / {:error, reason} tuples.\u{1D}Library functions return option for an absent value and result for an expected failure; names ending in ! fail instead.\u{1D}Take them apart with case, with, or a pattern in for.\u{1D}none, or ok(1) alone, needs a type from an annotation, parameter or return type.\u{1F}case\u{1D}with\u{1D}try\u{1F}returning and matching\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def halve(n: integer): result[integer, string] do\n    if n % 2 == 0 do\n      ok(n / 2)\n    else\n      error(\"\#{n} is odd\")\n    end\n  end\n\n  def first_even(items: list[integer]): option[integer] do\n    Enum.find(items, &(rem(&1, 2) == 0))\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(halve(10) == ok(5))\n    assert(halve(3) == error(\"3 is odd\"))\n    label = case first_even([1, 3]) do\n      some(n) -> \"found \#{n}\"\n      none -> \"none found\"\n    end\n    assert(label == \"none found\")\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}atoms\u{1F}Data\u{1F}:name is an atom; [name: value] is a keyword list.\u{1F}An atom is a name used as a value, of type atom. Atoms compare, sort, match in patterns, key maps and interpolate as their name.\u{1D}A colon starts an atom unless it follows a name or a closing bracket, where it introduces a type or a value: write a space before :name.\u{1D}Any atom fits the type atom, so a case over atoms needs a catch-all. For a fixed set of alternatives the compiler should check, use a sum type.\u{1D}Library functions do not return {:ok, value} tuples: they return option and result.\u{1D}[name: value, other: value] is a keyword list, a list[tuple[atom, T]] whose values share one type. It keeps its order and may repeat a key; the Keyword module reads and changes it.\u{1F}sum-types\u{1D}option-result\u{1D}Atom\u{1D}Keyword\u{1D}maps\u{1F}atoms and a keyword list\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def status(code: integer): atom do\n    case code do\n      200 -> :ok\n      404 -> :not_found\n      _ -> :error\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(status(404) == :not_found)\n    assert(\"status: \#{status(200)}\" == \"status: ok\")\n    counts = %{:red => 1, :blue => 2}\n    assert(counts[:blue] == 2)\n    options = [verbose: true, color: false]\n    assert(Keyword.get(options, :verbose) == some(true))\n    assert(Keyword.put(options, :color, true) == [color: true, verbose: true])\n    assert(options == [{:verbose, true}, {:color, false}])\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}ranges\u{1F}Data\u{1F}first..last: the integers from first to last inclusive.\u{1F}Counts down when first > last; first..last//step sets the step.\u{1D}A range is a Range struct, with first, last and step, and an enumerable of its integers: use it with Enum, Stream, for and in, and the Range functions. Written where a stream[integer] is expected, it is that stream.\u{1F}streams\u{1D}for\u{1D}Enum\u{1F}ranges\u{1C}assert(Enum.to_list(1..4) == [1, 2, 3, 4])\nassert(Enum.to_list(3..1) == [3, 2, 1])\nassert(Enum.to_list(0..10//5) == [0, 5, 10])\nassert(Enum.sum(1..100) == 5050)\nassert(3 in 1..5)\nr = 1..10//3\nassert(r.step == 3 and Range.size(r) == 4)\nassert(Enum.slice([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 1..-2) == [2, 3, 4])\n\u{1E}streams\u{1F}Data\u{1F}stream[T]: a lazy sequence, consumed by Enum.\u{1F}Stream functions build a pipeline and run nothing; an Enum function (to_list, take, sum, each, reduce) pulls only what it needs.\u{1D}Streams may be infinite: Stream.iterate, Stream.cycle, Stream.from_index, Stream.repeatedly, Stream.unfold.\u{1D}File.stream!(path) reads a file lazily by line, or by chunks of bytes with a size; IO.stream() reads standard input.\u{1D}Stream.chunk_every(n) batches a stream for processing in chunks.\u{1D}Streams cannot be compared or sent to a process.\u{1F}Stream\u{1D}Enum\u{1D}ranges\u{1D}File\u{1F}lazy pipelines\u{1C}evens = Stream.iterate(0, &(&1 + 2))\nassert(Enum.take(evens, 3) == [0, 2, 4])\nbatches = 1..1000000000\n  |> Stream.map(&(&1 * 2))\n  |> Stream.chunk_every(2)\n  |> Enum.take(2)\nassert(batches == [[2, 4], [6, 8]])\n\u{1E}sigils\u{1F}Data\u{1F}~r regex, ~w words, ~s strings, ~D ~T ~N ~U dates and times.\u{1F}~r/pattern/ is a regex checked at compile time (RE2 syntax); modifiers i, m, s, u may follow.\u{1D}~w(one two) is a list of strings; ~s(text) is a string that may contain double quotes.\u{1D}~D[2026-01-05] Date, ~T[13:04:05] Time, ~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05] NaiveDateTime, ~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z] DateTime.\u{1D}Delimiters may be / | \" ' ( ) [ ] { } < >; the text has no escapes.\u{1D}~s, ~w and ~r interpolate \#{expression}; ~S never does. A ~r that interpolates is compiled when it runs rather than checked at compile time.\u{1D}String.replace and String.split accept a ~r in place of the text to look for.\u{1D}~c(text) is the code points of the text as a list of integers; there are no charlists.\u{1D}~h is an HTML template: it builds Markup, escaping every value it interpolates (see alchem syntax templates); ~H does not interpolate.\u{1D}Any other letter is a sigil the program defines: ~x(text)mods calls sigil_x(text, \"mods\"), a function taking two strings. A lower-case one interpolates.\u{1F}Regex\u{1D}Date\u{1D}strings\u{1F}sigils\u{1C}assert(~w(red green blue) == [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"])\nassert(Regex.run(~r/(\\d+)-(\\d+)/, \"10-20\") == some([\"10-20\", \"10\", \"20\"]))\nassert(\"Hello\" =~ ~r/^hello$/i)\nassert(Date.add(~D[2026-12-31], 1) == ~D[2027-01-01])\nassert(~s(say \"hi\") == \"say \\\"hi\\\"\")\n\u{1D}interpolation and regular expressions with String\u{1C}name = \"ada\"\nassert(~s(hello \#{name}) == \"hello ada\")\nassert(~w(\#{name} lovelace) == [\"ada\", \"lovelace\"])\nassert(Regex.match?(~r/^\#{name}$/i, \"ADA\"))\nassert(~S(raw \#{name}) == \"raw \\\#{name}\")\nassert(String.replace(\"a1b22c\", ~r/[0-9]+/, \"-\") == \"a-b-c\")\nassert(String.split(\"a, b;c\", ~r/[,;] */) == [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n\u{1D}code points and a sigil of your own\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def sigil_p(text: string, modifiers: string): list[string] do\n    parts = String.split(text, \"/\")\n    if modifiers == \"r\" do\n      Enum.reverse(parts)\n    else\n      parts\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(~c(hi!) == [104, 105, 33])\n    assert(~p(usr/local/bin) == [\"usr\", \"local\", \"bin\"])\n    root = \"opt\"\n    assert(~p(\#{root}/bin)r == [\"bin\", \"opt\"])\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}modules\u{1F}Modules\u{1F}import \"file.alc\" brings another module's public functions and types.\u{1F}import takes a path relative to the importing file; call with the module's name: Math.double(3).\u{1D}def is public, defp private. Imported types are written Module.Type.\u{1D}Imports of a published package name a host path and version: import \"github.com/owner/repo@v1.2.0/lib/math.alc\"; versions are recorded in alchem.lock. See alchem syntax packages.\u{1D}Library modules (Enum, String, ...) need no import.\u{1F}program\u{1D}packages\u{1D}extern\u{1D}functions\u{1F}two files\u{1C}\# geometry.alc\ndefmodule Geometry do\n  struct Size do\n    width: integer\n    height: integer\n  end\n\n  def area(size: Size): integer do\n    size.width * size.height\n  end\nend\n\ndefmodule ShapesTest do\n  import \"geometry.alc\"\n\n  test \"imported functions and types\" do\n    size = %Geometry.Size{width: 2, height: 3}\n    assert(Geometry.area(size) == 6)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}packages\u{1F}Modules\u{1F}A package is a tagged repository of .alc files, imported by host path and version.\u{1F}import \"github.com/owner/repo@v1.2.0/lib/math.alc\" names the repository, an exact tag, and a file inside it. Files of a package import each other with relative paths, which cannot leave the package, and may bind extern functions to Go code inside it.\u{1D}alchem doc, run, build and check take the same path: alchem doc github.com/owner/repo@v1.2.0/lib/math.alc shows a file's documentation, and alchem run github.com/owner/repo@v1.2.0/tools/dev.alc -- arguments runs a program the package carries.\u{1D}Inside a project, alchem doc Module, alchem doc Module.function and alchem doc --search text also cover the project's own files and the packages they import, once those have been fetched by a check or a build.\u{1D}alchem.lock beside the entry file records the content of each package; commit it, and pass --locked in CI. A program run straight from a package keeps no lock file.\u{1D}For a private repository set ALCHEM_PRIVATE to comma-separated patterns such as github.com/owner/*: matching packages are fetched with git from their own host, using the credentials git already has, and nothing about them is sent to the public Go proxy or checksum database.\u{1D}require \"alchem\" \"v0.4.0\" in a file names the oldest Alchem release it works with; an older compiler reports that instead of an error about something it does not know. Releases before v0.3.8 do not know the line itself.\u{1D}One program uses one version of a package; two are the compile error conflicting versions.\u{1D}What a package builds a protocol or a service on is in the library: HTTP.take_over gives a handler its request's connection as a TCP socket, TCP.connect_tls opens an encrypted one, extern binds Go code, and a function may be defined for arguments of several types, as library functions are.\u{1D}Module names are shared by the whole program, so a package's module and one of the program's cannot have the same name, and neither can take a library module's name. The exception is a library module that is moving to a package (DiskStore, WebSocket, Version, OptionParser): a program that imports a module of that name uses it, and one that does not uses the builtin until it is dropped.\u{1F}modules\u{1D}extern\u{1D}comments\u{1F}a library file that states the release it needs\u{1C}\# temperature.alc\ndefmodule Temperature do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Conversions between scales.\n  \"\"\"\n  require \"alchem\" \"v0.3.0\"\n\n  @doc \"Degrees Fahrenheit for degrees Celsius.\"\n  def fahrenheit(celsius: integer): integer do\n    div(celsius * 9, 5) + 32\n  end\nend\n\ndefmodule TemperatureTest do\n  import \"temperature.alc\"\n\n  test \"a module of a package is used like any other\" do\n    assert(Temperature.fahrenheit(100) == 212)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}templates\u{1F}Text\u{1F}~h\"...\" and ~h\"\"\"...\"\"\" are HTML templates that escape what they interpolate.\u{1F}A template's own text is HTML as written. Each \#{...} value is escaped by its type: a string is escaped, Markup (such as another template) goes in as it is, integers, floats and atoms are written as text, and a list of Markup is joined.\u{1D}The result is a Markup value; Markup.to_string gives its text. Markup.raw makes Markup from trusted text, and Markup.escape escapes a string.\u{1D}Markup.parts takes a template's Markup apart into its fixed text and its values, for a library that sends the fixed text once and then only the values that change.\u{1D}Quote attribute values, href=\"\#{url}\": escaping makes a value safe inside quotes. It does not check what a URL points to.\u{1D}Any other type in \#{...}, such as a boolean or a map, is a compile error; convert it to a string first.\u{1F}sigils\u{1D}strings\u{1F}a list page\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  def item(name: string): Markup do\n    ~h\"<li>\#{name}</li>\"\n  end\n\n  def page(names: list[string]): Markup do\n    ~h\"\"\"\n    <h1>\#{length(names)} names</h1>\n    <ul>\#{Enum.map(names, &item/1)}</ul>\n    \"\"\"\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    html = Markup.to_string(page([\"ada\", \"<script>\"]))\n    assert(html == \"<h1>2 names</h1>\\n<ul><li>ada</li><li>&lt;script&gt;</li></ul>\\n\")\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}constants\u{1F}Modules\u{1F}@name value declares a module constant; @name anywhere in the module stands for the value.\u{1F}The value is built from literals, lists, tuples, maps, operators and earlier constants: @timeout 60 * 1000, @colors [:red, :green]. It cannot call functions or read bindings.\u{1D}A constant is visible throughout its module, including tests and struct field defaults, but not in other modules; share a value between modules with a function.\u{1D}Each use is replaced by the value when the program is compiled. A name may be defined once; @doc, @moduledoc, @typedoc and the test tags are not constants.\u{1F}modules\u{1D}values\u{1D}operators\u{1F}limits as constants\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  @attempts 3\n  @delay_ms 250\n  @budget_ms @attempts * @delay_ms\n\n  def plan(): string do\n    Integer.to_string(@attempts) <> \" tries within \" <> Integer.to_string(@budget_ms) <> \" ms\"\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(plan() == \"3 tries within 750 ms\")\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}protocols\u{1F}Modules\u{1F}defprotocol declares functions; defimpl supplies them for one type.\u{1F}defprotocol Name do def function(value): type ... end declares functions without bodies.\u{1D}defimpl Name, for: Type do ... end defines every function of the protocol for one type: a builtin type, a struct or sum type from any module, or a generic type such as list[a]. The first parameter is the value and needs no annotation.\u{1D}A call Name.function(value, ...) is resolved when the program is compiled, from the type of its first argument. There is no dispatch while the program runs, so the type must be known where the call is written: it cannot be a type variable.\u{1D}A protocol is known by its own name in every module of the program, and a type may be given an implementation in any module.\u{1D}An implementation that lacks a function, or disagrees with the protocol on the number of arguments or the result type, is a compile error.\u{1F}modules\u{1D}structs\u{1D}generics\u{1F}one protocol, three types\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  struct Circle do\n    radius: float\n  end\n\n  defprotocol Describe do\n    def describe(value): string\n  end\n\n  defimpl Describe, for: integer do\n    def describe(n): string do\n      \"the number \#{n}\"\n    end\n  end\n\n  defimpl Describe, for: Circle do\n    def describe(circle): string do\n      \"a circle of radius \#{circle.radius}\"\n    end\n  end\n\n  defimpl Describe, for: list[a] do\n    def describe(items): string do\n      \"a list of \#{length(items)}\"\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(Describe.describe(7) == \"the number 7\")\n    assert(Describe.describe(%Circle{radius: 1.5}) == \"a circle of radius 1.5\")\n    assert(Describe.describe([\"a\", \"b\"]) == \"a list of 2\")\n    assert(Enum.map([1, 2], &Describe.describe(&1)) == [\"the number 1\", \"the number 2\"])\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}macros\u{1F}Modules\u{1F}defmacro defines a template; a call is replaced by the template with its arguments filled in.\u{1F}defmacro name(parameters) do quote do ... end end. Inside the quote, unquote(parameter) stands for the argument given at the call.\u{1D}Expansion happens before anything is typed and runs no code: it copies the quoted code and substitutes. What comes out is checked like any other code, and a problem is reported where the macro was called.\u{1D}A last parameter written do: name takes a do ... end block: name(arguments) do ... end. Only a macro takes a block.\u{1D}A macro whose template is one expression can be used as a value; one with several statements is used as a statement.\u{1D}An argument is put in wherever it is unquoted, so unquoting it twice evaluates it twice. Bind it once in the template to avoid that.\u{1D}A name the template binds is separate from the same name at the call, and a function the template calls is the one in the macro's own module.\u{1D}From another module a macro is called Module.name(...). A macro named __using__ holds function definitions, which use Module, option: value adds to the module that says so.\u{1F}functions\u{1D}modules\u{1D}protocols\u{1F}a value, statements, and a block\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  defmacro square(value) do\n    quote do\n      unquote(value) * unquote(value)\n    end\n  end\n\n  defmacro check(label, value) do\n    quote do\n      result = unquote(value)\n      assert(result > 0)\n      IO.puts(\"\#{unquote(label)}: \#{result}\")\n    end\n  end\n\n  defmacro unless_empty(items, do: body) do\n    quote do\n      if unquote(items) != [] do\n        unquote(body)\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    result = 3\n    assert(square(result + 1) == 16)\n    check(\"doubled\", result * 2)\n    assert(result == 3)\n    unless_empty([1, 2]) do\n      IO.puts(\"has items\")\n    end\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}nodes\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Programs connect as nodes and their processes exchange messages, which are copied.\u{1F}Node.start(name, address, cookie) makes a program a node; Node.connect(address) connects it to another that knows the same cookie. With two arguments Node.start reads the cookie from ALCHEM_COOKIE.\u{1D}Process.register(pid, :name) names a process. Node.whereis(node, :name) finds it from another node as an option[pid[T]], where T comes from the annotation and must be the type it was registered with.\u{1D}Process.send to that handle copies the message across. A process handle inside a message crosses too, so the receiver can reply through it.\u{1D}A handle to a process of another node can be sent to and monitored: Process.monitor reports its exit as an event, or \"noconnection\" if its node is lost first. Process.alive tells whether its node is connected. Receiving, linking and awaiting need a process of this node. Node.monitor(node) reports a lost connection as an event whose reason is \"nodedown\" and the node's name. Node.monitor_nodes() reports every connection made (\"nodeup\") or lost (\"nodedown\"), for as long as the process runs. Node.connect(address, reconnect: true) makes a lost connection again, a second later at first and up to 30 seconds apart; reconnect: n gives up after n failed attempts in a row, reported as \"noreconnect\".\u{1D}Stream.publish(:name, fn -> stream end) offers a stream to other nodes, and Node.stream(node, :name) reads it from one: elements are pulled a chunk at a time as they are consumed, so an endless or very large stream stays where it is.\u{1D}Only values that can be copied cross: no functions, streams, regular expressions, ports, tasks, agents or servers. The compiler checks this where a process is registered or looked up.\u{1D}Nodes that exchange a message type must agree on it: share the module that declares it.\u{1D}Connections are encrypted with TLS 1.3, and the cookie decides who may connect: each node proves it knows the cookie without sending it. Use a long random cookie, and keep it out of source control.\u{1D}Node.start(name, address, cookie, [encrypted: false]) turns encryption off, for a network you trust. Only nodes with the same setting connect, and a mismatch is reported on both sides.\u{1D}A send to another node returns once the message is written, not once it is received, and a full mailbox there delays and then drops messages.\u{1F}processes\u{1D}Node\u{1D}Process\u{1F}starting a node and naming a process\u{1C}started = Node.start(\"example\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nassert(started == ok({}) or started == error(\"this program is already a node\"))\ninbox: pid[tuple[atom, integer]] = Process.self(8)\nProcess.register(inbox, :example_inbox)\nfound: option[pid[tuple[atom, integer]]] = Process.whereis(:example_inbox)\nsent = case found do\n  some(named) -> Process.send(named, {:count, 3}, 100)\n  none -> false\nend\nassert(sent)\nassert_receive(inbox, {:count, 3})\nProcess.unregister(:example_inbox)\nelsewhere: option[pid[tuple[atom, integer]]] = Node.whereis(\"other\", :example_inbox, 50)\nreached = case elsewhere do\n  some(_) -> true\n  none -> false\nend\nassert(not reached)\n\u{1E}extern\u{1F}Modules\u{1F}extern name(params): result = \"package.Function\" calls a Go function.\u{1F}The Alchem signature is checked against the Go one by alchem check.\u{1D}A Go (T, error) result is declared result[T, string]; (T, bool) is option[T].\u{1D}extern type Name = \"*package.Type\" names an opaque Go type; methods are \"package.Type.Method\" with the receiver first.\u{1D}A handle stays with the process that has it and is not compared. extern shared type Name = \"*package.Type\" declares one whose Go value is safe to use from several goroutines at once: it can then be sent in messages and kept by servers, like a socket or a file.\u{1D}require \"module/path\" \"v1.2.3\" adds a third-party Go module; \"./dir.Function\" refers to Go code beside the program.\u{1F}modules\u{1F}calling the Go standard library\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  extern shout(text: string): string = \"strings.ToUpper\"\n  extern to_number(text: string): result[integer, string] = \"strconv.Atoi\"\n\n  def main() do\n    assert(shout(\"hello\") == \"HELLO\")\n    assert(to_number(\"42\") == ok(42))\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}processes\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Lightweight processes with typed mailboxes.\u{1F}pid[T] is a process whose mailbox carries T. Process.spawn(function, capacity) starts one; the function receives its own pid and returns an exit status.\u{1D}Process.self(capacity) is the current process; annotate it to fix the message type.\u{1D}Process.send(pid, message, timeout_ms) and Process.receive(self, timeout_ms) -> {arrived, message}.\u{1D}For state, prefer Server (request/reply), Agent (shared state) or Task (one result) over raw processes.\u{1D}Supervisor restarts failed children; Process.monitor and Process.link observe failures.\u{1F}Process\u{1D}Server\u{1D}Task\u{1D}Agent\u{1D}Supervisor\u{1F}send and receive\u{1C}parent: pid[string] = Process.self(8)\nworker: pid[string] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[string]): integer do\n  {_, text} = Process.receive(self, 1000)\n  Process.send(parent, String.upcase(text), 1000)\n  0\nend, 8)\nProcess.send(worker, \"hello\", 1000)\nassert(Process.receive(parent, 1000) == {true, \"HELLO\"})\n\u{1D}tasks and a server\u{1C}defmodule Example do\n  type Request do\n    add(amount: integer)\n    read\n  end\n\n  def handle(total: integer, request: Request): tuple[integer, integer] do\n    case request do\n      Request.add(amount) -> {total + amount, total + amount}\n      Request.read -> {total, total}\n    end\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    squares = Task.async_stream(1..4, fn n -> n * n end) |> Enum.to_list()\n    assert(squares == [1, 4, 9, 16])\n    counter = Server.start(0, &handle)\n    assert(Server.call(counter, Request.add(5), 1000) == ok(5))\n    assert(Server.call(counter, Request.read, 1000) == ok(5))\n    Server.stop(counter)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}tests\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}Test files end in _test.alc and are run by alchem test.\u{1F}test \"name\" do ... end defines a test; describe \"group\" do ... end groups tests and may nest.\u{1D}assert, refute, assert_raise, assert_in_delta and flunk are the assertions; a failed comparison shows both sides.\u{1D}on_exit(fn -> ... end) runs when the test ends, passed or failed; capture_io(fn -> ... end) returns what was printed; assert_receive(self, pattern) takes the first matching message from the test's own mailbox and binds the pattern's names; refute_receive(self, pattern) fails if one arrives. Both accept a when guard, a timeout in milliseconds and a failure message; assert_received and refute_received do not wait.\u{1D}setup do ... end runs before each test in its describe; setup_all once per file. A setup that ends in a struct passes it to tests written test \"name\", context do.\u{1D}@tag \"slow\" or @tag :slow marks the next test, @describetag the next describe and @moduletag the file; a tag may have a value, @tag kind: :db, owner: \"ada\". Run with --tag or --exclude, by name (kind) or by name and value (kind:db). @tag timeout: 500 gives one test its own limit in milliseconds, or :infinity; @tag :skip or skip: \"reason\" skips.\u{1D}alchem test --seed N repeats an order; --only text, --trace, --max-failures N, --cover and --cover-min P are the other flags.\u{1D}Test files are compiled and run in parallel, one per processor; --jobs 1 runs them one at a time. Their output is printed in the same order either way.\u{1F}doctests\u{1D}Testing\u{1F}a test file\u{1C}defmodule CartTest do\n  struct Context do\n    prices: list[integer]\n  end\n\n  setup do\n    %Context{prices: [5, 10]}\n  end\n\n  describe \"totals\" do\n    test \"adds prices\", context do\n      assert(Enum.sum(context.prices) == 15)\n    end\n\n    describe \"empty carts\" do\n      test \"total nothing\" do\n        assert(Enum.sum(Enum.take([1], 0)) == 0)\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  @tag \"slow\"\n  test \"division by zero is reported\" do\n    assert_raise(\"division by zero\", fn -> 1 / 0 end)\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}properties\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}check all pattern <- generator, ... do ... end checks a property on generated values.\u{1F}Generators come from the Gen module: Gen.integer(), Gen.integer(low, high), Gen.string(:alphanumeric), Gen.list_of(g), Gen.tuple(a, b), Gen.member_of(list), Gen.one_of(generators), Gen.map, Gen.filter, Gen.bind and more.\u{1D}Each generator may use what the ones before it bound: check all low <- Gen.integer(0, 9), high <- Gen.integer(low, 20) do ... end.\u{1D}Conditions between the generators drop the values that fail them, and max_runs: n changes how many values are tried, 100 unless ALCHEM_PROPERTY_RUNS says otherwise.\u{1D}Values grow with each run. When the body fails, the value is shrunk to the smallest one that still fails, and that one is reported with the failure.\u{1D}The values come from the test seed, so alchem test --seed N repeats a failure.\u{1F}tests\u{1D}Gen\u{1F}a property\u{1C}defmodule ReverseTest do\n  test \"reversing twice gives the list back\" do\n    check all list <- Gen.list_of(Gen.integer()), max_runs: 50 do\n      assert(Enum.reverse(Enum.reverse(list)) == list)\n    end\n  end\n\n  test \"a sorted list is ordered\" do\n    check all list <- Gen.list_of(Gen.integer(-5, 5)) do\n      sorted = Enum.sort(list)\n      assert(Enum.all?(Enum.zip(sorted, Enum.drop(sorted, 1)), fn {a, b} -> a <= b end))\n    end\n  end\nend\n\u{1E}doctests\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}doctest Module runs the alx> examples in a module's @doc strings.\u{1F}In @doc, a line alx> expression is run; the lines after it, up to a blank line, are the expected value.\u{1D}...> continues the expression on the next line; an example without an expected value is only run.\u{1D}Examples in one @doc share bindings, top to bottom.\u{1D}The test file imports the module and says doctest Module.\u{1F}comments\u{1D}tests\u{1F}documented examples as tests\u{1C}\# price.alc\ndefmodule Price do\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Adds tax at a percentage.\n\n      alx> Price.with_tax(100, 20)\n      120\n      alx> net = 50\n      alx> Price.with_tax(net, 10)\n      55\n  \"\"\"\n  def with_tax(net: integer, percent: integer): integer do\n    net + net * percent / 100\n  end\nend\n\ndefmodule PriceTest do\n  import \"price.alc\"\n  doctest Price\nend\n"
  end

  def modules(): string do
    "Kernel\u{1F}Core\u{1F}Functions called without a module name: arithmetic helpers, list head and tail, conversion to text, and inspect. A module that defines a function of the same name reaches the builtin as Kernel.name, as in Kernel.div(n, 2).\u{1E}Atom\u{1F}Core\u{1F}Names used as values, written :name. Atoms compare, sort, key maps and match in patterns.\u{1E}Function\u{1F}Core\u{1F}Helpers for working with functions as values.\u{1E}Bitwise\u{1F}Core\u{1F}Bitwise operations on integers.\u{1E}Enum\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Eager operations over any enumerable: lists, maps, sets, ranges and streams. Results are finished values.\u{1E}Stream\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Lazy operations over any enumerable. Nothing runs until the stream is consumed by an Enum function, so streams may be infinite.\u{1E}List\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Operations specific to lists, such as positional updates.\u{1E}Map\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Immutable maps with integer, string or boolean keys.\u{1E}Keyword\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Keyword lists: lists of {atom, value} pairs written [name: value], kept in order and allowing a key more than once.\u{1E}MapSet\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Immutable sets of integers, strings or booleans; the type is set[T].\u{1E}String\u{1F}Text\u{1F}UTF-8 strings. Lengths and positions count Unicode code points.\u{1E}Regex\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Regular expressions in RE2 syntax: no backreferences or lookaround. Written ~r/pattern/ or compiled from text.\u{1E}Integer\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Integer parsing, formatting and number theory.\u{1E}Float\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Float parsing, formatting and rounding.\u{1E}Base\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Base 16, 32 and 64 encoding.\u{1E}JSON\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Converts between JSON text, the builtin JSON type and declared types.\u{1E}Date\u{1F}Dates and times\u{1F}Calendar dates, written ~D[2026-01-05].\u{1E}Time\u{1F}Dates and times\u{1F}Times of day, written ~T[13:04:05], and the program clock.\u{1E}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}Dates and times\u{1F}A date and time without a time zone, written ~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05].\u{1E}DateTime\u{1F}Dates and times\u{1F}A date and time in a time zone: UTC when written ~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z], another zone after DateTime.shift_zone.\u{1E}Calendar\u{1F}Dates and times\u{1F}Formatting shared by the four date and time types.\u{1E}Duration\u{1F}Dates and times\u{1F}An amount of time per unit, for shifting dates and times: Duration.months(1), Duration.add(...).\u{1E}File\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Reading, writing and managing files; File.stream! reads lazily.\u{1E}Path\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Manipulating file paths as text.\u{1E}IO\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Standard input, output and error.\u{1E}System\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Arguments, environment, external commands and exit.\u{1E}URI\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Percent-encoding and query strings.\u{1E}Version\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Parsing and comparing semantic versions. Moving to a package: this builtin is dropped in v0.4.0. Import github.com/SmithOperatingSolutions/alchem-packages@v0.2.0/version/version.alc, which has the same functions and types.\u{1E}OptionParser\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Reads command-line switches into a map. Moving to a package: this builtin is dropped in v0.4.0. Import github.com/SmithOperatingSolutions/alchem-packages@v0.2.0/option_parser/option_parser.alc, which has the same functions and takes the arguments as a list.\u{1E}Logger\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Leveled messages on standard error.\u{1E}Port\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}Runs an external program and exchanges text with it: its output arrives as messages, a line at a time.\u{1E}Process\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Lightweight processes with typed mailboxes, links and monitors.\u{1E}Node\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Connects this program to others so that their processes can exchange messages, which are copied between them.\u{1E}Supervisor\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Restarts child processes according to a strategy.\u{1E}Server\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}A process owning a state that answers typed requests in order.\u{1E}Task\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Runs a function in its own process and collects its result.\u{1E}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Runs tasks as temporary children of a supervisor: a task supervisor is a dynamic_supervisor, so DynamicSupervisor's functions apply to it too.\u{1E}Agent\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}A process holding a state that functions read and update.\u{1E}Testing\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}Assertions for test files, called without a module name.\u{1E}Gen\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}Generators of random values for check all properties. Values grow with each run, and a failing value shrinks to the smallest that still fails.\u{1E}Markup\u{1F}Text\u{1F}HTML built by ~h templates, which escape every value they interpolate; see alchem syntax templates.\u{1E}TCP\u{1F}Network\u{1F}TCP connections: listen, accept, connect, send and receive text. Calls that wait take milliseconds (negative waits indefinitely) and give error(\"timeout\") or error(\"closed\"); a process waiting in one still stops when asked. A socket belongs to the process that opened it and closes when that process ends.\u{1E}UDP\u{1F}Network\u{1F}UDP sockets: datagrams sent to and received from host:port addresses, passively with UDP.recv or actively as UDP.Event messages. UDP sockets close as TCP sockets do.\u{1E}HTTP\u{1F}Network\u{1F}An HTTP client, and a server that answers each request in a process of its own. Requests and responses are the HTTP.Request and HTTP.Response structs; header names are lower case.\u{1E}IO.ANSI\u{1F}Core\u{1F}Escape sequences that color and move text on a terminal. Each name, such as IO.ANSI.red(), gives its sequence; IO.ANSI.format joins text and names.\u{1E}Range\u{1F}Collections\u{1F}Ranges of integers, first..last//step: a Range struct with first, last and step, and an enumerable of its integers. A range written where a stream is expected is that stream.\u{1E}Registry\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}Finds processes by key. Each entry belongs to the process that registered it, holds a value, and goes away when that process ends.\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}Concurrency\u{1F}A supervisor that starts children while it runs, of any message type, and restarts the ones that fail.\u{1E}DiskStore\u{1F}Files and system\u{1F}A table of text under text keys, kept in one file so that it outlasts the program: a write is on the disk when it returns, keys are read in order by range or prefix, and any process may use it. Moving to a package: this builtin is dropped in v0.4.0. Import github.com/SmithOperatingSolutions/alchem-packages@v0.1.0/disk_store/disk_store.alc, which has the same functions under the same names; a program that imports it uses the package's, and its store type is DiskStore.Store.\u{1E}WebSocket\u{1F}Network\u{1F}Moving to a package: this builtin is dropped in v0.4.0. Import github.com/SmithOperatingSolutions/alchem-packages@v0.2.0/websocket/websocket.alc, which has the same functions; its socket is the type WebSocket.Socket. WebSockets, RFC 6455: a handler of HTTP.serve upgrades its request into a socket, and WebSocket.connect opens one to a server. Messages are text or binary; pings and the closing handshake are answered without the program's help. Calls that wait take milliseconds and give error(\"timeout\") or error(\"closed\"). A socket belongs to the process that made it and closes when that process ends, so a handler that upgrades either keeps the socket or hands it on with controlling_process.\u{1E}Crypto\u{1F}Text\u{1F}Cryptographic building blocks: random bytes from the operating system's secure source, hashes, HMAC signatures, password storage and a comparison that takes the same time however much of its input matches. Results are raw bytes; Base.encode16 or Base.encode64 makes them printable."
  end

  def functions(): string do
    "Agent.cast\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.cast(atom, fn[s,s]) -> boolean\u{1D}Agent.cast(agent[s], fn[s,s]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Queues a state update without waiting for it; false when the agent has stopped.\u{1F}alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 0 end)\nalx> Agent.cast(counter, fn n -> n + 1 end)\ntrue\nalx> Agent.get(counter, fn n -> n end)\n1\nalx> Agent.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Agent.get\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.get(atom, fn[s,r]) -> r\u{1D}Agent.get(agent[s], fn[s,r]) -> r\u{1D}Agent.get(agent[s], fn[s,r], integer) -> r\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function on the state inside the agent and returns its result. A last argument waits that many milliseconds instead of 5 seconds.\u{1F}alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 41 end)\nalx> Agent.get(counter, fn n -> n + 1 end)\n42\nalx> Agent.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Agent.get_and_update\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.get_and_update(atom, fn[s,tuple[r,s]]) -> r\u{1D}Agent.get_and_update(agent[s], fn[s,tuple[r,s]]) -> r\u{1D}Agent.get_and_update(agent[s], fn[s,tuple[r,s]], integer) -> r\u{1F}\u{1F}The function returns {reply, next_state}: one step that reads and changes the state.\u{1F}alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 10 end)\nalx> Agent.get_and_update(counter, fn n -> {n, n + 1} end)\n10\nalx> Agent.get(counter, fn n -> n end)\n11\nalx> Agent.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Agent.start\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.start(fn[s]) -> agent[s]\u{1D}Agent.start(fn[s], list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> agent[s]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a process holding the state the function returns.\u{1F}alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 0 end)\nalx> Agent.get(counter, fn n -> n end)\n0\nalx> Agent.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Agent.start_link\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.start_link(fn[s]) -> agent[s]\u{1D}Agent.start_link(fn[s], list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> agent[s]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like Agent.start, and also links the agent to the process starting it. With [name: :name] the agent is registered under the name, and the functions that take an agent also take the name; the state's type then comes from the function's annotation.\u{1F}alx> names = Agent.start_link(fn -> [\"ada\"] end)\nalx> Agent.get(names, &length(&1))\n1\nalx> Agent.stop(names)\ntrue\nalx> Agent.start_link(fn -> 1 end, [name: :doc_agent])\nalx> Agent.get(:doc_agent, fn(n: integer): integer do n + 1 end)\n2\u{1E}Agent.stop\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.stop(agent[s]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops the agent.\u{1F}alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 0 end)\nalx> Agent.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Agent.update\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.update(atom, fn[s,s])\u{1D}Agent.update(agent[s], fn[s,s])\u{1D}Agent.update(agent[s], fn[s,s], integer)\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces the state with the function's result, waiting until it is done.\u{1F}alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 0 end)\nalx> Agent.update(counter, fn n -> n + 5 end)\nalx> Agent.get(counter, fn n -> n end)\n5\nalx> Agent.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Agent.whereis\u{1F}Agent\u{1F}Agent.whereis(atom) -> option[agent[s]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Finds an agent by name. The state's type comes from the annotation; a name registered for another type is not found.\u{1F}alx> Agent.start(fn -> 3 end, [name: :doc_found])\nalx> found: option[agent[integer]] = Agent.whereis(:doc_found)\nalx> case found do\n...>   some(agent) -> Agent.get(agent, fn n -> n end)\n...>   none -> 0\n...> end\n3\u{1E}Atom.to_string\u{1F}Atom\u{1F}Atom.to_string(atom) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The name of an atom as a string, without the colon. Interpolation does the same.\u{1F}alx> Atom.to_string(:not_found)\n\"not_found\"\nalx> \"status: \#{:ok}\"\n\"status: ok\"\u{1E}Base.decode16\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.decode16(string) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.decode16(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes hexadecimal text, or none when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.decode16(\"6869\")\nsome(\"hi\")\u{1E}Base.decode16!\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.decode16!(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes hexadecimal text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.decode16!(\"6869\")\n\"hi\"\u{1E}Base.decode32\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.decode32(string) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.decode32(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.decode32(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes base 32 text, or none when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.decode32(Base.encode32(\"hi\"))\nsome(\"hi\")\u{1E}Base.decode32!\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.decode32!(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes base 32 text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.decode32!(Base.encode32(\"hi\"))\n\"hi\"\u{1E}Base.decode64\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.decode64(string) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.decode64(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.decode64(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes base 64 text, or none when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.decode64(\"aGk=\")\nsome(\"hi\")\nalx> Base.decode64(\"!\")\nnone\u{1E}Base.decode64!\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.decode64!(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes base 64 text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.decode64!(\"aGk=\")\n\"hi\"\u{1E}Base.encode16\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.encode16(string) -> string\u{1D}Base.encode16(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes bytes as hexadecimal text.\u{1F}alx> String.downcase(Base.encode16(\"hi\"))\n\"6869\"\u{1E}Base.encode32\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.encode32(string) -> string\u{1D}Base.encode32(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> string\u{1D}Base.encode32(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes bytes as base 32 text.\u{1F}alx> Base.encode32(\"hi\")\n\"NBUQ====\"\u{1E}Base.encode64\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.encode64(string) -> string\u{1D}Base.encode64(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> string\u{1D}Base.encode64(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes bytes as base 64 text.\u{1F}alx> Base.encode64(\"hi\")\n\"aGk=\"\u{1E}Base.hex_decode32\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.hex_decode32(string) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.hex_decode32(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> option[string]\u{1D}Base.hex_decode32(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes base 32 with the extended hexadecimal alphabet; none when the text is not valid.\u{1F}alx> Base.hex_decode32(\"CO======\")\nsome(\"f\")\u{1E}Base.hex_encode32\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.hex_encode32(string) -> string\u{1D}Base.hex_encode32(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> string\u{1D}Base.hex_encode32(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes in base 32 with the extended hexadecimal alphabet; options as for encode32.\u{1F}alx> Base.hex_encode32(\"f\")\n\"CO======\"\nalx> Base.hex_encode32(\"f\", [padding: false])\n\"CO\"\u{1E}Base.url_decode64\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.url_decode64(string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes URL-safe base 64 text, or none when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.url_decode64(Base.url_encode64(\"hi?\"))\nsome(\"hi?\")\u{1E}Base.url_decode64!\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.url_decode64!(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes URL-safe base 64 text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Base.url_decode64!(Base.url_encode64(\"hi?\"))\n\"hi?\"\u{1E}Base.url_encode64\u{1F}Base\u{1F}Base.url_encode64(string) -> string\u{1D}Base.url_encode64(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> string\u{1D}Base.url_encode64(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes bytes as base 64 text using the URL-safe alphabet.\u{1F}alx> Base.url_encode64(\"hi\")\n\"aGk=\"\u{1E}Bitwise.band\u{1F}Bitwise\u{1F}Bitwise.band(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Bitwise and.\u{1F}alx> Bitwise.band(12, 10)\n8\u{1E}Bitwise.bnot\u{1F}Bitwise\u{1F}Bitwise.bnot(integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Bitwise complement.\u{1F}alx> Bitwise.bnot(0)\n-1\u{1E}Bitwise.bor\u{1F}Bitwise\u{1F}Bitwise.bor(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Bitwise or.\u{1F}alx> Bitwise.bor(12, 10)\n14\u{1E}Bitwise.bsl\u{1F}Bitwise\u{1F}Bitwise.bsl(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Shifts left by a number of bits.\u{1F}alx> Bitwise.bsl(1, 4)\n16\u{1E}Bitwise.bsr\u{1F}Bitwise\u{1F}Bitwise.bsr(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Shifts right by a number of bits, keeping the sign.\u{1F}alx> Bitwise.bsr(16, 2)\n4\u{1E}Bitwise.bxor\u{1F}Bitwise\u{1F}Bitwise.bxor(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Bitwise exclusive or.\u{1F}alx> Bitwise.bxor(12, 10)\n6\u{1E}Calendar.strftime\u{1F}Calendar\u{1F}Calendar.strftime(a, string) -> string\u{1F}a is a Date, Time, NaiveDateTime or DateTime\u{1F}Formats a Date, Time, NaiveDateTime or DateTime. Directives: %Y %y year, %m month, %d day, %j day of year, %q quarter, %u weekday number (Monday is 1), %a %A weekday name, %b %B month name, %H %I hour, %M minute, %S second, %f fraction, %p %P AM/PM, %z %Z zone, %c %x %X preferred forms, %% a percent sign. A flag after % changes padding (- none, 0 zeros, _ spaces) and a number sets the width. A directive the value has no field for fails.\u{1F}alx> Calendar.strftime(~D[2026-03-05], \"%A %-d %B %Y\")\n\"Thursday 5 March 2026\"\nalx> Calendar.strftime(~U[2026-03-14T15:09:26Z], \"%y-%m-%d %I:%M:%S %p\")\n\"26-03-14 03:09:26 PM\"\nalx> Calendar.strftime(~T[09:04:05], \"%H:%M\")\n\"09:04\"\nalx> Calendar.strftime(~N[2026-03-05 09:04:05], \"%j %q %u %a %b %%\")\n\"064 1 4 Thu Mar %\"\u{1E}Crypto.hash\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.hash(atom, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The digest of some data with :md5, :sha1, :sha224, :sha256, :sha384 or :sha512, as raw bytes. MD5 and SHA-1 are for checking old formats, not for security.\u{1F}alx> Base.encode16(Crypto.hash(:sha256, \"abc\"), [case: :lower])\n\"ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad\"\u{1E}Crypto.hash_password\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.hash_password(string) -> string\u{1D}Crypto.hash_password(string, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}What to store in place of a password: its PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 key under a fresh random salt, written with the salt and the count of iterations as $pbkdf2-sha256$i=600000$salt$key, so that Crypto.verify_password needs nothing else. The same password gives a different result each time. [iterations: n] sets the count, 600,000 by default; passwords stored with an older count still verify. Moving to a package: this builtin is dropped in v0.4.0. Import github.com/SmithOperatingSolutions/alchem-packages@v0.1.0/password/password.alc and call Password.hash, which reads and writes the same stored text.\u{1F}alx> stored = Crypto.hash_password(\"correct horse\", [iterations: 1000])\nalx> String.starts_with?(stored, \"$pbkdf2-sha256$i=1000$\")\ntrue\nalx> Crypto.verify_password(\"correct horse\", stored)\ntrue\nalx> stored == Crypto.hash_password(\"correct horse\", [iterations: 1000])\nfalse\u{1E}Crypto.hmac\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.hmac(atom, string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The HMAC of data under a key with one of the hash algorithms, as raw bytes: a signature only someone holding the key can make. Compare signatures with Crypto.secure_compare.\u{1F}alx> Base.encode16(Crypto.hmac(:sha256, \"Jefe\", \"what do ya want for nothing?\"), [case: :lower])\n\"5bdcc146bf60754e6a042426089575c75a003f089d2739839dec58b964ec3843\"\u{1E}Crypto.pbkdf2\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.pbkdf2(atom, string, string, integer, integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}A key of the given number of bytes derived from a password and a salt by PBKDF2 over HMAC with one of the hash algorithms, as raw bytes. The count of iterations makes each guess at the password cost that much work. For storing passwords, Crypto.hash_password chooses the salt and the count.\u{1F}alx> Base.encode16(Crypto.pbkdf2(:sha256, \"password\", \"salt\", 4096, 32), [case: :lower])\n\"c5e478d59288c841aa530db6845c4c8d962893a001ce4e11a4963873aa98134a\"\u{1E}Crypto.secure_compare\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.secure_compare(string, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether two strings are equal, taking a time that depends only on their lengths, so that checking a signature or token does not reveal how much of a guess was right.\u{1F}alx> Crypto.secure_compare(\"token\", \"token\")\ntrue\nalx> Crypto.secure_compare(\"token\", \"tokem\")\nfalse\u{1E}Crypto.strong_rand_bytes\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The given number of random bytes from the operating system's secure source, for keys, tokens and nonces.\u{1F}alx> byte_size(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(16))\n16\nalx> Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(16) == Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(16)\nfalse\u{1E}Crypto.verify_password\u{1F}Crypto\u{1F}Crypto.verify_password(string, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether a password is the one Crypto.hash_password made a stored value from. Text that is not such a value matches no password. Moving to a package: this builtin is dropped in v0.4.0. Import github.com/SmithOperatingSolutions/alchem-packages@v0.1.0/password/password.alc and call Password.verify, which reads and writes the same stored text.\u{1F}alx> stored = Crypto.hash_password(\"correct horse\", [iterations: 1000])\nalx> {Crypto.verify_password(\"correct horse\", stored), Crypto.verify_password(\"wrong\", stored)}\n{true, false}\nalx> Crypto.verify_password(\"correct horse\", \"not a stored password\")\nfalse\u{1E}Date.add\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.add(Date, integer) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a number of days, which may be negative.\u{1F}alx> Date.add(~D[2026-02-27], 2)\n~D[2026-03-01]\u{1E}Date.after?\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.after?(Date, Date) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first date is later than the second.\u{1F}alx> Date.after?(~D[2026-03-01], ~D[2026-02-01])\ntrue\u{1E}Date.before?\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.before?(Date, Date) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first date is earlier than the second.\u{1F}alx> Date.before?(~D[2026-03-01], ~D[2026-02-01])\nfalse\u{1E}Date.beginning_of_month\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.beginning_of_month(Date) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}The first day of the date's month.\u{1F}alx> Date.beginning_of_month(~D[2026-03-18])\n~D[2026-03-01]\u{1E}Date.beginning_of_week\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.beginning_of_week(Date) -> Date\u{1D}Date.beginning_of_week(Date, atom) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}The Monday on or before the date.\u{1F}alx> Date.beginning_of_week(~D[2026-03-18])\n~D[2026-03-16]\u{1E}Date.compare\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.compare(Date, Date) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns :lt, :eq or :gt as the first date is earlier than, equal to or later than the second.\u{1F}alx> Date.compare(~D[2026-01-01], ~D[2026-01-02])\n:lt\u{1E}Date.day_of_week\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.day_of_week(Date) -> integer\u{1D}Date.day_of_week(Date, atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The day of the week, from 1 for Monday to 7 for Sunday.\u{1F}alx> Date.day_of_week(~D[2026-03-18])\n3\u{1E}Date.day_of_year\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.day_of_year(Date) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The day of the year, from 1.\u{1F}alx> Date.day_of_year(~D[2026-02-01])\n32\u{1E}Date.days_in_month\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.days_in_month(Date) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of days in the date's month.\u{1F}alx> Date.days_in_month(~D[2024-02-10])\n29\u{1E}Date.diff\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.diff(Date, Date) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of days from the second date to the first.\u{1F}alx> Date.diff(~D[2026-03-01], ~D[2026-02-01])\n28\u{1E}Date.end_of_month\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.end_of_month(Date) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}The last day of the date's month.\u{1F}alx> Date.end_of_month(~D[2026-02-10])\n~D[2026-02-28]\u{1E}Date.end_of_week\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.end_of_week(Date) -> Date\u{1D}Date.end_of_week(Date, atom) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}The Sunday on or after the date.\u{1F}alx> Date.end_of_week(~D[2026-03-18])\n~D[2026-03-22]\u{1E}Date.from_iso8601\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.from_iso8601(string) -> result[Date,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses YYYY-MM-DD text, returning ok(date) or error(reason).\u{1F}alx> Date.from_iso8601(\"2026-03-18\")\nok(~D[2026-03-18])\u{1E}Date.from_iso8601!\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.from_iso8601!(string) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses YYYY-MM-DD text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Date.from_iso8601!(\"2026-03-18\")\n~D[2026-03-18]\u{1E}Date.leap_year?\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.leap_year?(Date) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the date falls in a leap year.\u{1F}alx> Date.leap_year?(~D[2024-01-01])\ntrue\u{1E}Date.new\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.new(integer, integer, integer) -> result[Date,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds a date from year, month and day, returning an error for a date that does not exist.\u{1F}alx> Date.new(2026, 3, 18)\nok(~D[2026-03-18])\nalx> case Date.new(2026, 2, 30) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"invalid\"\n...> end\n\"invalid\"\u{1E}Date.new!\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.new!(integer, integer, integer) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds a date from year, month and day; fails for a date that does not exist.\u{1F}alx> Date.new!(2026, 3, 18)\n~D[2026-03-18]\u{1E}Date.quarter_of_year\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.quarter_of_year(Date) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The quarter, from 1 to 4.\u{1F}alx> Date.quarter_of_year(~D[2026-05-01])\n2\u{1E}Date.range\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.range(Date, Date) -> stream[Date]\u{1D}Date.range(Date, Date, integer) -> stream[Date]\u{1F}\u{1F}The dates from one to another, inclusive, a day apart or a given number of days; backwards when the last is earlier.\u{1F}alx> Date.range(~D[2026-01-30], ~D[2026-02-01]) |> Enum.to_list()\n[~D[2026-01-30], ~D[2026-01-31], ~D[2026-02-01]]\nalx> Date.range(~D[2026-01-01], ~D[2026-01-31], 7) |> Enum.count()\n5\u{1E}Date.shift\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.shift(Date, Duration) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}Moves a date by a duration of years, months, weeks and days. A month is added on the calendar: the day is kept, or becomes the last day the new month has. A duration with time units fails.\u{1F}alx> Date.shift(~D[2026-01-31], Duration.months(1))\n~D[2026-02-28]\nalx> Date.shift(~D[2024-02-29], Duration.add(Duration.years(1), Duration.days(1)))\n~D[2025-03-01]\u{1E}Date.to_iso8601\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.to_iso8601(Date) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The date as YYYY-MM-DD text.\u{1F}alx> Date.to_iso8601(~D[2026-03-18])\n\"2026-03-18\"\u{1E}Date.to_string\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.to_string(Date) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The date as YYYY-MM-DD text.\u{1F}alx> Date.to_string(~D[2026-03-18])\n\"2026-03-18\"\u{1E}Date.utc_today\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.utc_today() -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}Today's date in UTC.\u{1F}alx> Date.after?(Date.utc_today(), ~D[2020-01-01])\ntrue\u{1E}Date.week_of_year\u{1F}Date\u{1F}Date.week_of_year(Date) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The ISO 8601 week of the year, from 1 to 53.\u{1F}alx> Date.week_of_year(~D[2026-01-01])\n1\u{1E}DateTime.add\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.add(DateTime, integer) -> DateTime\u{1D}DateTime.add(DateTime, integer, atom) -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a number of seconds.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.add(~U[2026-01-05T23:59:59Z], 2)\n~U[2026-01-06T00:00:01Z]\u{1E}DateTime.after?\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.after?(DateTime, DateTime) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first is later than the second.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.after?(~U[2026-01-06T00:00:00Z], ~U[2026-01-05T00:00:00Z])\ntrue\u{1E}DateTime.before?\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.before?(DateTime, DateTime) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first is earlier than the second.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.before?(~U[2026-01-06T00:00:00Z], ~U[2026-01-05T00:00:00Z])\nfalse\u{1E}DateTime.compare\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.compare(DateTime, DateTime) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns :lt, :eq or :gt as the first is earlier than, equal to or later than the second.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.compare(~U[2026-01-06T00:00:00Z], ~U[2026-01-05T00:00:00Z])\n:gt\u{1E}DateTime.diff\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.diff(DateTime, DateTime) -> integer\u{1D}DateTime.diff(DateTime, DateTime, atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of seconds from the second to the first.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.diff(~U[2026-01-05T00:01:00Z], ~U[2026-01-05T00:00:00Z])\n60\u{1E}DateTime.from_iso8601\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.from_iso8601(string) -> result[tuple[DateTime,integer],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses text with a zone offset, returning the moment in UTC with the offset in seconds.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.from_iso8601(\"2026-01-05T13:04:05+02:00\")\nok({~U[2026-01-05T11:04:05Z], 7200})\u{1E}DateTime.from_naive\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.from_naive(NaiveDateTime, string) -> result[DateTime,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads a date and time as the wall clock of a time zone. A time the clocks skipped moves forward by the gap, and one they repeated takes its first occurrence.\u{1F}alx> case DateTime.from_naive(~N[2026-01-05 13:00:00], \"Europe/Paris\") do\n...>   ok(paris) -> DateTime.to_unix(paris) == DateTime.to_unix(~U[2026-01-05T12:00:00Z])\n...>   error(_) -> false\n...> end\ntrue\u{1E}DateTime.from_naive!\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.from_naive!(NaiveDateTime) -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Interprets a naive date and time as UTC.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.from_naive!(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\n~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z]\u{1E}DateTime.from_unix\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.from_unix(integer) -> result[DateTime,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The moment a number of seconds after the Unix epoch.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.from_unix(86400)\nok(~U[1970-01-02T00:00:00Z])\u{1E}DateTime.from_unix!\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.from_unix!(integer) -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}The moment a number of seconds after the Unix epoch; fails when out of range.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.from_unix!(0)\n~U[1970-01-01T00:00:00Z]\u{1E}DateTime.now\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.now(string) -> result[DateTime,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The current moment in a time zone.\u{1F}alx> case DateTime.now(\"Asia/Tokyo\") do\n...>   ok(now) -> now.utc_offset\n...>   error(_) -> 0\n...> end\n32400\u{1E}DateTime.shift\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.shift(DateTime, Duration) -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Moves a DateTime by a duration, staying in its time zone: calendar units move its wall clock, so a day later is the same time of day across a clock change, and time units are elapsed time.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.shift(~U[2026-01-31T12:00:00Z], Duration.months(1))\n~U[2026-02-28T12:00:00Z]\nalx> paris = DateTime.shift_zone!(~U[2026-03-28T11:00:00Z], \"Europe/Paris\")\nalx> DateTime.to_iso8601(DateTime.shift(paris, Duration.days(1)))\n\"2026-03-29T12:00:00+02:00\"\nalx> DateTime.to_iso8601(DateTime.shift(paris, Duration.hours(24)))\n\"2026-03-29T13:00:00+02:00\"\u{1E}DateTime.shift_zone\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.shift_zone(DateTime, string) -> result[DateTime,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The same moment as the wall clock of another time zone shows it, by its name in the zone database, which is built into the program. An unknown name is an error.\u{1F}alx> case DateTime.shift_zone(~U[2026-01-05T12:00:00Z], \"Europe/Paris\") do\n...>   ok(paris) -> DateTime.to_string(paris)\n...>   error(reason) -> reason\n...> end\n\"2026-01-05 13:00:00+01:00 CET Europe/Paris\"\nalx> DateTime.shift_zone(~U[2026-01-05T12:00:00Z], \"Mars/Olympus\")\nerror(\"unknown time zone \\\"Mars/Olympus\\\"\")\u{1E}DateTime.shift_zone!\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.shift_zone!(DateTime, string) -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}The same moment in another time zone; fails on an unknown zone name. The time_zone and utc_offset fields say where it is.\u{1F}alx> new_york = DateTime.shift_zone!(~U[2026-07-04T16:00:00Z], \"America/New_York\")\nalx> DateTime.to_iso8601(new_york)\n\"2026-07-04T12:00:00-04:00\"\nalx> new_york.utc_offset\n-14400\nalx> DateTime.compare(new_york, ~U[2026-07-04T16:00:00Z])\n:eq\nalx> Calendar.strftime(new_york, \"%H:%M %Z %z\")\n\"12:00 EDT -0400\"\u{1E}DateTime.to_date\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.to_date(DateTime) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}The date part.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.to_date(~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z])\n~D[2026-01-05]\u{1E}DateTime.to_iso8601\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.to_iso8601(DateTime) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The value as ISO 8601 text, ending in Z in UTC and in the offset, such as +01:00, in another zone.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.to_iso8601(~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z])\n\"2026-01-05T13:04:05Z\"\u{1E}DateTime.to_naive\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.to_naive(DateTime) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}The same date and time without the zone.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.to_naive(~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z])\n~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05]\u{1E}DateTime.to_string\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.to_string(DateTime) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The value as text, ending in Z in UTC; in another zone, with the offset, the zone's abbreviation and its name.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.to_string(~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z])\n\"2026-01-05 13:04:05Z\"\u{1E}DateTime.to_time\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.to_time(DateTime) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}The time part.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.to_time(~U[2026-01-05T13:04:05Z])\n~T[13:04:05]\u{1E}DateTime.to_unix\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.to_unix(DateTime) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Seconds since the Unix epoch.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.to_unix(~U[1970-01-02T00:00:00Z])\n86400\u{1E}DateTime.truncate\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.truncate(DateTime, atom) -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops precision, as Time.truncate does.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.truncate(~U[2026-01-01T10:00:00.5Z], :second)\n~U[2026-01-01T10:00:00Z]\u{1E}DateTime.utc_now\u{1F}DateTime\u{1F}DateTime.utc_now() -> DateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}The current moment.\u{1F}alx> DateTime.after?(DateTime.utc_now(), ~U[2020-01-01T00:00:00Z])\ntrue\u{1E}DiskStore.close\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.close(disk_store) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes a store once the writes under way are on the disk; false when it was closed already. A closed store refuses writes with error(\"closed\"), and reading one fails.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\nfalse\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"k\", \"v\")\nerror(\"closed\")\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.commit\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.commit(disk_store, list[tuple[string,string]], list[string]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Makes several changes as one: the {key, value} pairs are stored and the listed keys removed, and after a crash either all of it is there or none.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"cart:7\", \"pen\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.commit(store, [{\"order:1\", \"pen\"}, {\"stock:pen\", \"4\"}], [\"cart:7\"])\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.keys(store)\n[\"order:1\", \"stock:pen\"]\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.compact\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.compact(disk_store) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes the file afresh with only what the store holds now, dropping the record of earlier values. A store does this by itself when its file has grown well past what it holds, so calling it is seldom needed.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"k\", \"old\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"k\", \"new\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.compact(store)\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.get(store, \"k\")\nsome(\"new\")\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.delete\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.delete(disk_store, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes a key; a key that holds nothing is left as it is.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"k\", \"v\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.delete(store, \"k\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.get(store, \"k\")\nnone\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.get\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.get(disk_store, string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The value under a key, or none.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.get(store, \"missing\")\nnone\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.has_key?\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.has_key?(disk_store, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether a key holds a value.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"k\", \"\")\nok({})\nalx> {DiskStore.has_key?(store, \"k\"), DiskStore.has_key?(store, \"other\")}\n{true, false}\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.keys\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.keys(disk_store) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Every key, in order.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.commit(store, [{\"b\", \"2\"}, {\"a\", \"1\"}], [])\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.keys(store)\n[\"a\", \"b\"]\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.open\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.open(string) -> result[disk_store,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Opens the store in a file, making the file when there is none. What a crash cut short at the end of the file is dropped. Opening a path that is already open gives the same store, and one program opens a given file at a time. A file that is something else is an error. Every key and value is also held in memory, where reads are answered, so a store suits data that fits there. Values of other types are kept as text: JSON.encode! writes one and JSON.decode_as reads it back.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"greeting\", \"hello\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> ok(again) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.get(again, \"greeting\")\nsome(\"hello\")\nalx> DiskStore.close(again)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.prefix\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.prefix(disk_store, string) -> list[tuple[string,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The {key, value} pairs whose keys begin with a prefix, in key order: with keys written as kind:id, everything of one kind.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.commit(store, [{\"user:1\", \"al\"}, {\"user:2\", \"bo\"}, {\"visit:1\", \"x\"}], [])\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.prefix(store, \"user:\")\n[{\"user:1\", \"al\"}, {\"user:2\", \"bo\"}]\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.put\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.put(disk_store, string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Stores a value under a key, replacing what the key held, and returns when the change is on the disk.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"user:1\", \"al\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.get(store, \"user:1\")\nsome(\"al\")\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.range\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.range(disk_store, string, string) -> list[tuple[string,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The {key, value} pairs whose keys are at or after the first key and before the second, in key order.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.commit(store, [{\"a\", \"1\"}, {\"b\", \"2\"}, {\"c\", \"3\"}], [])\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.range(store, \"a\", \"c\")\n[{\"a\", \"1\"}, {\"b\", \"2\"}]\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.size\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.size(disk_store) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of keys.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> DiskStore.put(store, \"k\", \"v\")\nok({})\nalx> DiskStore.size(store)\n1\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}DiskStore.update\u{1F}DiskStore\u{1F}DiskStore.update(disk_store, string, fn[option[string],option[string]]) -> result[option[string],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Changes one key by a function of what it holds, none when it holds nothing: the function gives some(value) to store or none to remove the key, and no other write to the store comes between its reading and its writing. The result is what the function gave. The function must not itself write to the store, which waits for it.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"store_doc_\" <> Base.encode16(Crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6)))\nalx> ok(store) = DiskStore.open(path)\nalx> count = fn(held: option[string]): option[string] do\n...>   case held do\n...>     some(n) -> some(Integer.to_string(String.to_integer(n) + 1))\n...>     none -> some(\"1\")\n...>   end\n...> end\nalx> DiskStore.update(store, \"visits\", count)\nok(some(\"1\"))\nalx> DiskStore.update(store, \"visits\", count)\nok(some(\"2\"))\nalx> DiskStore.close(store)\ntrue\nalx> File.rm(path)\nok({})\u{1E}Duration.add\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.add(Duration, Duration) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds two durations unit by unit. Nothing is carried from one unit to another.\u{1F}alx> Duration.add(Duration.months(1), Duration.days(10)) |> Duration.to_iso8601()\n\"P1M10D\"\u{1E}Duration.days\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.days(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many days; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.days(3).day\n3\u{1E}Duration.from_iso8601\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.from_iso8601(string) -> result[Duration,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses an ISO 8601 duration such as P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S.\u{1F}alx> Duration.from_iso8601(\"P2Y1M4DT3H7M1.5S\")\nok(%Duration{year: 2, month: 1, week: 0, day: 4, hour: 3, minute: 7, second: 1, microsecond: 500000})\nalx> case Duration.from_iso8601(\"soon\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"invalid\"\n...> end\n\"invalid\"\u{1E}Duration.hours\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.hours(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many hours; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.hours(3).hour\n3\u{1E}Duration.microseconds\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.microseconds(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many microseconds; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.microseconds(3).microsecond\n3\u{1E}Duration.minutes\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.minutes(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many minutes; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.minutes(3).minute\n3\u{1E}Duration.months\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.months(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many months; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.months(3).month\n3\u{1E}Duration.multiply\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.multiply(Duration, integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}Multiplies every unit.\u{1F}alx> Duration.multiply(Duration.hours(2), 3).hour\n6\u{1E}Duration.negate\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.negate(Duration) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}The same amounts with the opposite sign, for shifting backwards.\u{1F}alx> Date.shift(~D[2026-03-31], Duration.negate(Duration.months(1)))\n~D[2026-02-28]\u{1E}Duration.new!\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.new!(list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration from a keyword list of year:, month:, week:, day:, hour:, minute:, second: and microsecond:.\u{1F}alx> Duration.new!([hour: 2, minute: 30]).minute\n30\u{1E}Duration.seconds\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.seconds(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many seconds; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.seconds(3).second\n3\u{1E}Duration.subtract\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.subtract(Duration, Duration) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}Subtracts the second duration from the first, unit by unit.\u{1F}alx> Duration.subtract(Duration.days(10), Duration.days(3)).day\n7\u{1E}Duration.to_iso8601\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.to_iso8601(Duration) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The duration in ISO 8601 form, leaving out units that are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.to_iso8601(Duration.add(Duration.years(1), Duration.minutes(90)))\n\"P1YT90M\"\nalx> Duration.to_iso8601(Duration.days(0))\n\"PT0S\"\u{1E}Duration.weeks\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.weeks(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many weeks; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.weeks(3).week\n3\u{1E}Duration.years\u{1F}Duration\u{1F}Duration.years(integer) -> Duration\u{1F}\u{1F}A duration of that many years; the other units are zero.\u{1F}alx> Duration.years(3).year\n3\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.alive\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.alive(dynamic_supervisor) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the supervisor is running.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.alive(sup)\ntrue\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.count_children\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.count_children(dynamic_supervisor) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}How many children are running.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.count_children(sup)\n0\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.restarts\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.restarts(dynamic_supervisor) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}How many times children have been restarted.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(worker) = DynamicSupervisor.start_child(sup, fn(self: pid[string]): integer do\n...>   Process.crash(\"boom\")\n...> end)\nalx> Process.sleep(50)\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.restarts(sup) > 0\ntrue\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.start_child\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.start_child(dynamic_supervisor, fn[pid[a],integer]) -> result[pid[a],string]\u{1D}DynamicSupervisor.start_child(dynamic_supervisor, fn[pid[a],integer], atom) -> result[pid[a],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a process under the supervisor and returns its pid. The restart is :permanent (restarted whenever it ends, the default), :transient (restarted when it fails) or :temporary (never restarted). A restarted child is a new process with a new pid.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(worker) = DynamicSupervisor.start_child(sup, fn(self: pid[string]): integer do\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end)\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.count_children(sup)\n1\nalx> ok(once) = DynamicSupervisor.start_child(sup, fn(self: pid[string]): integer do 0 end, :temporary)\nalx> Process.await(once, 1000)\n{true, \"normal\"}\nalx> Process.sleep(20)\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.count_children(sup)\n1\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.start_link\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.start_link() -> dynamic_supervisor\u{1D}DynamicSupervisor.start_link(list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> dynamic_supervisor\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a supervisor with no children. It gives up, stopping its children, after more than max_restarts restarts within max_seconds: 3 within 5 unless the options say otherwise. With max_children: it runs at most that many children at once, and start_child returns error(\"max_children\") beyond them.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.count_children(sup)\n0\nalx> strict = DynamicSupervisor.start_link([max_restarts: 1, max_seconds: 10])\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.alive(strict)\ntrue\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.start_server\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.start_server(dynamic_supervisor, s, fn[s,q,tuple[s,r]]) -> result[server[q,r],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a server under the supervisor. The server restarts itself after a handler failure, within the supervisor's limits, so its handle stays good; it stops when the supervisor stops.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(counter) = DynamicSupervisor.start_server(sup, 0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 2, 1000)\nok(2)\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.stop(sup)\ntrue\nalx> Server.alive(counter)\nfalse\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.stop\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.stop(dynamic_supervisor) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops the supervisor and its children, waiting up to five seconds; true when they ended.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.stop(sup)\ntrue\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.alive(sup)\nfalse\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.terminate_child\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.terminate_child(dynamic_supervisor, pid[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops a child and forgets it, so it is not restarted; false when the pid is not a child.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(worker) = DynamicSupervisor.start_child(sup, fn(self: pid[string]): integer do\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end)\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.terminate_child(sup, worker)\ntrue\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.count_children(sup)\n0\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.terminate_child(sup, worker)\nfalse\u{1E}DynamicSupervisor.which_children\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor\u{1F}DynamicSupervisor.which_children(dynamic_supervisor) -> list[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The process ids of the running children, in the order they were started.\u{1F}alx> sup = DynamicSupervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(worker) = DynamicSupervisor.start_child(sup, fn(self: pid[string]): integer do\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end)\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.which_children(sup) == [Process.id(worker)]\ntrue\u{1E}Enum.all?\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.all?(enum[boolean]) -> boolean\u{1D}Enum.all?(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether every element is true, or satisfies the function.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?([2, 4, 6], fn n -> rem(n, 2) == 0 end)\ntrue\nalx> Enum.all?([true, false])\nfalse\u{1E}Enum.any?\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.any?(enum[boolean]) -> boolean\u{1D}Enum.any?(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether any element is true, or satisfies the function.\u{1F}alx> Enum.any?([1, 2, 3], &(&1 > 2))\ntrue\nalx> Enum.any?([false, false])\nfalse\u{1E}Enum.at\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.at(enum[a], integer, a) -> a\u{1D}Enum.at(enum[a], integer) -> option[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the element at a zero-based index as an option, or the given default. Negative indexes count from the end.\u{1F}alx> Enum.at([10, 20, 30], 1)\nsome(20)\nalx> Enum.at([10, 20, 30], -1)\nsome(30)\nalx> Enum.at([10, 20, 30], 9, 0)\n0\u{1E}Enum.chunk_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.chunk_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> list[list[a]]\u{1F}b supports equality\u{1F}Splits into lists, starting a new one each time the function's result changes.\u{1F}alx> Enum.chunk_by([1, 3, 2, 4, 5], &(rem(&1, 2) == 1))\n[[1, 3], [2, 4], [5]]\u{1E}Enum.chunk_every\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.chunk_every(enum[a], integer) -> list[list[a]]\u{1D}Enum.chunk_every(enum[a], integer, integer) -> list[list[a]]\u{1D}Enum.chunk_every(enum[a], integer, integer, atom) -> list[list[a]]\u{1D}Enum.chunk_every(enum[a], integer, integer, enum[a]) -> list[list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits into lists of count elements; with a step, a new chunk starts every step elements. The last chunk may be shorter.\u{1F}alx> Enum.chunk_every([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], 2)\n[[10, 20], [30, 40], [50]]\nalx> Enum.chunk_every([5, 6, 7, 8], 3, 1)\n[[5, 6, 7], [6, 7, 8]]\u{1E}Enum.chunk_while\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.chunk_while(enum[a], s, fn[a,s,tuple[option[c],s]], fn[s,option[c]]) -> list[c]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits into chunks under the control of a state. The step function takes an element and the state and returns {emit, next_state}, where emit is some(chunk) to produce a chunk or none; the last function may produce one more chunk from the final state.\u{1F}alx> Enum.chunk_while([1, 2, 6, 7, 20], Enum.take([0], 0), fn n, run ->\n...>   case run do\n...>     [last | _] when n - last > 1 -> {some(Enum.reverse(run)), [n]}\n...>     _ -> {none, prepend(run, n)}\n...>   end\n...> end, fn run -> if run == [] do none else some(Enum.reverse(run)) end end)\n[[1, 2], [6, 7], [20]]\u{1E}Enum.concat\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.concat(enum[list[a]]) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.concat(enum[a], enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Joins two enumerables, or a list of lists, into one list.\u{1F}alx> Enum.concat([1, 2], 3..4)\n[1, 2, 3, 4]\nalx> Enum.concat([[1], [2, 3]])\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}Enum.count\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.count(enum[a]) -> integer\u{1D}Enum.count(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Counts the elements, or those satisfying the function.\u{1F}alx> Enum.count([1, 2, 3])\n3\nalx> Enum.count([1, 2, 3], &(&1 > 1))\n2\u{1E}Enum.count_until\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.count_until(enum[a], integer) -> integer\u{1D}Enum.count_until(enum[a], fn[a,boolean], integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Counts elements, or those satisfying the function, stopping at the limit.\u{1F}alx> Enum.count_until(1..1000000, 5)\n5\nalx> Enum.count_until([1, 2, 3], &(&1 > 1), 10)\n2\u{1E}Enum.dedup\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.dedup(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Removes consecutive duplicate elements.\u{1F}alx> Enum.dedup([1, 1, 2, 2, 1])\n[1, 2, 1]\u{1E}Enum.dedup_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.dedup_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> list[a]\u{1F}b supports equality\u{1F}Removes consecutive elements for which the function returns the same value.\u{1F}alx> Enum.dedup_by([1, 3, 2, 4, 5], &rem(&1, 2))\n[1, 2, 5]\u{1E}Enum.drop\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.drop(enum[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops the first count elements, or the last when count is negative.\u{1F}alx> Enum.drop([1, 2, 3, 4], 1)\n[2, 3, 4]\nalx> Enum.drop([1, 2, 3, 4], -1)\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}Enum.drop_every\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.drop_every(enum[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops every nth element, starting with the first.\u{1F}alx> Enum.drop_every([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)\n[2, 4]\u{1E}Enum.drop_while\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.drop_while(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops elements from the start while the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Enum.drop_while([1, 2, 3, 1], &(&1 < 3))\n[3, 1]\u{1E}Enum.each\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.each(enum[a], fn[a,b])\u{1F}\u{1F}Calls the function with each element, for its effect.\u{1F}alx> Enum.each([\"a\", \"b\"], fn text -> IO.write(text) end)\u{1E}Enum.empty?\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.empty?(enum[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether there are no elements.\u{1F}alx> Enum.empty?([1])\nfalse\nalx> Enum.empty?(Enum.filter([1], &(&1 > 5)))\ntrue\u{1E}Enum.fetch\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.fetch(enum[a], integer) -> option[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the element at an index as an option.\u{1F}alx> Enum.fetch([10, 20], 1)\nsome(20)\nalx> Enum.fetch([10, 20], 5)\nnone\u{1E}Enum.fetch!\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.fetch!(enum[a], integer) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the element at an index, failing when it is out of bounds.\u{1F}alx> Enum.fetch!([10, 20], 0)\n10\u{1E}Enum.filter\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.filter(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Keeps the elements for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Enum.filter([1, 2, 3, 4], fn n -> rem(n, 2) == 0 end)\n[2, 4]\u{1E}Enum.find\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.find(enum[a], a, fn[a,boolean]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.find(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> option[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the first element satisfying the function, as an option or with a default.\u{1F}alx> Enum.find([1, 2, 3], &(&1 > 1))\nsome(2)\nalx> Enum.find([1, 2, 3], 0, &(&1 > 5))\n0\u{1E}Enum.find_index\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.find_index(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> option[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the index of the first element satisfying the function.\u{1F}alx> Enum.find_index([\"a\", \"b\"], &(&1 == \"b\"))\nsome(1)\u{1E}Enum.find_value\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.find_value(enum[a], b, fn[a,option[b]]) -> b\u{1D}Enum.find_value(enum[a], fn[a,option[b]]) -> option[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the first some(...) the function produces, or with a default, the value inside it or the default when there is none.\u{1F}alx> Enum.find_value([1, 2, 3], fn n -> if n > 1, do: some(n * 10), else: none end)\nsome(20)\nalx> Enum.find_value([1, 3], 0, fn n -> if rem(n, 2) == 0, do: some(n), else: none end)\n0\u{1E}Enum.flat_map\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.flat_map(enum[a], fn[a,list[b]]) -> list[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Maps each element to a list and concatenates the results.\u{1F}alx> Enum.flat_map([1, 2], fn n -> [n, n * 10] end)\n[1, 10, 2, 20]\u{1E}Enum.flat_map_reduce\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.flat_map_reduce(enum[a], c, fn[a,c,tuple[list[b],c]]) -> tuple[list[b],c]\u{1F}\u{1F}Maps to lists and threads an accumulator, returning the concatenation and the final accumulator.\u{1F}alx> Enum.flat_map_reduce([1, 2], 0, fn n, acc -> {[n, n], acc + n} end)\n{[1, 1, 2, 2], 3}\u{1E}Enum.frequencies\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.frequencies(enum[a]) -> map[a,integer]\u{1F}a is a map key type\u{1F}Counts how often each element occurs.\u{1F}alx> Enum.frequencies([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"])\n%{\"a\" => 2, \"b\" => 1}\u{1E}Enum.frequencies_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.frequencies_by(enum[a], fn[a,k]) -> map[k,integer]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Counts how often each result of the function occurs.\u{1F}alx> Enum.frequencies_by([\"aa\", \"b\", \"cc\"], &String.length(&1))\n%{1 => 1, 2 => 2}\u{1E}Enum.group_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.group_by(enum[a], fn[a,k]) -> map[k,list[a]]\u{1D}Enum.group_by(enum[a], fn[a,k], fn[a,v]) -> map[k,list[v]]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Groups elements into a map keyed by the function's result; a second function transforms the grouped values.\u{1F}alx> Enum.group_by([1, 2, 3, 4], &rem(&1, 2))\n%{0 => [2, 4], 1 => [1, 3]}\nalx> Enum.group_by([\"ant\", \"bee\", \"asp\"], &String.slice(&1, 0, 1), &String.length(&1))\n%{\"a\" => [3, 3], \"b\" => [3]}\u{1E}Enum.intersperse\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.intersperse(enum[a], a) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Places the separator between elements.\u{1F}alx> Enum.intersperse([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], \"/\")\n[\"a\", \"/\", \"b\", \"/\", \"c\"]\u{1E}Enum.into\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.into(enum[a], c) -> c\u{1D}Enum.into(enum[x], c, fn[x,a]) -> c\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds the elements to a collectable: a list or set takes them as they are, a map takes {key, value} tuples, and a string takes strings. An empty %{} or [] takes its type from the elements. for ... into: does the same.\u{1F}alx> Enum.into([{\"a\", 1}], %{\"z\" => 0})\n%{\"a\" => 1, \"z\" => 0}\nalx> Enum.into([1, 2], [0])\n[0, 1, 2]\nalx> Enum.into([\"b\", \"c\"], \"a\")\n\"abc\"\nalx> Enum.into([3, 1, 3], MapSet.new([2])) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[1, 2, 3]\nalx> Enum.into([{1, \"one\"}], %{})\n%{1 => \"one\"}\u{1E}Enum.join\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.join(enum[a]) -> string\u{1D}Enum.join(enum[a], string) -> string\u{1F}a is a string, integer, float or boolean\u{1F}Joins strings, integers, floats or booleans into a string, with an optional separator.\u{1F}alx> Enum.join([1, 2, 3], \", \")\n\"1, 2, 3\"\nalx> Enum.join([\"a\", \"b\"])\n\"ab\"\u{1E}Enum.map\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.map(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> list[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns a list with the function applied to each element.\u{1F}alx> Enum.map([1, 2, 3], fn n -> n * 2 end)\n[2, 4, 6]\nalx> Enum.map(%{\"a\" => 1}, fn {key, value} -> \"\#{key}=\#{value}\" end)\n[\"a=1\"]\u{1E}Enum.map_every\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.map_every(enum[a], integer, fn[a,a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Applies the function to every nth element, starting with the first.\u{1F}alx> Enum.map_every([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2, &(&1 * 10))\n[10, 2, 30, 4, 50]\u{1E}Enum.map_intersperse\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.map_intersperse(enum[a], b, fn[a,b]) -> list[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Maps each element and places the separator between the results.\u{1F}alx> Enum.map_intersperse([1, 2], \"-\", &Integer.to_string(&1))\n[\"1\", \"-\", \"2\"]\u{1E}Enum.map_join\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.map_join(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> string\u{1D}Enum.map_join(enum[a], string, fn[a,b]) -> string\u{1F}b is a string, integer, float or boolean\u{1F}Maps each element and joins the results into a string.\u{1F}alx> Enum.map_join([4, 5, 6], \"+\", &(&1 * 10))\n\"40+50+60\"\u{1E}Enum.map_reduce\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.map_reduce(enum[a], c, fn[a,c,tuple[b,c]]) -> tuple[list[b],c]\u{1F}\u{1F}Maps while threading an accumulator, returning the mapped list and the final accumulator.\u{1F}alx> Enum.map_reduce([5, 6, 7], 0, fn n, acc -> {n * 2, acc + n} end)\n{[10, 12, 14], 18}\u{1E}Enum.max\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.max(enum[a]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.max(enum[a], fn[a]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.max(enum[a], fn[a,a,boolean]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.max(enum[a], fn[a,a,boolean], fn[a]) -> a\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Returns the greatest element. On an empty enumerable it fails, or returns what the given function returns.\u{1F}alx> Enum.max([3, 9, 2])\n9\nalx> Enum.max(Enum.take([1], 0), fn -> 0 end)\n0\u{1E}Enum.max_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.max_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.max_by(enum[a], fn[a,b], fn[b,b,boolean]) -> a\u{1F}b has an order\u{1F}Returns the element for which the function's result is greatest.\u{1F}alx> Enum.max_by([\"a\", \"ccc\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1))\n\"ccc\"\u{1E}Enum.member?\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.member?(enum[a], a) -> boolean\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Reports whether the enumerable contains the value. The in operator does the same.\u{1F}alx> Enum.member?([1, 2, 3], 2)\ntrue\nalx> 5 in 1..3\nfalse\u{1E}Enum.min\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.min(enum[a]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.min(enum[a], fn[a]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.min(enum[a], fn[a,a,boolean]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.min(enum[a], fn[a,a,boolean], fn[a]) -> a\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Returns the least element. On an empty enumerable it fails, or returns what the given function returns.\u{1F}alx> Enum.min([3, 9, 2])\n2\nalx> Enum.min(Enum.take([1], 0), fn -> 0 end)\n0\u{1E}Enum.min_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.min_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.min_by(enum[a], fn[a,b], fn[b,b,boolean]) -> a\u{1F}b has an order\u{1F}Returns the element for which the function's result is least.\u{1F}alx> Enum.min_by([\"ccc\", \"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1))\n\"a\"\u{1E}Enum.min_max\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.min_max(enum[a]) -> tuple[a,a]\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Returns the least and greatest elements as a tuple.\u{1F}alx> Enum.min_max([3, 9, 2])\n{2, 9}\u{1E}Enum.min_max_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.min_max_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> tuple[a,a]\u{1F}b has an order\u{1F}Returns the elements with the least and greatest function results.\u{1F}alx> Enum.min_max_by([\"ccc\", \"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1))\n{\"a\", \"ccc\"}\u{1E}Enum.product\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.product(enum[a]) -> a\u{1F}a is an integer or float\u{1F}Multiplies integers or floats.\u{1F}alx> Enum.product([2, 3, 4])\n24\u{1E}Enum.product_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.product_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> b\u{1F}b is an integer or float\u{1F}Multiplies the function's results.\u{1F}alx> Enum.product_by([1, 2, 3], &(&1 + 1))\n24\u{1E}Enum.random\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.random(enum[a]) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns a random element; fails on an empty enumerable. Seeded by ALCHEM_SEED.\u{1F}alx> Enum.random([7, 7, 7])\n7\u{1E}Enum.reduce\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.reduce(enum[a], fn[a,a,a]) -> a\u{1D}Enum.reduce(enum[a], b, fn[a,b,b]) -> b\u{1F}\u{1F}Folds the elements into one value. The function takes the element first, then the accumulator; without an initial value the first element starts.\u{1F}alx> Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, fn n, acc -> acc + n end)\n6\nalx> Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], &(&1 * &2))\n6\u{1E}Enum.reduce_while\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.reduce_while(enum[a], b, fn[a,b,step[b]]) -> b\u{1F}\u{1F}Folds until the function returns halt(acc); cont(acc) continues.\u{1F}alx> Enum.reduce_while(1..100, 0, fn n, acc -> if acc > 5 do halt(acc) else cont(acc + n) end end)\n6\u{1E}Enum.reject\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.reject(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops the elements for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Enum.reject([1, 2, 3, 4], &(rem(&1, 2) == 0))\n[1, 3]\u{1E}Enum.reverse\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.reverse(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.reverse(enum[a], enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reverses the elements; with a second enumerable, appends it after the reversed elements.\u{1F}alx> Enum.reverse([1, 2, 3])\n[3, 2, 1]\nalx> Enum.reverse([1, 2], [9])\n[2, 1, 9]\u{1E}Enum.reverse_slice\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.reverse_slice(enum[a], integer, integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reverses count elements starting at an index.\u{1F}alx> Enum.reverse_slice([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], 1, 3)\n[10, 40, 30, 20, 50]\u{1E}Enum.scan\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.scan(enum[a], fn[a,a,a]) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.scan(enum[a], b, fn[a,b,b]) -> list[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns every intermediate result of a fold.\u{1F}alx> Enum.scan([1, 2, 3, 4], &(&1 + &2))\n[1, 3, 6, 10]\nalx> Enum.scan([1, 2], 10, &(&1 + &2))\n[11, 13]\u{1E}Enum.shuffle\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.shuffle(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the elements in random order. Seeded by ALCHEM_SEED.\u{1F}alx> Enum.sort(Enum.shuffle([3, 1, 2]))\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}Enum.slice\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.slice(enum[a], Range) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.slice(enum[a], integer, integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns count elements starting at an index, or the elements at a range of indexes; a negative index counts from the end.\u{1F}alx> Enum.slice([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], 1, 3)\n[20, 30, 40]\nalx> Enum.slice([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], -2, 2)\n[40, 50]\nalx> Enum.slice([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], 1..-2)\n[20, 30, 40]\u{1E}Enum.slide\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.slide(enum[a], integer, integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Moves the element at an index to another, shifting those between.\u{1F}alx> Enum.slide([:a, :b, :c, :d], 0, 2)\n[:b, :c, :a, :d]\u{1E}Enum.sort\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.sort(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.sort(enum[a], atom) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.sort(enum[a], fn[a,a,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Sorts ascending, or by a function saying whether its first argument may come before its second.\u{1F}alx> Enum.sort([3, 1, 2])\n[1, 2, 3]\nalx> Enum.sort([3, 1, 2], &(&1 >= &2))\n[3, 2, 1]\u{1E}Enum.sort_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.sort_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.sort_by(enum[a], fn[a,b], atom) -> list[a]\u{1D}Enum.sort_by(enum[a], fn[a,b], fn[b,b,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}b has an order\u{1F}Sorts by the function's result, optionally with a sorter for those results.\u{1F}alx> Enum.sort_by([\"ccc\", \"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1))\n[\"a\", \"bb\", \"ccc\"]\nalx> Enum.sort_by([\"ccc\", \"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1), &(&1 >= &2))\n[\"ccc\", \"bb\", \"a\"]\u{1E}Enum.split\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.split(enum[a], integer) -> tuple[list[a],list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits into the first count elements and the rest.\u{1F}alx> Enum.split([1, 2, 3], 2)\n{[1, 2], [3]}\u{1E}Enum.split_while\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.split_while(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> tuple[list[a],list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits at the first element for which the function returns false.\u{1F}alx> Enum.split_while([1, 2, 3, 1], &(&1 < 3))\n{[1, 2], [3, 1]}\u{1E}Enum.split_with\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.split_with(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> tuple[list[a],list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Partitions into the elements that satisfy the function and those that do not.\u{1F}alx> Enum.split_with([1, 2, 3, 4], &(rem(&1, 2) == 0))\n{[2, 4], [1, 3]}\u{1E}Enum.sum\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.sum(enum[a]) -> a\u{1F}a is an integer or float\u{1F}Adds integers or floats.\u{1F}alx> Enum.sum(1..100)\n5050\nalx> Enum.sum([1.5, 2.5])\n4.0\u{1E}Enum.sum_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.sum_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> b\u{1F}b is an integer or float\u{1F}Adds the function's results.\u{1F}alx> Enum.sum_by([\"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1))\n3\u{1E}Enum.take\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.take(enum[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes the first count elements, or the last when count is negative.\u{1F}alx> Enum.take([1, 2, 3], 2)\n[1, 2]\nalx> Enum.take([1, 2, 3], -1)\n[3]\u{1E}Enum.take_every\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.take_every(enum[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes every nth element, starting with the first.\u{1F}alx> Enum.take_every([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)\n[1, 3, 5]\u{1E}Enum.take_random\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.take_random(enum[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes count random elements. Seeded by ALCHEM_SEED.\u{1F}alx> length(Enum.take_random([1, 2, 3, 4], 2))\n2\u{1E}Enum.take_while\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.take_while(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes elements from the start while the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Enum.take_while([1, 2, 3, 1], &(&1 < 3))\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Enum.to_list\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.to_list(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Collects any enumerable into a list.\u{1F}alx> Enum.to_list(1..3)\n[1, 2, 3]\nalx> Enum.to_list(%{\"a\" => 1})\n[{\"a\", 1}]\u{1E}Enum.uniq\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.uniq(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Removes duplicate elements, keeping the first of each.\u{1F}alx> Enum.uniq([1, 2, 1, 3, 2])\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}Enum.uniq_by\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.uniq_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> list[a]\u{1F}b supports equality\u{1F}Removes elements whose function result has been seen before.\u{1F}alx> Enum.uniq_by([\"a\", \"bb\", \"c\"], &String.length(&1))\n[\"a\", \"bb\"]\u{1E}Enum.unzip\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.unzip(enum[tuple[a,b]]) -> tuple[list[a],list[b]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits a list of pairs into a pair of lists.\u{1F}alx> Enum.unzip([{1, \"a\"}, {2, \"b\"}])\n{[1, 2], [\"a\", \"b\"]}\u{1E}Enum.with_index\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.with_index(enum[a]) -> list[tuple[a,integer]]\u{1D}Enum.with_index(enum[a], fn[a,integer,b]) -> list[b]\u{1D}Enum.with_index(enum[a], integer) -> list[tuple[a,integer]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Pairs each element with its index, optionally starting from an offset.\u{1F}alx> Enum.with_index([\"a\", \"b\"])\n[{\"a\", 0}, {\"b\", 1}]\nalx> Enum.with_index([\"a\"], 5)\n[{\"a\", 5}]\u{1E}Enum.zip\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.zip(enum[list[a]]) -> list[list[a]]\u{1D}Enum.zip(enum[a], enum[b]) -> list[tuple[a,b]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Pairs elements of two enumerables, stopping at the shorter.\u{1F}alx> Enum.zip([1, 2, 3], [\"a\", \"b\"])\n[{1, \"a\"}, {2, \"b\"}]\u{1E}Enum.zip_reduce\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.zip_reduce(enum[a], enum[b], c, fn[a,b,c,c]) -> c\u{1F}\u{1F}Folds over the paired elements of two enumerables.\u{1F}alx> Enum.zip_reduce([2, 3], [4, 5], 0, fn a, b, acc -> acc + a * b end)\n23\u{1E}Enum.zip_with\u{1F}Enum\u{1F}Enum.zip_with(enum[a], enum[b], fn[a,b,c]) -> list[c]\u{1D}Enum.zip_with(enum[list[a]], fn[list[a],b]) -> list[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Combines paired elements of two enumerables with the function.\u{1F}alx> Enum.zip_with([1, 2], [10, 20], &(&1 + &2))\n[11, 22]\u{1E}File.append\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.append(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Appends text, or any bytes, to a file, creating it when missing.\u{1F}alx> File.write(\"doc_append.txt\", \"a\")\nok({})\nalx> File.append(\"doc_append.txt\", \"b\")\nok({})\nalx> File.read(\"doc_append.txt\")\nok(\"ab\")\u{1E}File.chmod\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.chmod(string, integer) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets a file's permission bits, written in octal as 0o644.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"file_doc.txt\")\nalx> File.write(path, \"z\")\nok({})\nalx> File.chmod(path, 0o600)\nok({})\nalx> ok(stat) = File.stat(path)\nalx> stat.access in [:read_write, :read]\ntrue\u{1E}File.close\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.close(file) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes a file opened with File.open; false when it was already closed.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"file_doc.txt\")\nalx> ok(f) = File.open(path, [:write])\nalx> File.close(f)\ntrue\nalx> File.close(f)\nfalse\u{1E}File.cp\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.cp(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Copies a file.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_cp.txt\", \"data\")\nalx> File.cp(\"doc_cp.txt\", \"doc_cp_copy.txt\")\nok({})\nalx> File.read!(\"doc_cp_copy.txt\")\n\"data\"\u{1E}File.cp_r\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.cp_r(string, string) -> result[list[string],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Copies a file, or a directory and everything in it, and returns the paths written.\u{1F}alx> from = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"cp_r_doc\")\nalx> File.rm_rf(from)\nalx> File.mkdir_p(from)\nalx> File.write(Path.join(from, \"a.txt\"), \"a\")\nok({})\nalx> to = from <> \"_copy\"\nalx> File.rm_rf(to)\nalx> ok(written) = File.cp_r(from, to)\nalx> length(written)\n2\nalx> File.read(Path.join(to, \"a.txt\"))\nok(\"a\")\u{1E}File.cwd\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.cwd() -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The working directory, as a result.\u{1F}alx> File.cwd() == ok(File.cwd!())\ntrue\u{1E}File.cwd!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.cwd!() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The working directory; fails when it cannot be determined.\u{1F}alx> String.length(File.cwd!()) > 0\ntrue\u{1E}File.dir?\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.dir?(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the path is a directory.\u{1F}alx> File.dir?(\".\")\ntrue\u{1E}File.exists\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.exists(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the path exists. Same as File.exists?.\u{1F}alx> File.exists(\"no_such_file.txt\")\nfalse\u{1E}File.exists?\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.exists?(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the path exists.\u{1F}alx> File.exists?(\".\")\ntrue\u{1E}File.list\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.list(string) -> result[list[string],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The names in a directory, sorted. Same as File.ls.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir_p(\"doc_list\")\nok({})\nalx> File.write!(\"doc_list/a.txt\", \"\")\nalx> File.list(\"doc_list\")\nok([\"a.txt\"])\u{1E}File.ln\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.ln(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Makes a hard link: a second name for the same file.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"file_doc.txt\")\nalx> File.write(path, \"x\")\nok({})\nalx> link = path <> \".hard\"\nalx> File.rm(link)\nalx> File.ln(path, link)\nok({})\nalx> File.read(link)\nok(\"x\")\u{1E}File.ln_s\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.ln_s(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Makes a symbolic link to a path.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"file_doc.txt\")\nalx> File.write(path, \"y\")\nok({})\nalx> link = path <> \".soft\"\nalx> File.rm(link)\nalx> case File.ln_s(path, link) do\n...>   ok(_) -> File.read(link)\n...>   error(_) -> ok(\"y\")\n...> end\nok(\"y\")\u{1E}File.ls\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.ls(string) -> result[list[string],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The names in a directory, sorted.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir_p(\"doc_ls\")\nok({})\nalx> File.write!(\"doc_ls/a.txt\", \"\")\nalx> File.ls(\"doc_ls\")\nok([\"a.txt\"])\u{1E}File.ls!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.ls!(string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The names in a directory, sorted; fails when it cannot be read.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir_p(\"doc_ls_bang\")\nok({})\nalx> File.ls!(\"doc_ls_bang\")\nEnum.take([\"\"], 0)\u{1E}File.lstat\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.lstat(string) -> result[File.Stat,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like File.stat, describing a symbolic link itself rather than what it points to.\u{1F}alx> ok(stat) = File.lstat(System.tmp_dir!())\nalx> stat.type in [:directory, :symlink]\ntrue\u{1E}File.mkdir\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.mkdir(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Creates a directory whose parent exists.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir(\"doc_mkdir\")\nok({})\nalx> File.dir?(\"doc_mkdir\")\ntrue\u{1E}File.mkdir_p\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.mkdir_p(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Creates a directory and any missing parents; succeeds when it already exists.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir_p(\"doc_mkdir_p/a/b\")\nok({})\u{1E}File.open\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.open(string, list[atom]) -> result[file,string]\u{1D}File.open(string, list[atom], fn[file,a]) -> result[a,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Opens a file with modes: :read (the default), :write (from empty), :append, :exclusive (only a new file), with :utf8 and :binary accepted. The file belongs to the process that opened it and closes when it ends. Given a function, the file is passed to it and closed afterwards, and the result is the function's.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"file_doc.txt\")\nalx> ok(f) = File.open(path, [:write])\nalx> IO.puts(f, \"line\")\nok({})\nalx> File.close(f)\ntrue\nalx> File.open(path, [:read], fn file -> IO.read(file, :line) end)\nok(some(\"line\\n\"))\nalx> case File.open(Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"no/such/dir/x\"), [:read]) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"opened\"\n...>   error(reason) -> reason\n...> end\n\"enoent\"\u{1E}File.read\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.read(string) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads a whole file of text, returning ok(contents) or error(reason); a file whose bytes are not UTF-8 is error(\"file is not valid UTF-8\"), and File.read_binary reads it.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_read.txt\", \"hello\")\nalx> File.read(\"doc_read.txt\")\nok(\"hello\")\nalx> case File.read(\"no_such_file.txt\") do\n...>   ok(text) -> text\n...>   error(_) -> \"missing\"\n...> end\n\"missing\"\u{1E}File.read!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.read!(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads a whole file; fails when it cannot be read.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_read_bang.txt\", \"hello\")\nalx> File.read!(\"doc_read_bang.txt\")\n\"hello\"\u{1E}File.read_binary\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.read_binary(string) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads a whole file as the bytes it holds, whether or not they are text: an image, an archive or an upload. File.read is for text and refuses bytes that are not UTF-8.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_read_binary.bin\", \"a\\x00\\xff\")\nalx> File.read_binary(\"doc_read_binary.bin\")\nok(\"a\\x00\\xff\")\nalx> File.read(\"doc_read_binary.bin\")\nerror(\"file is not valid UTF-8\")\u{1E}File.read_binary!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.read_binary!(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads a whole file as the bytes it holds; fails when it cannot be read.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_read_binary_bang.bin\", \"\\xfe\\xff\")\nalx> byte_size(File.read_binary!(\"doc_read_binary_bang.bin\"))\n2\u{1E}File.regular?\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.regular?(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the path is an ordinary file.\u{1F}alx> File.regular?(\".\")\nfalse\u{1E}File.remove\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.remove(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Deletes a file. Same as File.rm.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_remove.txt\", \"\")\nalx> File.remove(\"doc_remove.txt\")\nok({})\u{1E}File.rename\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.rename(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Renames or moves a file or directory.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_rename.txt\", \"x\")\nalx> File.rename(\"doc_rename.txt\", \"doc_renamed.txt\")\nok({})\nalx> File.exists?(\"doc_rename.txt\")\nfalse\u{1E}File.rm\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.rm(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Deletes a file.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_rm.txt\", \"\")\nalx> File.rm(\"doc_rm.txt\")\nok({})\nalx> File.exists?(\"doc_rm.txt\")\nfalse\u{1E}File.rm!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.rm!(string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Deletes a file; fails when it cannot be deleted.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_rm_bang.txt\", \"\")\nalx> File.rm!(\"doc_rm_bang.txt\")\u{1E}File.rm_rf\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.rm_rf(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Deletes a file or a directory with everything in it; succeeds when the path does not exist.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir_p(\"doc_rm_rf/a\")\nok({})\nalx> File.rm_rf(\"doc_rm_rf\")\nok({})\u{1E}File.rmdir\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.rmdir(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Deletes an empty directory.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir(\"doc_rmdir\")\nok({})\nalx> File.rmdir(\"doc_rmdir\")\nok({})\u{1E}File.stat\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.stat(string) -> result[File.Stat,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}What is known about a file: its size in bytes, type (:regular, :directory, :symlink or :other), the access this program has (:read, :write, :read_write or :none), when it last changed in seconds since 1970, and its permission bits.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"file_doc.txt\")\nalx> File.write(path, \"12345\")\nok({})\nalx> ok(stat) = File.stat(path)\nalx> {stat.size, stat.type}\n{5, :regular}\u{1E}File.stream!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.stream!(string) -> stream[string]\u{1D}File.stream!(string, integer) -> stream[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}A lazy stream of a file's lines, each keeping its line ending; with a size, of chunks of that many bytes. The file is read as the stream is consumed and closed when it ends.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_stream.txt\", \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\")\nalx> File.stream!(\"doc_stream.txt\") |> Stream.map(&String.trim(&1)) |> Enum.take(2)\n[\"one\", \"two\"]\nalx> File.stream!(\"doc_stream.txt\", 4) |> Enum.take(1)\n[\"one\\n\"]\u{1E}File.touch\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.touch(string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Creates an empty file, or updates the modification time of an existing one.\u{1F}alx> File.touch(\"doc_touch.txt\")\nok({})\nalx> File.exists?(\"doc_touch.txt\")\ntrue\u{1E}File.write\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.write(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1D}File.write(string, string, list[atom]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes text, or any bytes, to a file, replacing its contents.\u{1F}alx> File.write(\"doc_write.txt\", \"hello\")\nok({})\u{1E}File.write!\u{1F}File\u{1F}File.write!(string, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes text to a file; fails when it cannot be written.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_write_bang.txt\", \"hello\")\nalx> File.read!(\"doc_write_bang.txt\")\n\"hello\"\u{1E}Float.ceil\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.ceil(float) -> float\u{1D}Float.ceil(float, integer) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}Rounds up, optionally keeping a number of decimal places. Rounding works on the exact value of the float, so 3.7, which is stored as slightly more than 3.7, rounds up to 3.8.\u{1F}alx> Float.ceil(2.11, 1)\n2.2\nalx> Float.ceil(2.1)\n3.0\nalx> Float.ceil(3.7, 1)\n3.8\nalx> Float.ceil(12.5, 1)\n12.5\u{1E}Float.floor\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.floor(float) -> float\u{1D}Float.floor(float, integer) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}Rounds down, optionally keeping a number of decimal places, working on the exact value of the float: 0.3 is stored as slightly less than 0.3.\u{1F}alx> Float.floor(2.19, 1)\n2.1\nalx> Float.floor(0.3, 1)\n0.2\u{1E}Float.from_integer\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.from_integer(integer) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}Converts an integer to a float.\u{1F}alx> Float.from_integer(3)\n3.0\u{1E}Float.max_finite\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.max_finite() -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}The largest finite float.\u{1F}alx> Float.max_finite() > 1.0e308\ntrue\u{1E}Float.min_finite\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.min_finite() -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}The most negative finite float.\u{1F}alx> Float.min_finite() < -1.0e308\ntrue\u{1E}Float.parse\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.parse(string) -> result[float,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses a float, returning ok(value) or error(reason).\u{1F}alx> Float.parse(\"2.5\")\nok(2.5)\u{1E}Float.pow\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.pow(float, float) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}Raises a float to a power.\u{1F}alx> Float.pow(2.0, 3.0)\n8.0\u{1E}Float.ratio\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.ratio(float) -> tuple[integer,integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The float as an exact fraction, {numerator, denominator}.\u{1F}alx> Float.ratio(0.75)\n{3, 4}\u{1E}Float.round\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.round(float) -> float\u{1D}Float.round(float, integer) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}Rounds to the nearest value, optionally keeping a number of decimal places; halves round away from zero. It works on the exact value of the float: 1.15 is stored as slightly less than 1.15.\u{1F}alx> Float.round(3.14159, 2)\n3.14\nalx> Float.round(2.5)\n3.0\nalx> Float.round(1.15, 1)\n1.1\u{1E}Float.to_string\u{1F}Float\u{1F}Float.to_string(float) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The float as the shortest text that reads back as the same value, in exponent form when that is shorter or the value is at least 2**53, as in Elixir. Interpolation and to_string do the same; inspect keeps plain digits up to 1.0e16.\u{1F}alx> Float.to_string(2.5)\n\"2.5\"\nalx> Float.to_string(1000.0)\n\"1.0e3\"\nalx> Float.to_string(1250.0)\n\"1250.0\"\nalx> inspect(1000.0)\n\"1000.0\"\u{1E}Function.identity\u{1F}Function\u{1F}Function.identity(a) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns its argument.\u{1F}alx> Enum.map([1, 2], &Function.identity(&1))\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Gen.bind\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.bind(generator[a], fn[a,generator[b]]) -> generator[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}A generator chosen from each value of another: the second may depend on the first. check all does this for each generator after the first.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.bind(Gen.integer(0, 3), fn n -> Gen.list_of(Gen.constant(n), n, n) end), 10), fn l -> Enum.all?(l, fn n -> n == length(l) end) end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.boolean\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.boolean() -> generator[boolean]\u{1F}\u{1F}true or false, shrinking to false.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.boolean(), 10), fn b -> b or not b end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.check\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.check(generator[a], fn[a,b])\u{1D}Gen.check(generator[a], fn[a,b], integer)\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function on generated values, 100 or the given number of times, and fails with the smallest failing value, shrunk, when it fails. check all is the usual way to write it.\u{1F}alx> Gen.check(Gen.integer(), fn n -> assert(n + 0 == n) end, 20)\u{1E}Gen.constant\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.constant(a) -> generator[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Always the same value.\u{1F}alx> Gen.sample(Gen.constant(7), 3)\n[7, 7, 7]\u{1E}Gen.filter\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.filter(generator[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> generator[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The generator's values that pass the test; generating 100 in a row that fail it is an error, so filter out few.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.filter(Gen.integer(), fn n -> n != 0 end), 10), fn n -> n != 0 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.float\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.float() -> generator[float]\u{1F}\u{1F}Floats between -size and size, shrinking toward 0.\u{1F}alx> length(Gen.sample(Gen.float(), 5))\n5\u{1E}Gen.integer\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.integer() -> generator[integer]\u{1D}Gen.integer(integer, integer) -> generator[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}Integers: without bounds, between -size and size as the runs grow; with bounds, from low to high. Shrinks toward 0, or the bound nearest it.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.integer(1, 6), 20), fn n -> n >= 1 and n <= 6 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.list_of\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.list_of(generator[a]) -> generator[list[a]]\u{1D}Gen.list_of(generator[a], integer, integer) -> generator[list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lists of the generator's values, up to size long or from min to max long. Shrinks by leaving elements out, then by shrinking them.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.list_of(Gen.boolean(), 2, 3), 10), fn l -> length(l) >= 2 and length(l) <= 3 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.map\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.map(generator[a], fn[a,b]) -> generator[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}The generator's values changed by the function.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.map(Gen.integer(), fn n -> n * 2 end), 10), fn n -> rem(n, 2) == 0 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.map_of\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.map_of(generator[k], generator[v]) -> generator[map[k,v]]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Maps with generated keys and values.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.map_of(Gen.integer(1, 3), Gen.boolean()), 10), fn m -> map_size(m) <= 3 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.member_of\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.member_of(enum[a]) -> generator[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}One of the given values, shrinking toward the first.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.member_of([:red, :green]), 10), fn c -> c in [:red, :green] end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.one_of\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.one_of(list[generator[a]]) -> generator[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}A value of one of the generators, chosen at random.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.one_of([Gen.constant(0), Gen.integer(10, 20)]), 10), fn n -> n == 0 or n >= 10 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.positive_integer\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.positive_integer() -> generator[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}Integers from 1, shrinking toward 1.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.positive_integer(), 20), fn n -> n >= 1 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.resize\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.resize(generator[a], integer) -> generator[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The generator with its size fixed: Gen.resize(Gen.integer(), 3) stays between -3 and 3.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.resize(Gen.integer(), 3), 20), fn n -> n >= -3 and n <= 3 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.sample\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.sample(generator[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}That many values of a generator, growing in size, to see what it makes.\u{1F}alx> length(Gen.sample(Gen.integer(), 4))\n4\u{1E}Gen.string\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.string(atom) -> generator[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Strings of :alphanumeric, :alpha, :lower, :digit, :ascii or :printable characters, up to size long, shrinking to shorter and earlier ones.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.string(:digit), 10), fn s -> String.match?(s, ~r/^[0-9]*$/) end)\ntrue\u{1E}Gen.tuple\u{1F}Gen\u{1F}Gen.tuple(generator[a], generator[b]) -> generator[tuple[a,b]]\u{1D}Gen.tuple(generator[a], generator[b], generator[c]) -> generator[tuple[a,b,c]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Tuples of two or three generators' values.\u{1F}alx> Enum.all?(Gen.sample(Gen.tuple(Gen.integer(0, 1), Gen.constant(\"x\")), 5), fn {n, s} -> n <= 1 and s == \"x\" end)\ntrue\u{1E}HTTP.address\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.address(http_server) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The address a server listens on, host:port, with the port chosen when it was 0.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> String.starts_with?(HTTP.address(server), \"127.0.0.1:\")\ntrue\u{1E}HTTP.await\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.await(http_server, integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds (negative waits indefinitely) for a server to stop; a program that only serves ends main with HTTP.await(server, -1).\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> HTTP.await(server, 10)\nfalse\u{1E}HTTP.chunked\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.chunked(integer, list[tuple[string,string]], enum[string]) -> HTTP.Response\u{1F}\u{1F}A response whose body is sent as the enumerable gives it, each piece as soon as it is made: a server can stream a large or slow body without holding it whole.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn _ -> HTTP.chunked(200, [], Stream.map(1..3, fn n -> to_string(n) end)) end)\nalx> ok(response) = HTTP.get(\"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server))\nalx> response.body\n\"123\"\u{1E}HTTP.get\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.get(string) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1D}HTTP.get(string, list[tuple[string,string]]) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a GET request, with optional headers, waiting up to 30 seconds. A response with any status is ok; error is for a request that got no response.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> base = \"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server)\nalx> ok(response) = HTTP.get(base <> \"/a\", [{\"accept\", \"text/plain\"}])\nalx> response.body\n\"GET /a \"\u{1E}HTTP.header\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.header(list[tuple[string,string]], string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The first value of a header, by name in any case.\u{1F}alx> HTTP.header([{\"content-type\", \"text/plain\"}], \"Content-Type\")\nsome(\"text/plain\")\u{1E}HTTP.json\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.json(integer, JSON) -> HTTP.Response\u{1F}\u{1F}A response with a status and a JSON body.\u{1F}alx> HTTP.json(200, JSON.object(%{\"ok\" => JSON.boolean(true)})).body\n\"{\\\"ok\\\":true}\"\u{1E}HTTP.match\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.match(HTTP.Request, string, string) -> option[map[string,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Matches a request against a method (or \"*\") and a path pattern, whose segments may be :name for one segment or *name for the rest: some of the parameters, or none.\u{1F}alx> request = %HTTP.Request{method: \"GET\", path: \"/users/7\", query: \"\", headers: [], body: \"\"}\nalx> HTTP.match(request, \"GET\", \"/users/:id\")\nsome(%{\"id\" => \"7\"})\nalx> HTTP.match(request, \"GET\", \"/posts/:id\")\nnone\u{1E}HTTP.post\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.post(string, string, list[tuple[string,string]]) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a POST request with a body and headers.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> base = \"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server)\nalx> ok(response) = HTTP.post(base <> \"/items\", \"apple\", [{\"content-type\", \"text/plain\"}])\nalx> response.body\n\"POST /items apple\"\u{1E}HTTP.read\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.read(HTTP.Response) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The whole body of a response: its body, followed by its chunks when it has any.\u{1F}alx> HTTP.read(HTTP.chunked(200, [], [\"a\", \"b\"]))\n\"ab\"\nalx> HTTP.read(HTTP.text(200, \"c\"))\n\"c\"\u{1E}HTTP.request\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.request(string, string, list[tuple[string,string]], string) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1D}HTTP.request(string, string, list[tuple[string,string]], string, integer) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1D}HTTP.request(string, string, list[tuple[string,string]], string, list[tuple[atom,string]]) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a request with any method, headers and body, waiting up to 30 seconds or the given milliseconds; error(\"timeout\") when no response came in time. Options may take the place of the milliseconds: timeout: (as text), ca_file: a PEM file of certificates to trust, insecure: \"true\" to trust any, stream: \"true\" for the body as chunks, as HTTP.stream gives it, and redirect: \"false\" to return a redirect as it came, with its status and location header, instead of following it.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> base = \"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server)\nalx> ok(response) = HTTP.request(\"PUT\", base <> \"/x\", [], \"v\")\nalx> response.body\n\"PUT /x v\"\nalx> ok(kept) = HTTP.request(\"GET\", base <> \"/x\", [], \"\", [redirect: \"false\"])\nalx> kept.status\n200\nalx> case HTTP.request(\"GET\", \"http://127.0.0.1:1/\", [], \"\", 1000) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"answered\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"no response\"\n...> end\n\"no response\"\u{1E}HTTP.route\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.route(HTTP.Request, list[tuple[string,string,fn[HTTP.Request,map[string,string],HTTP.Response]]]) -> HTTP.Response\u{1F}\u{1F}Answers a request with the first of a list of {method, pattern, handler} routes that matches, as HTTP.match does; the handler also gets the parameters. A path no route takes is 404, and one only other methods take is 405 with an allow header.\u{1F}alx> show = fn(_: HTTP.Request, params: map[string, string]): HTTP.Response do HTTP.text(200, Map.get(params, \"id\", \"\")) end\nalx> request = %HTTP.Request{method: \"GET\", path: \"/users/7\", query: \"\", headers: [], body: \"\"}\nalx> HTTP.route(request, [{\"GET\", \"/users/:id\", show}]).body\n\"7\"\nalx> HTTP.route(request, [{\"POST\", \"/users/:id\", show}]).status\n405\u{1E}HTTP.serve\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.serve(string, fn[HTTP.Request,HTTP.Response]) -> result[http_server,string]\u{1D}HTTP.serve(string, fn[HTTP.Request,HTTP.Response], list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[http_server,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Serves HTTP on an address, host:port. Each request runs the handler in a process of its own; a handler that fails answers 500. The server stops when HTTP.stop is called or the program ends. A third argument sets limits: read_timeout:, write_timeout:, idle_timeout: (how long a kept-alive connection waits for its next request) and header_timeout: in milliseconds, max_header_bytes:, max_body_bytes: (64 MB by default; larger requests get 413), keep_alive: 0 to close each connection after one request, and compress: 1 to send bodies of 1 KB or more with gzip to clients that accept it, leaving streamed bodies, images and other media, and responses the handler encoded itself as they are.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> base = \"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server)\nalx> ok(response) = HTTP.get(base <> \"/hello\")\nalx> {response.status, response.body}\n{200, \"GET /hello \"}\nalx> HTTP.stop(server)\ntrue\u{1E}HTTP.serve_tls\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.serve_tls(string, fn[HTTP.Request,HTTP.Response], list[tuple[atom,string]]) -> result[http_server,string]\u{1D}HTTP.serve_tls(string, fn[HTTP.Request,HTTP.Response], list[tuple[atom,string]], list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[http_server,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Serves HTTPS with a certificate and key read from PEM files, given as [certfile: path, keyfile: path], and the same limits as HTTP.serve. Clients trust it through the system's authorities, or HTTP.request with [ca_file: path].\u{1F}alx> case HTTP.serve_tls(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn _ -> HTTP.text(200, \"secure\") end, [certfile: \"missing.pem\", keyfile: \"missing.pem\"]) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"serving\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"no certificate\"\n...> end\n\"no certificate\"\u{1E}HTTP.stop\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.stop(http_server) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops a server and closes its connections.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> HTTP.stop(server)\ntrue\nalx> HTTP.await(server, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}HTTP.stream\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.stream(string, string, list[tuple[string,string]], string) -> result[HTTP.Response,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a request and returns once the head of the response has come, with the body as some(stream) in chunks, read as it arrives: for long or endless responses. HTTP.read reads it whole.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   HTTP.text(200, request.method <> \" \" <> request.path <> \" \" <> request.body)\n...> end)\nalx> base = \"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server)\nalx> ok(response) = HTTP.stream(\"GET\", base <> \"/s\", [], \"\")\nalx> HTTP.read(response)\n\"GET /s \"\u{1E}HTTP.take_over\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.take_over(HTTP.Request) -> result[socket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Called by an HTTP.serve handler, gives it the connection of its request as a TCP socket, for a protocol that begins as HTTP and goes on as something else, as a WebSocket does. The server writes nothing more to the connection, the response the handler returns afterwards is not sent, and what the client sent after the request is what the socket reads first. The socket belongs to the handler's process and closes when it ends, unless TCP.controlling_process hands it on.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case HTTP.take_over(request) do\n...>     ok(conn) ->\n...>       TCP.send(conn, \"HTTP/1.1 200 OK\\r\\nContent-Length: 2\\r\\nConnection: close\\r\\n\\r\\nhi\")\n...>       TCP.close(conn)\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(500, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(reply) = HTTP.get(\"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/\")\nalx> reply.body\n\"hi\"\u{1E}HTTP.text\u{1F}HTTP\u{1F}HTTP.text(integer, string) -> HTTP.Response\u{1F}\u{1F}A response with a status and a plain text body.\u{1F}alx> response = HTTP.text(404, \"not found\")\nalx> {response.status, response.body}\n{404, \"not found\"}\u{1E}IO.binread\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.binread(integer) -> option[string]\u{1D}IO.binread(file, integer) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads that many bytes from standard input, or from a file opened with File.open; none at the end.\u{1F}alx> ok(f) = File.open(Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"io_binread_doc.txt\"), [:write])\nalx> IO.write(f, \"bytes\")\nok({})\nalx> File.close(f)\ntrue\nalx> File.open(Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"io_binread_doc.txt\"), [:read], fn file -> IO.binread(file, 3) end)\nok(some(\"byt\"))\u{1E}IO.gets\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.gets(string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a prompt and reads one line from standard input without its line ending; none at end of input.\u{1F}\u{1E}IO.inspect\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.inspect(a) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes any value to standard output in source form and returns it, so it can sit in a pipeline.\u{1F}alx> [1, 2] |> IO.inspect() |> length()\n2\u{1E}IO.puts\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.puts(string)\u{1D}IO.puts(atom, string)\u{1D}IO.puts(file, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a string to standard output, or to the device given first, :stdio or :stderr, followed by a newline. Interpolate other values: IO.puts(\"total: \#{n}\").\u{1F}alx> IO.puts(\"hello\")\nalx> IO.puts(\"total: \#{1 + 2}\")\nalx> IO.puts(:stderr, \"to standard error\")\u{1E}IO.read\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.read(atom) -> option[string]\u{1D}IO.read(integer) -> option[string]\u{1D}IO.read(atom, atom) -> option[string]\u{1D}IO.read(file, atom) -> option[string]\u{1D}IO.read(file, integer) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads standard input: :line gives the next line with its newline, :eof the rest of the input, a number that many characters; none at the end. Given a file opened with File.open first, it reads that file.\u{1F}alx> ok(f) = File.open(Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"io_read_doc.txt\"), [:write])\nalx> IO.write(f, \"one\\ntwo\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> File.close(f)\ntrue\nalx> File.open(Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"io_read_doc.txt\"), [:read], fn file -> {IO.read(file, :line), IO.read(file, 2), IO.read(file, :eof)} end)\nok({some(\"one\\n\"), some(\"tw\"), some(\"o\\n\")})\u{1E}IO.read_all\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.read_all() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads all of standard input.\u{1F}\u{1E}IO.read_line\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.read_line() -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads one line from standard input without its line ending; none at end of input.\u{1F}\u{1E}IO.stream\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.stream() -> stream[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}A lazy stream of the lines of standard input, each keeping its line ending.\u{1F}\u{1E}IO.warn\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.warn(string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a line to standard error.\u{1F}alx> IO.warn(\"careful\")\u{1E}IO.write\u{1F}IO\u{1F}IO.write(string)\u{1D}IO.write(file, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a string to standard output without a newline.\u{1F}alx> IO.write(\"no newline\")\u{1E}IO.ANSI.black\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.black() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for black.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.black(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.black_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.black_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for black background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.black_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.blink_rapid\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.blink_rapid() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for blink rapid.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.blink_rapid(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.blink_slow\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.blink_slow() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for blink slow.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.blink_slow(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.blue\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.blue() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for blue.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.blue(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.blue_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.blue_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for blue background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.blue_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.bright\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.bright() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for bright.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.bright(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.clear\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.clear() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that clears the screen.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.clear()\n\"\\e[2J\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.clear_line\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.clear_line() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that clears the current line.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.clear_line()\n\"\\e[2K\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.color\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.color(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence for one of the 256 terminal colors.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.color(208)\n\"\\e[38;5;208m\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.color_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.color_background(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence for one of the 256 terminal colors as the background.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.color_background(17)\n\"\\e[48;5;17m\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.conceal\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.conceal() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for conceal.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.conceal(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.crossed_out\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.crossed_out() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for crossed out.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.crossed_out(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cursor\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cursor(integer, integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that moves the cursor to a line and column, counted from 1.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.cursor(2, 5)\n\"\\e[2;5H\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cursor_down\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cursor_down(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that moves the cursor down some lines.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.cursor_down(1)\n\"\\e[1B\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cursor_left\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cursor_left(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that moves the cursor left some columns.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.cursor_left(2)\n\"\\e[2D\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cursor_right\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cursor_right(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that moves the cursor right some columns.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.cursor_right(2)\n\"\\e[2C\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cursor_up\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cursor_up(integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that moves the cursor up some lines.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.cursor_up(3)\n\"\\e[3A\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cyan\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cyan() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for cyan.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.cyan(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.cyan_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.cyan_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for cyan background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.cyan_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.default_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.default_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for default background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.default_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.default_color\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.default_color() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for default color.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.default_color(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.enabled?\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.enabled?() -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether standard output is a terminal that shows escape sequences: not when it is redirected, when TERM is dumb or NO_COLOR is set.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.enabled?() or not IO.ANSI.enabled?()\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.faint\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.faint() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for faint.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.faint(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.format\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.format(list[tuple[atom,string]], boolean) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Joins the parts, given as name: text pairs: text: is written as it is, and any other name, such as red: or reset:, puts its escape sequence there, with a reset at the end. With false the sequences are left out, for output that is not a terminal.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.format([red: \"\", text: \"alert\"], false)\n\"alert\"\nalx> IO.ANSI.format([bright: \"\", text: \"x\"], true)\n\"\\e[1mx\\e[0m\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.green\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.green() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for green.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.green(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.green_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.green_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for green background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.green_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.home\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.home() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The sequence that moves the cursor to the top left.\u{1F}alx> IO.ANSI.home()\n\"\\e[H\"\u{1E}IO.ANSI.italic\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.italic() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for italic.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.italic(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_black\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_black() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light black.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_black(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_black_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_black_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light black background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_black_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_blue\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_blue() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light blue.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_blue(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_blue_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_blue_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light blue background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_blue_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_cyan\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_cyan() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light cyan.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_cyan(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_cyan_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_cyan_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light cyan background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_cyan_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_green\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_green() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light green.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_green(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_green_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_green_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light green background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_green_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_magenta\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_magenta() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light magenta.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_magenta(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_magenta_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_magenta_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light magenta background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_magenta_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_red\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_red() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light red.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_red(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_red_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_red_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light red background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_red_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_white\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_white() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light white.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_white(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_white_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_white_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light white background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_white_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_yellow\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_yellow() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light yellow.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_yellow(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.light_yellow_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.light_yellow_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for light yellow background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.light_yellow_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.magenta\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.magenta() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for magenta.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.magenta(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.magenta_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.magenta_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for magenta background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.magenta_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.no_underline\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.no_underline() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for no underline.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.no_underline(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.normal\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.normal() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for normal.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.normal(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.not_italic\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.not_italic() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for not italic.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.not_italic(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.red\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.red() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for red.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.red(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.red_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.red_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for red background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.red_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.reset\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.reset() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for reset.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.reset(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.reverse\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.reverse() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for reverse.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.reverse(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.underline\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.underline() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for underline.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.underline(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.white\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.white() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for white.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.white(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.white_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.white_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for white background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.white_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.yellow\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.yellow() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for yellow.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.yellow(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}IO.ANSI.yellow_background\u{1F}IO.ANSI\u{1F}IO.ANSI.yellow_background() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The escape sequence for yellow background.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(IO.ANSI.yellow_background(), \"\\e[\")\ntrue\u{1E}Integer.ceil_div\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.ceil_div(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Integer division rounding toward positive infinity.\u{1F}alx> Integer.ceil_div(7, 2)\n4\u{1E}Integer.digits\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.digits(integer) -> list[integer]\u{1D}Integer.digits(integer, integer) -> list[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The digits of a non-negative integer, optionally in a base.\u{1F}alx> Integer.digits(123)\n[1, 2, 3]\nalx> Integer.digits(5, 2)\n[1, 0, 1]\u{1E}Integer.extended_gcd\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.extended_gcd(integer, integer) -> tuple[integer,integer,integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The greatest common divisor g with coefficients x and y such that a*x + b*y == g.\u{1F}alx> Integer.extended_gcd(99, 78)\n{3, -11, 14}\u{1E}Integer.floor_div\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.floor_div(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Integer division rounding toward negative infinity.\u{1F}alx> Integer.floor_div(-7, 2)\n-4\u{1E}Integer.gcd\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.gcd(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The greatest common divisor.\u{1F}alx> Integer.gcd(12, 18)\n6\u{1E}Integer.is_even\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.is_even(integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the integer is even.\u{1F}alx> Integer.is_even(4)\ntrue\u{1E}Integer.is_odd\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.is_odd(integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the integer is odd.\u{1F}alx> Integer.is_odd(4)\nfalse\u{1E}Integer.mod\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.mod(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The modulo, with the sign of the divisor.\u{1F}alx> Integer.mod(-7, 3)\n2\u{1E}Integer.parse\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.parse(string) -> result[integer,string]\u{1D}Integer.parse(string, integer) -> result[integer,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses an integer, optionally in a base, returning ok(value) or error(reason).\u{1F}alx> Integer.parse(\"42\")\nok(42)\nalx> Integer.parse(\"ff\", 16)\nok(255)\nalx> case Integer.parse(\"4x\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"number\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"not a number\"\n...> end\n\"not a number\"\u{1E}Integer.pow\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.pow(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Raises an integer to a non-negative power.\u{1F}alx> Integer.pow(2, 10)\n1024\u{1E}Integer.to_string\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.to_string(integer) -> string\u{1D}Integer.to_string(integer, integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The integer as text, optionally in a base.\u{1F}alx> Integer.to_string(255)\n\"255\"\nalx> Integer.to_string(255, 16)\n\"FF\"\u{1E}Integer.undigits\u{1F}Integer\u{1F}Integer.undigits(enum[integer]) -> integer\u{1D}Integer.undigits(enum[integer], integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds an integer from its digits, optionally in a base.\u{1F}alx> Integer.undigits([1, 2, 3])\n123\nalx> Integer.undigits([1, 0, 1], 2)\n5\u{1E}JSON.decode\u{1F}JSON\u{1F}JSON.decode(string) -> result[JSON,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses JSON text into the JSON type, whose constructors are JSON.null, JSON.boolean, JSON.integer, JSON.float, JSON.string, JSON.list and JSON.object.\u{1F}alx> JSON.decode(\"[1, true]\")\nok(JSON.list([JSON.integer(1), JSON.boolean(true)]))\nalx> case JSON.decode(\"{\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"invalid\"\n...> end\n\"invalid\"\u{1E}JSON.decode!\u{1F}JSON\u{1F}JSON.decode!(string) -> JSON\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses JSON text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> JSON.decode!(\"{\\\"a\\\": null}\")\nJSON.object(%{\"a\" => JSON.null})\u{1E}JSON.decode_as\u{1F}JSON\u{1F}JSON.decode_as(JSON) -> result[a,string]\u{1D}JSON.decode_as(string) -> result[a,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads JSON as the type the result is annotated with, from text or from a JSON value: the reverse of what JSON.encode! writes for that type. A struct is read from an object by field name, where a field that is an option may be left out and members the struct has no field for are passed over; a variant from its name, or from {\"name\": [fields]} when it has fields; lists, sets and tuples from arrays; a map from an object, its keys read as strings, atoms, integers, floats or booleans; an option from null or the value; and an integer is accepted where a float is wanted. JSON that does not fit is an error that says where, as in \"items[2].price: expected an integer, found a string\". A type holding functions or processes cannot be read. In a generic function the type may be one of the function's type variables, and is read as the type the function was called with, which must then be one that can be read.\u{1F}alx> prices: result[map[string, list[integer]], string] = JSON.decode_as(\"{\\\"pen\\\": [3, 4]}\")\nalx> prices\nok(%{\"pen\" => [3, 4]})\nalx> pair: result[tuple[string, option[float]], string] = JSON.decode_as(JSON.decode!(\"[\\\"x\\\", null]\"))\nalx> pair\nok({\"x\", none})\nalx> wrong: result[map[string, list[integer]], string] = JSON.decode_as(\"{\\\"pen\\\": [3, \\\"four\\\"]}\")\nalx> wrong\nerror(\"pen[1]: expected an integer, found a string\")\u{1E}JSON.encode\u{1F}JSON\u{1F}JSON.encode(a) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes any value as JSON: ok(text), or error(reason) for a value with no JSON form, such as an infinite float. Structs become objects by field name, maps objects with their keys as text, lists, sets and tuples arrays, atoms strings, none null and some(x) x; a variant without fields becomes its name and one with fields {\"name\": [fields]}. JSON.encode! takes the same values, and [pretty: true] indents the result.\u{1F}alx> JSON.encode(%{\"a\" => [1, 2]})\nok(\"{\\\"a\\\":[1,2]}\")\nalx> JSON.encode!({:ok, some(1.5)})\n\"[\\\"ok\\\",1.5]\"\u{1E}JSON.encode!\u{1F}JSON\u{1F}JSON.encode!(a) -> string\u{1D}JSON.encode!(JSON) -> string\u{1D}JSON.encode!(a, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a JSON value as text, with object keys in ascending order.\u{1F}alx> JSON.encode!(JSON.object(%{\"name\" => JSON.string(\"al\"), \"tags\" => JSON.list([JSON.integer(1)])}))\n\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"al\\\",\\\"tags\\\":[1]}\"\u{1E}abs\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}abs(a) -> a\u{1F}a is an integer or float\u{1F}The absolute value of an integer or float.\u{1F}alx> abs(-3)\n3\nalx> abs(-2.5)\n2.5\u{1E}binary_part\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}binary_part(string, integer, integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The bytes of a string from a position, of a length; a negative length counts back from the position.\u{1F}alx> binary_part(\"hello\", 1, 3)\n\"ell\"\nalx> binary_part(\"hello\", 5, -2)\n\"lo\"\u{1E}byte_size\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}byte_size(string) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of bytes in a string, which exceeds String.length for non-ASCII text.\u{1F}alx> byte_size(\"héllo\")\n6\u{1E}ceil\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}ceil(float) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Rounds a float up to an integer.\u{1F}alx> ceil(2.1)\n3\u{1E}concat\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}concat(list[a], list[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Joins two lists. The ++ operator does the same.\u{1F}alx> concat([1], [2, 3])\n[1, 2, 3]\nalx> [1] ++ [2, 3]\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}div\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}div(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Integer division, truncating toward zero. Fails on division by zero.\u{1F}alx> div(7, 2)\n3\nalx> div(-7, 2)\n-3\u{1E}filter\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}filter(list[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Keeps the list elements for which the function returns true. See also Enum.filter.\u{1F}alx> filter([1, 2, 3], fn(n: integer): boolean do n > 1 end)\n[2, 3]\u{1E}floor\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}floor(float) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Rounds a float down to an integer.\u{1F}alx> floor(2.9)\n2\u{1E}get\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}get(map[k,v], k, v) -> v\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the value for the key, or the default when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> get(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"z\", 0)\n0\u{1E}has_key\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}has_key(map[k,v], k) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the map has the key. Map.has_key? does the same.\u{1F}alx> has_key(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")\ntrue\u{1E}hd\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}hd(list[a]) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}The first element of a list; fails on an empty list.\u{1F}alx> hd([1, 2, 3])\n1\u{1E}inspect\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}inspect(a) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Any value as text in source form, for diagnostics.\u{1F}alx> inspect([some(1), none])\n\"[some(1), none]\"\nalx> inspect({\"a\", 1.5})\n\"{\\\"a\\\", 1.5}\"\u{1E}length\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}length(tuple) -> integer\u{1D}length(list[a]) -> integer\u{1D}length(map[k,v]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of elements of a list, entries of a map, or positions of a tuple.\u{1F}alx> length([1, 2, 3])\n3\nalx> length(%{\"a\" => 1})\n1\u{1E}map\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}map(list[a], fn[a,b]) -> list[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Applies a named or annotated function to each element of a list. Enum.map also accepts inferred functions and any enumerable.\u{1F}alx> map([1, 2, 3], fn(n: integer): integer do n * 2 end)\n[2, 4, 6]\u{1E}map_size\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}map_size(map[k,v]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of entries in a map.\u{1F}alx> map_size(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2})\n2\u{1E}max\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}max(a, a) -> a\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}The greater of two integers, floats or strings.\u{1F}alx> max(3, 9)\n9\u{1E}min\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}min(a, a) -> a\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}The lesser of two integers, floats or strings.\u{1F}alx> min(3, 9)\n3\u{1E}prepend\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}prepend(list[a], a) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the list with the value added at the front, in constant time.\u{1F}alx> prepend([2, 3], 1)\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}put\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}put(map[k,v], k, v) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the map with the key set to the value. Map.put does the same.\u{1F}alx> put(%{\"x\" => 1}, \"y\", 2)\n%{\"x\" => 1, \"y\" => 2}\u{1E}raise\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}raise(string)\u{1D}raise(Exception)\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails the current process with a message, or with an exception declared by defexception, whose message field is the message. try/rescue can catch it: e in Name binds the exception.\u{1F}alx> try do raise(\"boom\") rescue message -> message end\n\"boom\"\u{1E}reduce\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}reduce(list[a], b, fn[b,a,b]) -> b\u{1F}\u{1F}Folds a list; the function takes the accumulator first, then the element. Enum.reduce takes them the other way round.\u{1F}alx> reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, fn(acc: integer, n: integer): integer do acc + n end)\n6\u{1E}rem\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}rem(integer, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The remainder of integer division, with the sign of the dividend.\u{1F}alx> rem(7, 2)\n1\nalx> rem(-7, 2)\n-1\u{1E}round\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}round(float) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Rounds a float to the nearest integer, halves away from zero.\u{1F}alx> round(2.5)\n3\u{1E}tap\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}tap(a, fn[a,b]) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}Calls the function with the value for its effect and returns the value. Useful in a pipeline.\u{1F}alx> tap(5, fn n -> IO.write(Integer.to_string(n)) end)\n5\u{1E}then\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}then(a, fn[a,b]) -> b\u{1F}\u{1F}Passes the value to the function and returns its result. Useful in a pipeline.\u{1F}alx> 5 |> then(fn n -> n * 2 end)\n10\u{1E}throw\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}throw(a)\u{1F}\u{1F}Unwinds to the nearest enclosing try with a catch arm for the value's type, leaving loops and calls on the way. Uncaught, it fails the process with \"uncaught throw\".\u{1F}alx> try do\n...>   Enum.each([1, 4, 9], fn n -> if n > 3 do throw(n) end end)\n...>   0\n...> catch\n...>   n -> n\n...> end\n4\u{1E}tl\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}tl(list[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The list without its first element; fails on an empty list.\u{1F}alx> tl([1, 2, 3])\n[2, 3]\u{1E}to_string\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}to_string(a) -> string\u{1F}a is a string, integer, float or boolean\u{1F}A string, integer, float or boolean as text. Interpolation \"\#{value}\" does the same.\u{1F}alx> to_string(42)\n\"42\"\nalx> to_string(true)\n\"true\"\u{1E}trunc\u{1F}Kernel\u{1F}trunc(float) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops the fractional part of a float.\u{1F}alx> trunc(-2.9)\n-2\u{1E}Keyword.delete\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.delete(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes every pair with the key.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.delete([x: 1, y: 2, x: 3], :x)\n[y: 2]\u{1E}Keyword.drop\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.drop(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[atom]) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes the pairs whose key is in the list.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.drop([x: 1, y: 2, z: 3], [:x, :z])\n[y: 2]\u{1E}Keyword.fetch\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.fetch(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> option[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}The value of the first pair with the key, as an option.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.fetch([a: 1], :a)\nsome(1)\u{1E}Keyword.fetch!\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.fetch!(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> v\u{1F}\u{1F}The value of the first pair with the key; fails when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.fetch!([a: 1], :a)\n1\u{1E}Keyword.get\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.get(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, v) -> v\u{1D}Keyword.get(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> option[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}The value of the first pair with the key, as an option or with a default.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.get([a: 1, b: 2], :b)\nsome(2)\nalx> Keyword.get([a: 1], :z, 0)\n0\u{1E}Keyword.get_lazy\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.get_lazy(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, fn[v]) -> v\u{1F}\u{1F}The value of a key, or what the function gives when it is missing; the function runs only then.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.get_lazy([a: 1], :b, fn -> 2 end)\n2\u{1E}Keyword.get_values\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.get_values(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> list[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Every value stored under the key, in order.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.get_values([x: 1, y: 2, x: 3], :x)\n[1, 3]\u{1E}Keyword.has_key?\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.has_key?(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether a pair has the key.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.has_key?([a: 1], :a)\ntrue\u{1E}Keyword.keys\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.keys(list[tuple[atom,v]]) -> list[atom]\u{1F}\u{1F}The keys in order, repeats included.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.keys([x: 1, y: 2])\n[:x, :y]\u{1E}Keyword.merge\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.merge(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[tuple[atom,v]]) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1D}Keyword.merge(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[tuple[atom,v]], fn[atom,v,v,v]) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The first list without the keys the second has, followed by the second.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.merge([a: 1, b: 2], [b: 9, c: 3])\n[a: 1, b: 9, c: 3]\u{1E}Keyword.pop\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.pop(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom) -> tuple[option[v],list[tuple[atom,v]]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The first value under the key, as an option, with the list without that key.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.pop([a: 1, b: 2], :a)\n{some(1), [b: 2]}\u{1E}Keyword.pop_lazy\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.pop_lazy(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, fn[v]) -> tuple[v,list[tuple[atom,v]]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes a key and returns its value, or what the function gives when it is missing, with the rest of the list.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.pop_lazy([a: 1, b: 2], :a, fn -> 0 end)\n{1, [b: 2]}\u{1E}Keyword.put\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.put(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, v) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes every pair with the key and puts the new pair first.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.put([x: 1, y: 2, x: 3], :x, 9)\n[x: 9, y: 2]\u{1E}Keyword.put_new\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.put_new(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, v) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds the pair only when the key is absent.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.put_new([x: 1], :x, 9)\n[x: 1]\nalx> Keyword.put_new([x: 1], :y, 2)\n[y: 2, x: 1]\u{1E}Keyword.put_new_lazy\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.put_new_lazy(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, fn[v]) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a key with what the function gives, only when the key is missing.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.put_new_lazy([a: 1], :b, fn -> 2 end)\n[b: 2, a: 1]\u{1E}Keyword.split\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.split(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[atom]) -> tuple[list[tuple[atom,v]],list[tuple[atom,v]]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Separates the pairs with the given keys from the others.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.split([x: 1, y: 2, z: 3], [:x, :z])\n{[x: 1, z: 3], [y: 2]}\u{1E}Keyword.take\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.take(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[atom]) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Keeps the pairs whose key is in the list.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.take([x: 1, y: 2, z: 3], [:x, :z])\n[x: 1, z: 3]\u{1E}Keyword.to_map\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.to_map(list[tuple[atom,v]]) -> map[atom,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}A map from each key to its first value.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.to_map([x: 1, y: 2, x: 3])\n%{:x => 1, :y => 2}\u{1E}Keyword.update\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.update(list[tuple[atom,v]], atom, v, fn[v,v]) -> list[tuple[atom,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Applies the function to the first value under the key, or adds the default at the end when the key is absent.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.update([a: 1], :a, 0, &(&1 + 1))\n[a: 2]\nalx> Keyword.update([a: 1], :b, 0, &(&1 + 1))\n[a: 1, b: 0]\u{1E}Keyword.validate\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.validate(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[atom]) -> result[list[tuple[atom,v]],list[atom]]\u{1D}Keyword.validate(list[tuple[atom,v]], list[tuple[atom,v]]) -> result[list[tuple[atom,v]],list[atom]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Checks that a keyword list has only the allowed keys: ok(list), with the defaults of the allowed keys it lacks, or error(the unknown keys). The allowed keys are given as a keyword list of defaults, or as a list of names.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.validate([a: 1], [a: 0, b: 2])\nok([a: 1, b: 2])\nalx> Keyword.validate([c: 1], [:a, :b])\nerror([:c])\u{1E}Keyword.values\u{1F}Keyword\u{1F}Keyword.values(list[tuple[atom,v]]) -> list[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}The values in order.\u{1F}alx> Keyword.values([x: 4, y: 5])\n[4, 5]\u{1E}List.contains\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.contains(list[a], a) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the list contains the value.\u{1F}alx> List.contains([1, 2, 3], 2)\ntrue\u{1E}List.delete\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.delete(enum[a], a) -> list[a]\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Removes the first occurrence of a value.\u{1F}alx> List.delete([1, 2, 1], 1)\n[2, 1]\u{1E}List.delete_at\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.delete_at(enum[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes the element at an index; negative indexes count from the end.\u{1F}alx> List.delete_at([1, 2, 3], -1)\n[1, 2]\u{1E}List.drop\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.drop(list[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops the first count elements, sharing storage with the original.\u{1F}alx> List.drop([1, 2, 3], 2)\n[3]\u{1E}List.duplicate\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.duplicate(a, integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}A list of the value repeated count times.\u{1F}alx> List.duplicate(\"a\", 3)\n[\"a\", \"a\", \"a\"]\u{1E}List.ends_with?\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.ends_with?(enum[a], enum[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Reports whether the list ends with the given elements.\u{1F}alx> List.ends_with?([1, 2, 3], [2, 3])\ntrue\u{1E}List.first\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.first(list[a], a) -> a\u{1D}List.first(list[a]) -> option[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The first element as an option, or the default for an empty list.\u{1F}alx> List.first([1, 2])\nsome(1)\nalx> List.first(Enum.take([1], 0), 0)\n0\u{1E}List.first!\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.first!(list[a]) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}The first element; fails on an empty list.\u{1F}alx> List.first!([1, 2])\n1\u{1E}List.flatten\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.flatten(enum[list[a]]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Concatenates a list of lists, one level deep.\u{1F}alx> List.flatten([[1], [2, 3]])\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}List.foldl\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.foldl(enum[a], b, fn[a,b,b]) -> b\u{1F}\u{1F}Folds from the left; the function takes the element, then the accumulator.\u{1F}alx> List.foldl([1, 2, 3], \"\", fn n, acc -> acc <> Integer.to_string(n) end)\n\"123\"\u{1E}List.foldr\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.foldr(enum[a], b, fn[a,b,b]) -> b\u{1F}\u{1F}Folds from the right.\u{1F}alx> List.foldr([1, 2, 3], \"\", fn n, acc -> acc <> Integer.to_string(n) end)\n\"321\"\u{1E}List.insert_at\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.insert_at(enum[a], integer, a) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Inserts a value at an index; -1 appends.\u{1F}alx> List.insert_at([1, 3], 1, 2)\n[1, 2, 3]\nalx> List.insert_at([1, 2], -1, 3)\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}List.keydelete\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keydelete(list[tuple[k,v]], k) -> list[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}k supports equality\u{1F}The list without the first pair that has the key.\u{1F}alx> List.keydelete([{\"red\", 4}, {\"blue\", 5}, {\"red\", 6}], \"red\")\n[{\"blue\", 5}, {\"red\", 6}]\u{1E}List.keyfind\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keyfind(list[tuple[k,v]], k) -> option[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}k supports equality\u{1F}The first pair whose key, its first element, equals the given one. The List.key functions treat a list of pairs as a table keyed on the first element, as Elixir's do at position 0.\u{1F}alx> List.keyfind([{\"a\", 1}, {\"b\", 2}], \"b\")\nsome({\"b\", 2})\nalx> List.keyfind([{\"a\", 1}], \"z\")\nnone\u{1E}List.keymember?\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keymember?(list[tuple[k,v]], k) -> boolean\u{1F}k supports equality\u{1F}Reports whether a pair has the key.\u{1F}alx> List.keymember?([{:x, 1}], :x)\ntrue\u{1E}List.keyreplace\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keyreplace(list[tuple[k,v]], k, tuple[k,v]) -> list[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}k supports equality\u{1F}The list with the first pair that has the key replaced; unchanged when no pair has it.\u{1F}alx> List.keyreplace([{\"a\", 1}, {\"b\", 2}], \"b\", {\"b\", 20})\n[{\"a\", 1}, {\"b\", 20}]\u{1E}List.keysort\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keysort(list[tuple[k,v]]) -> list[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}k has an order\u{1F}The pairs sorted by key; pairs with equal keys keep their order.\u{1F}alx> List.keysort([{3, \"c\"}, {1, \"a\"}, {3, \"b\"}])\n[{1, \"a\"}, {3, \"c\"}, {3, \"b\"}]\u{1E}List.keystore\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keystore(list[tuple[k,v]], k, tuple[k,v]) -> list[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}k supports equality\u{1F}As keyreplace, but adds the pair at the end when no pair has the key.\u{1F}alx> List.keystore([{\"a\", 1}], \"c\", {\"c\", 3})\n[{\"a\", 1}, {\"c\", 3}]\u{1E}List.keytake\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.keytake(list[tuple[k,v]], k) -> option[tuple[tuple[k,v],list[tuple[k,v]]]]\u{1F}k supports equality\u{1F}The first pair with the key and the list without it, or none.\u{1F}alx> List.keytake([{\"a\", 1}, {\"b\", 2}], \"a\")\nsome({{\"a\", 1}, [{\"b\", 2}]})\u{1E}List.last\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.last(list[a], a) -> a\u{1D}List.last(list[a]) -> option[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The last element as an option, or the default for an empty list.\u{1F}alx> List.last([1, 2])\nsome(2)\u{1E}List.last!\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.last!(list[a]) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}The last element; fails on an empty list.\u{1F}alx> List.last!([1, 2])\n2\u{1E}List.myers_difference\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.myers_difference(list[a], list[a]) -> list[tuple[atom,list[a]]]\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}The edits that turn one list into another, as runs of {:eq | :del | :ins, elements}.\u{1F}alx> List.myers_difference([1, 2, 3, 4], [1, 4, 5])\n[eq: [1], del: [2, 3], eq: [4], ins: [5]]\u{1E}List.pop_at\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.pop_at(enum[a], integer) -> tuple[option[a],list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes the element at an index, returning it as an option with the remaining list.\u{1F}alx> List.pop_at([1, 2, 3], 1)\n{some(2), [1, 3]}\u{1E}List.range\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.range(integer, integer) -> list[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The integers from first to last inclusive, as a list; empty when last is below first.\u{1F}alx> List.range(1, 4)\n[1, 2, 3, 4]\u{1E}List.replace_at\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.replace_at(enum[a], integer, a) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces the element at an index.\u{1F}alx> List.replace_at([4, 5, 6], 1, 0)\n[4, 0, 6]\u{1E}List.reverse\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.reverse(list[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reverses a list.\u{1F}alx> List.reverse([1, 2, 3])\n[3, 2, 1]\u{1E}List.sort\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.sort(list[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sorts a list of integers, floats or strings ascending.\u{1F}alx> List.sort([3, 1, 2])\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}List.starts_with?\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.starts_with?(enum[a], enum[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Reports whether the list starts with the given elements.\u{1F}alx> List.starts_with?([1, 2, 3], [1, 2])\ntrue\u{1E}List.take\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.take(list[a], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes the first count elements, sharing storage with the original.\u{1F}alx> List.take([1, 2, 3], 2)\n[1, 2]\u{1E}List.update_at\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.update_at(enum[a], integer, fn[a,a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Applies the function to the element at an index.\u{1F}alx> List.update_at([4, 5, 6], 0, &(&1 * 10))\n[40, 5, 6]\u{1E}List.zip\u{1F}List\u{1F}List.zip(enum[list[a]]) -> list[list[a]]\u{1D}List.zip(list[a], list[b]) -> list[tuple[a,b]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Pairs the elements of two lists.\u{1F}alx> List.zip([1, 2], [\"a\", \"b\"])\n[{1, \"a\"}, {2, \"b\"}]\u{1E}Logger.add_handler\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.add_handler(atom, fn[atom,string,list[tuple[atom,string]],b]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends every message that passes the level, with its level and metadata, to a function as well as to the console; false when the name is taken. :console names the console itself, which remove_handler can turn off.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Logger.add_handler(:doc_handler, fn level, text, _ -> Process.send(me, to_string(level) <> \" \" <> text, 100) end)\ntrue\nalx> Logger.error(\"handled\")\nalx> Process.receive(me, 1000)\n{true, \"error handled\"}\nalx> Logger.remove_handler(:doc_handler)\ntrue\u{1E}Logger.configure\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.configure(string)\u{1D}Logger.configure(list[tuple[atom,string]])\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets the lowest level that is written: debug, info, warning, error or none. It starts at debug, or at what the ALCHEM_LOG_LEVEL environment variable names.\u{1F}alx> Logger.configure(\"warning\")\nalx> Logger.level()\n\"warning\"\nalx> Logger.configure(\"debug\")\u{1E}Logger.debug\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.debug(string)\u{1D}Logger.debug(fn[string])\u{1D}Logger.debug(string, list[tuple[atom,string]])\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a debug message to standard error, when the level allows it.\u{1F}alx> Logger.debug(\"cache miss\")\u{1E}Logger.error\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.error(string)\u{1D}Logger.error(fn[string])\u{1D}Logger.error(string, list[tuple[atom,string]])\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes an error message to standard error.\u{1F}alx> Logger.error(\"connection lost\")\u{1E}Logger.info\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.info(string)\u{1D}Logger.info(fn[string])\u{1D}Logger.info(string, list[tuple[atom,string]])\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes an informational message to standard error as time, level and text.\u{1F}alx> Logger.info(\"listening on 8080\")\u{1E}Logger.level\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.level() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The lowest level that is currently written.\u{1F}alx> Logger.configure(\"info\")\nalx> Logger.level()\n\"info\"\nalx> Logger.configure(\"debug\")\u{1E}Logger.log\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.log(atom, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Logs a message at a level given as :debug, :info, :warning or :error.\u{1F}alx> Logger.log(:debug, \"from the docs\")\u{1E}Logger.metadata\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.metadata(list[tuple[atom,string]])\u{1D}Logger.metadata() -> list[tuple[atom,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds name: value pairs to the calling process's metadata, which goes with every message it logs; without arguments, the metadata so far.\u{1F}alx> Logger.metadata([request_id: \"r1\"])\nalx> Logger.metadata()\n[request_id: \"r1\"]\u{1E}Logger.remove_handler\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.remove_handler(atom) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops sending messages to a handler; false when there is none by that name.\u{1F}alx> Logger.remove_handler(:never_added)\nfalse\u{1E}Logger.warning\u{1F}Logger\u{1F}Logger.warning(string)\u{1D}Logger.warning(fn[string])\u{1D}Logger.warning(string, list[tuple[atom,string]])\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a warning to standard error.\u{1F}alx> Logger.warning(\"retrying\")\u{1E}Map.delete\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.delete(map[k,v], k) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the map without the key.\u{1F}alx> Map.delete(%{\"x\" => 1, \"y\" => 2}, \"x\")\n%{\"y\" => 2}\u{1E}Map.drop\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.drop(map[k,v], enum[k]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the map without the given keys.\u{1F}alx> Map.drop(%{\"x\" => 1, \"y\" => 2}, [\"x\"])\n%{\"y\" => 2}\u{1E}Map.entries\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.entries(map[k,v]) -> list[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The {key, value} pairs in ascending key order. Map.to_list does the same.\u{1F}alx> Map.entries(%{\"a\" => 1})\n[{\"a\", 1}]\u{1E}Map.equal?\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.equal?(map[k,v], map[k,v]) -> boolean\u{1F}v supports equality\u{1F}Reports whether two maps hold the same entries.\u{1F}alx> Map.equal?(%{\"a\" => 1}, %{\"a\" => 1})\ntrue\u{1E}Map.fetch\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.fetch(map[k,v], k) -> option[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}The value for a key as an option.\u{1F}alx> Map.fetch(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")\nsome(1)\u{1E}Map.fetch!\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.fetch!(map[k,v], k) -> v\u{1F}\u{1F}The value for a key; fails when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.fetch!(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")\n1\u{1E}Map.filter\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.filter(map[k,v], fn[tuple[k,v],boolean]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}k has an order\u{1F}Keeps the entries for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Map.filter(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 9}, fn {_, value} -> value > 5 end)\n%{\"b\" => 9}\u{1E}Map.from_keys\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.from_keys(enum[k], v) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}A map with every given key set to one value.\u{1F}alx> Map.from_keys([\"a\", \"b\"], 0)\n%{\"a\" => 0, \"b\" => 0}\u{1E}Map.get\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.get(map[k,v], k, v) -> v\u{1D}Map.get(map[k,v], k) -> option[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}The value for a key as an option, or the default when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.get(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")\nsome(1)\nalx> Map.get(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"z\", 0)\n0\u{1E}Map.get_and_update\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.get_and_update(map[k,v], k, fn[option[v],tuple[r,option[v]]]) -> tuple[r,map[k,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Passes the value under a key, or none, to the function, which returns what to give back and the new value, or none to remove the key; the result is that and the updated map.\u{1F}alx> Map.get_and_update(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", fn current -> {current, some(5)} end)\n{some(1), %{\"a\" => 5}}\u{1E}Map.get_lazy\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.get_lazy(map[k,v], k, fn[v]) -> v\u{1F}\u{1F}The value for a key, calling the function only when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.get_lazy(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"z\", fn -> 99 end)\n99\u{1E}Map.has_key?\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.has_key?(map[k,v], k) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the map has the key.\u{1F}alx> Map.has_key?(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")\ntrue\u{1E}Map.intersect\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.intersect(map[k,v], map[k,v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Keeps the keys both maps have, with the second map's values.\u{1F}alx> Map.intersect(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2}, %{\"b\" => 0})\n%{\"b\" => 0}\u{1E}Map.keys\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.keys(map[k,v]) -> list[k]\u{1F}\u{1F}The keys in ascending order.\u{1F}alx> Map.keys(%{\"b\" => 2, \"a\" => 1})\n[\"a\", \"b\"]\u{1E}Map.merge\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.merge(map[k,v], map[k,v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1D}Map.merge(map[k,v], map[k,v], fn[k,v,v,v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}k has an order\u{1F}Merges two maps, the second winning on shared keys; a function may resolve those instead.\u{1F}alx> Map.merge(%{\"a\" => 1}, %{\"a\" => 5, \"b\" => 2})\n%{\"a\" => 5, \"b\" => 2}\nalx> Map.merge(%{\"a\" => 1}, %{\"a\" => 5}, fn _, left, right -> left + right end)\n%{\"a\" => 6}\u{1E}Map.new\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.new(enum[tuple[k,v]]) -> map[k,v]\u{1D}Map.new(enum[a], fn[a,tuple[k,v]]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Builds a map from {key, value} pairs, or from elements transformed into pairs.\u{1F}alx> Map.new([{\"x\", 7}, {\"y\", 8}])\n%{\"x\" => 7, \"y\" => 8}\nalx> Map.new([1, 2], fn n -> {n, n * n} end)\n%{1 => 1, 2 => 4}\u{1E}Map.pop\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.pop(map[k,v], k, v) -> tuple[v,map[k,v]]\u{1D}Map.pop(map[k,v], k) -> tuple[option[v],map[k,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes a key, returning its value (an option, or the default) with the remaining map.\u{1F}alx> Map.pop(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")\n{some(1), Map.delete(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\")}\u{1E}Map.pop_lazy\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.pop_lazy(map[k,v], k, fn[v]) -> tuple[v,map[k,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes a key and returns its value with the rest of the map, or what the function gives and the map unchanged when it is missing.\u{1F}alx> Map.pop_lazy(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", fn -> 0 end)\n{1, %{}}\u{1E}Map.put\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.put(map[k,v], k, v) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the map with the key set to the value.\u{1F}alx> Map.put(%{\"x\" => 1}, \"y\", 2)\n%{\"x\" => 1, \"y\" => 2}\u{1E}Map.put_new\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.put_new(map[k,v], k, v) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets the key only when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.put_new(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", 9)\n%{\"a\" => 1}\u{1E}Map.put_new_lazy\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.put_new_lazy(map[k,v], k, fn[v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets the key from the function only when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.put_new_lazy(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"b\", fn -> 2 end)\n%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2}\u{1E}Map.reject\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.reject(map[k,v], fn[tuple[k,v],boolean]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}k has an order\u{1F}Drops the entries for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Map.reject(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 9}, fn {_, value} -> value > 5 end)\n%{\"a\" => 1}\u{1E}Map.replace\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.replace(map[k,v], k, v) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets the key only when it is present.\u{1F}alx> Map.replace(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"z\", 9)\n%{\"a\" => 1}\u{1E}Map.replace!\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.replace!(map[k,v], k, v) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets the key; fails when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.replace!(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", 9)\n%{\"a\" => 9}\u{1E}Map.replace_lazy\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.replace_lazy(map[k,v], k, fn[v,v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces the value of a key with what the function makes of it, when the key is there.\u{1F}alx> Map.replace_lazy(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", fn v -> v + 1 end)\n%{\"a\" => 2}\u{1E}Map.split\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.split(map[k,v], enum[k]) -> tuple[map[k,v],map[k,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits into the entries with the given keys and the rest.\u{1F}alx> Map.split(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2}, [\"a\"])\n{%{\"a\" => 1}, %{\"b\" => 2}}\u{1E}Map.split_with\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.split_with(map[k,v], fn[tuple[k,v],boolean]) -> tuple[map[k,v],map[k,v]]\u{1F}k has an order\u{1F}Partitions the entries by the function.\u{1F}alx> Map.split_with(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 9}, fn {_, value} -> value > 5 end)\n{%{\"b\" => 9}, %{\"a\" => 1}}\u{1E}Map.take\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.take(map[k,v], enum[k]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Keeps only the given keys.\u{1F}alx> Map.take(%{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2}, [\"a\", \"z\"])\n%{\"a\" => 1}\u{1E}Map.to_list\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.to_list(enum[tuple[k,v]]) -> list[tuple[k,v]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The {key, value} pairs in ascending key order.\u{1F}alx> Map.to_list(%{\"a\" => 1})\n[{\"a\", 1}]\u{1E}Map.update\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.update(map[k,v], k, v, fn[v,v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Applies the function to the key's value, or sets the default when the key is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.update(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", 0, &(&1 + 1))\n%{\"a\" => 2}\u{1E}Map.update!\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.update!(map[k,v], k, fn[v,v]) -> map[k,v]\u{1F}\u{1F}Applies the function to the key's value; fails when it is absent.\u{1F}alx> Map.update!(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", &(&1 * 5))\n%{\"a\" => 5}\u{1E}Map.values\u{1F}Map\u{1F}Map.values(map[k,v]) -> list[v]\u{1F}\u{1F}The values in ascending key order.\u{1F}alx> Map.values(%{\"b\" => 2, \"a\" => 1})\n[1, 2]\u{1E}MapSet.delete\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.delete(set[a], a) -> set[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the set without the value.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.delete(MapSet.new([1, 2]), 1) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[2]\u{1E}MapSet.difference\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.difference(set[a], set[a]) -> set[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The elements of the first set that are not in the second.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.difference(MapSet.new([4, 5, 6]), MapSet.new([5])) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[4, 6]\u{1E}MapSet.disjoint?\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.disjoint?(set[a], set[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether two sets share no elements.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.disjoint?(MapSet.new([1]), MapSet.new([2]))\ntrue\u{1E}MapSet.equal?\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.equal?(set[a], set[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether two sets hold the same elements.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.equal?(MapSet.new([3, 4]), MapSet.new([4, 3]))\ntrue\u{1E}MapSet.filter\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.filter(set[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> set[a]\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Keeps the elements for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.filter(MapSet.new(1..5), &(&1 > 3)) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[4, 5]\u{1E}MapSet.intersection\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.intersection(set[a], set[a]) -> set[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The elements in both sets.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.intersection(MapSet.new([3, 4]), MapSet.new([4, 5])) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[4]\u{1E}MapSet.member?\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.member?(set[a], a) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the set contains the value.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.member?(MapSet.new([1, 2]), 2)\ntrue\u{1E}MapSet.new\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.new() -> set[a]\u{1D}MapSet.new(enum[a]) -> set[a]\u{1D}MapSet.new(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> set[b]\u{1F}a is a map key type\u{1D}b is a map key type\u{1F}Builds a set from an enumerable, optionally transforming each element. With no arguments, an empty set whose type comes from its annotation.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.new([3, 1, 3]) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[1, 3]\nalx> MapSet.new([\"a\", \"bb\"], &String.length(&1)) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[1, 2]\u{1E}MapSet.put\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.put(set[a], a) -> set[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns the set with the value added.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.put(MapSet.new([1]), 2) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[1, 2]\u{1E}MapSet.reject\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.reject(set[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> set[a]\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Drops the elements for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.reject(MapSet.new(1..5), &(&1 > 2)) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[1, 2]\u{1E}MapSet.size\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.size(set[a]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of elements.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.size(MapSet.new([1, 1, 2]))\n2\u{1E}MapSet.split_with\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.split_with(set[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> tuple[set[a],set[a]]\u{1F}a has an order\u{1F}Partitions the set by the function.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.split_with(MapSet.new(1..3), &(&1 == 2)) == {MapSet.new([2]), MapSet.new([1, 3])}\ntrue\u{1E}MapSet.subset?\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.subset?(set[a], set[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether every element of the first set is in the second.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.subset?(MapSet.new([1]), MapSet.new([1, 2]))\ntrue\u{1E}MapSet.symmetric_difference\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.symmetric_difference(set[a], set[a]) -> set[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The elements in exactly one of the sets.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.symmetric_difference(MapSet.new([3, 4]), MapSet.new([4, 5])) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[3, 5]\u{1E}MapSet.to_list\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.to_list(enum[a]) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The elements in ascending order.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.to_list(MapSet.new([3, 1, 2]))\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}MapSet.union\u{1F}MapSet\u{1F}MapSet.union(set[a], set[a]) -> set[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}The elements in either set.\u{1F}alx> MapSet.union(MapSet.new([1]), MapSet.new([2])) |> MapSet.to_list()\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Markup.escape\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.escape(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Text made safe inside an HTML element or a quoted attribute value: &, <, >, \" and ' become character references.\u{1F}alx> Markup.escape(\"<a href='x'>&</a>\")\n\"&lt;a href=&\#39;x&\#39;&gt;&amp;&lt;/a&gt;\"\u{1E}Markup.join\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.join(list[Markup]) -> Markup\u{1F}\u{1F}Pieces of markup one after another.\u{1F}alx> items = Enum.map([\"a\", \"b\"], fn x -> ~h\"<li>\#{x}</li>\" end)\nalx> Markup.to_string(Markup.join(items))\n\"<li>a</li><li>b</li>\"\u{1E}Markup.of\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.of(atom) -> Markup\u{1D}Markup.of(float) -> Markup\u{1D}Markup.of(Markup) -> Markup\u{1D}Markup.of(string) -> Markup\u{1D}Markup.of(integer) -> Markup\u{1D}Markup.of(list[Markup]) -> Markup\u{1F}\u{1F}What a template does with a value it is given: a string is escaped, markup is kept, a number or atom is written as text and a list of markup is joined.\u{1F}alx> Markup.to_string(Markup.of(\"1 < 2\"))\n\"1 &lt; 2\"\nalx> Markup.to_string(Markup.of(~h\"<i>kept</i>\"))\n\"<i>kept</i>\"\nalx> Markup.to_string(Markup.of(42))\n\"42\"\u{1E}Markup.parts\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.parts(Markup) -> Markup.Parts\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes markup apart, for a library that sends a page's fixed text once and afterwards only what changed. For markup a ~h template made: template names the template (the same for every value of one ~h, and for another only when its text is the same), fixed is the template's own text in the pieces around its values (one more piece than there are values), and values are the values in order: text already escaped, the Markup of a template inside it, or the list of Markup a list gave. Markup made by Markup.raw, Markup.of or Markup.join has nothing fixed: an empty name, two empty pieces and one value, its text or its list.\u{1F}alx> name = \"<ada>\"\nalx> parts = Markup.parts(~h\"<p>\#{name}</p>\")\nalx> {parts.fixed, parts.values}\n{[\"<p>\", \"</p>\"], [Markup.Value.text(\"&lt;ada&gt;\")]}\nalx> parts.template == Markup.parts(~h\"<p>\#{name}</p>\").template\ntrue\nalx> Markup.parts(Markup.raw(\"<br>\")).values\n[Markup.Value.text(\"<br>\")]\u{1E}Markup.raw\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.raw(string) -> Markup\u{1F}\u{1F}Markup made from text exactly as given, for HTML the program trusts: never for text from users.\u{1F}alx> Markup.to_string(Markup.raw(\"<br>\"))\n\"<br>\"\u{1E}Markup.template\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.template(string, list[string], list[Markup]) -> Markup\u{1F}\u{1F}What ~h builds: markup from a template's name, its fixed text in pieces and the values that go between them. Written by the compiler; a program writes ~h.\u{1F}alx> Markup.to_string(Markup.template(\"greeting\", [\"<p>\", \"</p>\"], [Markup.of(\"hi\")]))\n\"<p>hi</p>\"\u{1E}Markup.to_string\u{1F}Markup\u{1F}Markup.to_string(Markup) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The HTML text of markup, to send or store.\u{1F}alx> name = \"<b>ada</b>\"\nalx> Markup.to_string(~h\"<p>\#{name}</p>\")\n\"<p>&lt;b&gt;ada&lt;/b&gt;</p>\"\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.add\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.add(NaiveDateTime, integer) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1D}NaiveDateTime.add(NaiveDateTime, integer, atom) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a number of seconds.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.add(~N[2026-01-05 23:59:59], 2)\n~N[2026-01-06 00:00:01]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.after?\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.after?(NaiveDateTime, NaiveDateTime) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first is later than the second.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.after?(~N[2026-01-06 00:00:00], ~N[2026-01-05 00:00:00])\ntrue\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.before?\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.before?(NaiveDateTime, NaiveDateTime) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first is earlier than the second.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.before?(~N[2026-01-06 00:00:00], ~N[2026-01-05 00:00:00])\nfalse\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.compare\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.compare(NaiveDateTime, NaiveDateTime) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns :lt, :eq or :gt as the first is earlier than, equal to or later than the second.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.compare(~N[2026-01-05 00:00:00], ~N[2026-01-06 00:00:00])\n:lt\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.diff\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.diff(NaiveDateTime, NaiveDateTime) -> integer\u{1D}NaiveDateTime.diff(NaiveDateTime, NaiveDateTime, atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of seconds from the second to the first.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.diff(~N[2026-01-06 00:00:00], ~N[2026-01-05 00:00:00])\n86400\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.from_iso8601\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.from_iso8601(string) -> result[NaiveDateTime,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses date and time text, returning ok(value) or error(reason).\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.from_iso8601(\"2026-01-05T13:04:05\")\nok(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.from_iso8601!\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.from_iso8601!(string) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses date and time text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.from_iso8601!(\"2026-01-05 13:04:05\")\n~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.new\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.new(Date, Time) -> result[NaiveDateTime,string]\u{1D}NaiveDateTime.new(integer, integer, integer, integer, integer, integer) -> result[NaiveDateTime,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds from a date and a time, or from year, month, day, hour, minute and second.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.new(~D[2026-01-05], ~T[13:04:05])\nok(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\nalx> NaiveDateTime.new(2026, 1, 5, 13, 4, 5)\nok(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.new!\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.new!(Date, Time) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1D}NaiveDateTime.new!(integer, integer, integer, integer, integer, integer) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds from a date and a time, or from six numbers; fails when invalid.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.new!(~D[2026-01-05], ~T[13:04:05])\n~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.shift\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.shift(NaiveDateTime, Duration) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Moves a date and time by a duration: the calendar units first, then the time units.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.shift(~N[2026-01-31 22:00:00], Duration.add(Duration.months(1), Duration.hours(3)))\n~N[2026-03-01 01:00:00]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.to_date\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.to_date(NaiveDateTime) -> Date\u{1F}\u{1F}The date part.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.to_date(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\n~D[2026-01-05]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.to_iso8601\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.to_iso8601(NaiveDateTime) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The value as YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS text.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.to_iso8601(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\n\"2026-01-05T13:04:05\"\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.to_string\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.to_string(NaiveDateTime) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The value as YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS text.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.to_string(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\n\"2026-01-05 13:04:05\"\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.to_time\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.to_time(NaiveDateTime) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}The time part.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.to_time(~N[2026-01-05 13:04:05])\n~T[13:04:05]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.truncate\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.truncate(NaiveDateTime, atom) -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops precision, as Time.truncate does.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.truncate(~N[2026-01-01 10:00:00.5], :second)\n~N[2026-01-01 10:00:00]\u{1E}NaiveDateTime.utc_now\u{1F}NaiveDateTime\u{1F}NaiveDateTime.utc_now() -> NaiveDateTime\u{1F}\u{1F}The current date and time in UTC, without a zone.\u{1F}alx> NaiveDateTime.after?(NaiveDateTime.utc_now(), ~N[2020-01-01 00:00:00])\ntrue\u{1E}Node.address\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.address() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The address this node listens at, with the port that was picked.\u{1F}alx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> String.starts_with?(Node.address(), \"127.0.0.1:\")\ntrue\u{1E}Node.call\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.call(string, atom, a) -> result[b,string]\u{1D}Node.call(string, atom, a, integer) -> result[b,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs a function a node serves and returns its result: ok(value), or error(reason) when it failed, nothing is served under the name, or no answer came within 5 seconds or the given milliseconds. The result's type comes from the annotation.\u{1F}alx> Node.serve(:square_doc, fn(n: integer): integer do n * n end)\ntrue\nalx> squared: result[integer, string] = Node.call(Node.self(), :square_doc, 7)\nalx> squared\nok(49)\u{1E}Node.cast\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.cast(string, atom, a) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs a function a node serves without waiting for it, whatever the function returns. On this node it reports whether the name is served; a cast to another node is true once it is on its way, and is dropped if that node serves nothing under the name.\u{1F}alx> Node.serve(:noted_doc, fn(n: integer): integer do n end)\ntrue\nalx> Node.cast(Node.self(), :noted_doc, 1)\ntrue\u{1E}Node.connect\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.connect(string) -> result[string,string]\u{1D}Node.connect(string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> result[string,string]\u{1D}Node.connect(string, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Connects to the node listening at an address and returns its name. Both nodes must have the same cookie. Messages to its processes are then copied over the connection. With reconnect: true the connection is kept: when it is lost it is made again, a second later at first and up to 30 seconds apart while that keeps failing, until the program ends. With reconnect: n it is given up after n failed attempts in a row, which Node.monitor_nodes reports as \"noreconnect\" and the node's name.\u{1F}alx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> case Node.connect(\"127.0.0.1:1\") do\n...>   ok(name) -> name\n...>   error(_) -> \"not reachable\"\n...> end\n\"not reachable\"\u{1E}Node.disconnect\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.disconnect(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes the connection to a node; false when there is none.\u{1F}alx> Node.disconnect(\"nobody\")\nfalse\u{1E}Node.list\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.list() -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The names of the nodes this one is connected to, in order.\u{1F}alx> length(Node.list())\n0\u{1E}Node.monitor\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.monitor(string) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Asks for one event when the connection to a node ends, read with Process.event: its reason is \"nodedown\" and the node's name. A node that is not connected produces the event at once.\u{1F}alx> reference = Node.monitor(\"elsewhere\")\nalx> {arrived, noticed, _, reason} = Process.event(1000)\nalx> arrived and noticed == reference and reason == \"nodedown elsewhere\"\ntrue\u{1E}Node.monitor_nodes\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.monitor_nodes() -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Subscribes the calling process to an event, read with Process.event, for every connection to another node from now on: its reason is \"nodeup\" and the node's name when one is made, \"nodedown\" when one is lost, and \"noreconnect\" when a connection kept with reconnect: n is given up. Every event carries the reference returned. The program must be a node.\u{1F}alx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> reference = Node.monitor_nodes()\nalx> {arrived, _, _, _} = Process.event(10)\nalx> arrived or reference == 0\nfalse\u{1E}Node.register_global\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.register_global(pid[a], atom) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Registers a process under a name that no connected node has for the same message type, so that any of them finds it with whereis_global; false when one has.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Node.register_global(me, :global_doc)\ntrue\nalx> Node.register_global(me, :global_doc)\nfalse\u{1E}Node.self\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.self() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}This node's name, or \"\" before Node.start.\u{1F}alx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> Node.self()\n\"docs\"\u{1E}Node.serve\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.serve(atom, fn[a,b]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Offers a function to other nodes under a name, for Node.call and Node.cast: each call runs it in a process of its own, and a failure is returned to the caller as error(reason). Its argument and result must be able to cross between nodes.\u{1F}alx> Node.serve(:twice_doc, fn(n: integer): integer do n * 2 end)\ntrue\u{1E}Node.start\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.start(string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1D}Node.start(string, string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1D}Node.start(string, string, string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Makes this program a node: it listens at the address for other nodes, under a name, and accepts those that know the same cookie. Port 0 picks a free port. With two arguments the cookie comes from the ALCHEM_COOKIE environment variable. Connections are encrypted; a fourth argument, [encrypted: false], turns that off for a trusted network, and only nodes with the same setting connect. A program becomes a node once.\u{1F}alx> started = Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> started == ok({}) or started == error(\"this program is already a node\")\ntrue\nalx> Node.self()\n\"docs\"\nalx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\", [loud: true])\nerror(\"unknown option :loud; Node.start takes encrypted:\")\u{1E}Node.stream\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.stream(string, atom) -> result[stream[a],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Finds a stream another node has published and returns one that pulls from it, a chunk at a time, as it is consumed. The element type comes from the annotation and must be the one published. An endless stream can be read from; a reader that stops early pulls no more. Reading fails if the other node stops answering.\u{1F}alx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> squares: result[stream[integer], string] = Node.stream(\"elsewhere\", :doc_squares)\nalx> case squares do\n...>   ok(found) -> Enum.take(found, 3)\n...>   error(_) -> Enum.take([0], 0)\n...> end\nEnum.take([0], 0)\u{1E}Node.whereis\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.whereis(string, atom) -> option[pid[a]]\u{1D}Node.whereis(string, atom, integer) -> option[pid[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Finds a process another node has registered under a name, waiting up to 5 seconds or the given milliseconds. The message type comes from the annotation and must be the type the name was registered with. The handle can be sent to and put in messages; receiving, monitoring and linking need a process of this node.\u{1F}alx> Node.start(\"docs\", \"127.0.0.1:0\", \"secret\")\nalx> found: option[pid[string]] = Node.whereis(\"elsewhere\", :logger, 100)\nalx> case found do\n...>   some(logger) -> Process.send(logger, \"hello\", 100)\n...>   none -> false\n...> end\nfalse\u{1E}Node.whereis_global\u{1F}Node\u{1F}Node.whereis_global(atom) -> option[pid[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Finds a process registered under a name on this node or any connected one. The message type comes from the annotation.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Node.register_global(me, :global_find_doc)\ntrue\nalx> found: option[pid[string]] = Node.whereis_global(:global_find_doc)\nalx> case found do\n...>   some(_) -> \"found\"\n...>   none -> \"missing\"\n...> end\n\"found\"\u{1E}OptionParser.parse\u{1F}OptionParser\u{1F}OptionParser.parse(enum[string], map[string,string]) -> tuple[map[string,string],list[string],list[tuple[string,string]]]\u{1D}OptionParser.parse(enum[string], map[string,string], map[string,string]) -> tuple[map[string,string],list[string],list[tuple[string,string]]]\u{1D}OptionParser.parse(enum[string], list[tuple[atom,list[tuple[atom,atom]]]]) -> tuple[list[tuple[atom,string]],list[string],list[tuple[string,option[string]]]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads switches from command-line arguments. The second argument maps each switch to its type: boolean, string, integer, float or count; a third maps one-letter aliases to switch names. It returns the values as text by name, the arguments that are not switches, and the switches that are unknown or have a value of the wrong type. --name=value and --name value both work, --no-name sets a boolean to false, dashes in a name become underscores, and -- ends the switches.\u{1F}alx> {options, files, invalid} = OptionParser.parse([\"--verbose\", \"--jobs=4\", \"a.txt\", \"--dry-run\", \"--color\", \"red\", \"b.txt\"], %{\"verbose\" => \"boolean\", \"jobs\" => \"integer\", \"dry_run\" => \"boolean\"})\nalx> options\n%{\"dry_run\" => \"true\", \"jobs\" => \"4\", \"verbose\" => \"true\"}\nalx> files\n[\"a.txt\", \"red\", \"b.txt\"]\nalx> invalid\n[{\"--color\", \"\"}]\nalx> {short, _, _} = OptionParser.parse([\"-v\", \"-v\", \"--no-color\", \"-n\", \"x\"], %{\"verbose\" => \"count\", \"color\" => \"boolean\", \"name\" => \"string\"}, %{\"v\" => \"verbose\", \"n\" => \"name\"})\nalx> short\n%{\"color\" => \"false\", \"name\" => \"x\", \"verbose\" => \"2\"}\u{1E}OptionParser.parse_head\u{1F}OptionParser\u{1F}OptionParser.parse_head(enum[string], list[tuple[atom,list[tuple[atom,atom]]]]) -> tuple[list[tuple[atom,string]],list[string],list[tuple[string,option[string]]]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like OptionParser.parse with options, stopping at the first argument that is not a switch.\u{1F}alx> OptionParser.parse_head([\"--x\", \"1\", \"cmd\", \"--y\"], [strict: [x: :integer]])\n{[x: \"1\"], [\"cmd\", \"--y\"], []}\u{1E}OptionParser.split\u{1F}OptionParser\u{1F}OptionParser.split(string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits a command line into arguments as a shell does: at spaces outside quotes, with backslash escapes.\u{1F}alx> OptionParser.split(\"a 'b c' \\\"d e\\\"\")\n[\"a\", \"b c\", \"d e\"]\u{1E}OptionParser.to_argv\u{1F}OptionParser\u{1F}OptionParser.to_argv(list[tuple[atom,string]]) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes switches back as arguments: --name value, and --name or --no-name for \"true\" and \"false\".\u{1F}alx> OptionParser.to_argv([name: \"ada\", debug: \"true\", color: \"false\"])\n[\"--name\", \"ada\", \"--debug\", \"--no-color\"]\u{1E}Path.absname\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.absname(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The path made absolute against the working directory.\u{1F}alx> Path.absname(\"a.txt\") == Path.join(File.cwd!(), \"a.txt\")\ntrue\u{1E}Path.basename\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.basename(string) -> string\u{1D}Path.basename(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The last component, optionally without an extension.\u{1F}alx> Path.basename(\"src/main.alc\")\n\"main.alc\"\nalx> Path.basename(\"src/main.alc\", \".alc\")\n\"main\"\u{1E}Path.dirname\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.dirname(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Everything before the last component.\u{1F}alx> Path.dirname(\"src/app/main.alc\")\n\"src/app\"\u{1E}Path.expand\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.expand(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}An absolute path with . and .. resolved and a leading ~ replaced by the home directory.\u{1F}alx> String.ends_with?(Path.expand(\"a/b/../c\"), \"/a/c\")\ntrue\u{1E}Path.extname\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.extname(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The extension of the last component, with its dot.\u{1F}alx> Path.extname(\"main.alc\")\n\".alc\"\nalx> Path.extname(\"README\")\n\"\"\u{1E}Path.join\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.join(enum[string]) -> string\u{1D}Path.join(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Joins path components with a separator.\u{1F}alx> Path.join(\"src\", \"main.alc\")\n\"src/main.alc\"\nalx> Path.join([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n\"a/b/c\"\u{1E}Path.relative_to\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.relative_to(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The path relative to a base, or unchanged when it is not under the base.\u{1F}alx> Path.relative_to(\"/usr/local/bin\", \"/usr\")\n\"local/bin\"\u{1E}Path.rootname\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.rootname(string) -> string\u{1D}Path.rootname(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The path without its extension, or without the given one.\u{1F}alx> Path.rootname(\"src/main.alc\")\n\"src/main\"\nalx> Path.rootname(\"main.alc\", \".go\")\n\"main.alc\"\u{1E}Path.safe_relative\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.safe_relative(string) -> option[string]\u{1D}Path.safe_relative(string, string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}A relative path cleaned of . and .., when it stays inside the directory it is relative to; none when it would leave it or is absolute. Given a directory, an absolute path inside it is made relative to it first.\u{1F}alx> Path.safe_relative(\"a/../b\")\nsome(\"b\")\nalx> Path.safe_relative(\"../etc\")\nnone\u{1E}Path.split\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.split(string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The components of a path.\u{1F}alx> Path.split(\"src/app/main.alc\")\n[\"src\", \"app\", \"main.alc\"]\u{1E}Path.type\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.type(string) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}Whether a path is :absolute, :relative, or on Windows :volumerelative.\u{1F}alx> Path.type(Path.expand(\"docs\"))\n:absolute\nalx> Path.type(\"docs\")\n:relative\u{1E}Path.wildcard\u{1F}Path\u{1F}Path.wildcard(string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The existing paths matching a glob pattern, sorted.\u{1F}alx> File.mkdir_p(\"doc_wildcard\")\nok({})\nalx> File.write!(\"doc_wildcard/a.txt\", \"\")\nalx> Path.wildcard(\"doc_wildcard/*.txt\")\n[\"doc_wildcard/a.txt\"]\u{1E}Port.close\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.close(port) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes the program's standard input, so a program that reads to the end can finish; false when it was already closed. The program is ended when its owner, the process its output goes to, ends, and when the Alchem program does, also when it is interrupted or asked to terminate; Port.kill ends it on request.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(8)\nalx> case Port.open(\"cat\", Enum.take([\"\"], 0), me) do\n...>   ok(echo) -> {Port.close(echo), Port.close(echo)}\n...>   error(_) -> {false, false}\n...> end\n{true, false}\nalx> assert_receive(me, PortEvent.exit(0), 5000)\u{1E}Port.command\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.command(port, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes text to the program's standard input; false once the port is closed or the program has gone.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(8)\nalx> case Port.open(\"cat\", Enum.take([\"\"], 0), me) do\n...>   ok(echo) -> Port.command(echo, \"hello\\n\") and Port.close(echo)\n...>   error(_) -> false\n...> end\ntrue\nalx> assert_receive(me, PortEvent.data(\"hello\"), 5000)\u{1E}Port.info\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.info(port) -> option[Port.Info]\u{1F}\u{1F}What is known about a port's running program: its name, operating system process id and the bytes written to and read from it; none once it has ended.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(8)\nalx> ok(port) = Port.open(\"cat\", [], me)\nalx> some(info) = Port.info(port)\nalx> info.name\n\"cat\"\nalx> Port.close(port)\ntrue\u{1E}Port.kill\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.kill(port) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Ends the program and waits briefly until it has gone; false when it had already ended. The program is asked to stop first, with an interrupt where the system has one, so that a program which runs others can stop them, and is killed if it has not gone half a second later. The owner still receives PortEvent.exit.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(8)\nalx> case Port.open(\"cat\", Enum.take([\"\"], 0), me) do\n...>   ok(waiting) -> {Port.kill(waiting), Port.kill(waiting)}\n...>   error(_) -> {false, false}\n...> end\n{true, false}\nalx> assert_receive(me, PortEvent.exit(_), 5000)\u{1E}Port.monitor\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.monitor(port, pid[PortEvent]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends the program's exit status to another process too, as PortEvent.exit, when it ends; false when it has already ended.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(8)\nalx> ok(port) = Port.open(\"true\", [], me)\nalx> Process.receive(me, 5000)\n{true, PortEvent.exit(0)}\nalx> Port.monitor(port, me)\nfalse\u{1E}Port.open\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.open(string, enum[string], pid[PortEvent]) -> result[port,string]\u{1D}Port.open(string, enum[string], pid[PortEvent], list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> result[port,string]\u{1D}Port.open(string, enum[string], pid[PortEvent], list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[port,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts an external program with arguments. Each line it writes, to standard output or standard error, is sent to the owner as PortEvent.data(text), and PortEvent.exit(status) follows when it ends. The result is an error when the program cannot be started.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(16)\nalx> case Port.open(\"sort\", Enum.take([\"\"], 0), me) do\n...>   ok(sorter) ->\n...>     Port.command(sorter, \"pear\\napple\\n\")\n...>     Port.close(sorter)\n...>   error(_) -> false\n...> end\ntrue\nalx> assert_receive(me, PortEvent.data(first), 5000)\nalx> first\n\"apple\"\nalx> assert_receive(me, PortEvent.data(\"pear\"), 5000)\nalx> assert_receive(me, PortEvent.exit(status), 5000)\nalx> status\n0\nalx> case Port.open(\"alchem-no-such-program\", Enum.take([\"\"], 0), me) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"started\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"not found\"\n...> end\n\"not found\"\u{1E}Port.os_pid\u{1F}Port\u{1F}Port.os_pid(port) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The operating system's process identifier of the program.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[PortEvent] = Process.self(8)\nalx> case Port.open(\"cat\", Enum.take([\"\"], 0), me) do\n...>   ok(echo) -> Port.os_pid(echo) > 0 and Port.close(echo)\n...>   error(_) -> false\n...> end\ntrue\u{1E}Process.alive\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.alive(pid[t]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the process is still running.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(1)\nalx> Process.alive(me)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.await\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.await(pid[t], integer) -> tuple[boolean,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for a process and everything it started to end, returning whether it did and its exit reason.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do 0 end, 1)\nalx> Process.await(worker, 1000)\n{true, \"normal\"}\u{1E}Process.cancel_timer\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.cancel_timer(integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Cancels a timer from Process.send_after or Server.send_after; false when it had already fired or been cancelled.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> timer = Process.send_after(me, \"late\", 60000)\nalx> Process.cancel_timer(timer)\ntrue\nalx> Process.cancel_timer(timer)\nfalse\u{1E}Process.count\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.count() -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}How many processes of the program are running, the caller among them. A request being handled, a task and a server each count as one.\u{1F}alx> before = Process.count()\nalx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(inbox: pid[integer]): integer do\n...>   {_, value} = Process.receive(inbox, 1000)\n...>   value\n...> end, 1)\nalx> Process.count() == before + 1\ntrue\nalx> Process.send(worker, 0, 100)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.crash\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.crash(string) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails the current process with a reason. Typed as integer so a worker can end with it.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.crash(\"bad input\") end, 1)\nalx> {ended, reason} = Process.await(worker, 1000)\nalx> ended and String.contains?(reason, \"bad input\")\ntrue\u{1E}Process.demonitor\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.demonitor(pid[t], integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Cancels a monitor and discards its notification if already queued.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end, 1)\nalx> reference = Process.monitor(worker)\nalx> Process.demonitor(worker, reference)\ntrue\nalx> Process.stop(worker)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.event\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.event(integer) -> tuple[boolean,integer,integer,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for a monitor or link notification: {arrived, reference, process_id, reason}. Link events have reference 0.\u{1F}alx> {arrived, _, _, _} = Process.event(1)\nalx> arrived\nfalse\u{1E}Process.id\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.id(pid[t]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The process's unique positive identifier. Pids are compared by id.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(1)\nalx> Process.id(me) > 0\ntrue\u{1E}Process.link\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.link(pid[t]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Links the current process with another: when either fails, the other is stopped too unless it traps exits.\u{1F}alx> Process.trap_exits(true)\nalx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end, 1)\nalx> Process.link(worker)\ntrue\nalx> Process.unlink(worker)\ntrue\nalx> Process.stop(worker)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.loop\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.loop(s, fn[s,tuple[boolean,s]]) -> s\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs a state loop without growing the stack: the function returns {continue, next_state}, and the final state is returned once continue is false.\u{1F}alx> Process.loop(1, fn(n: integer): tuple[boolean, integer] do {n < 100, n * 2} end)\n256\u{1E}Process.monitor\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.monitor(pid[t]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Asks to be notified once when the process ends, returning a reference. Process.event receives the notification.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do 0 end, 1)\nalx> reference = Process.monitor(worker)\nalx> {arrived, noticed, _, reason} = Process.event(1000)\nalx> arrived and noticed == reference and reason == \"normal\"\ntrue\u{1E}Process.receive\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.receive(pid[t], integer) -> tuple[boolean,t]\u{1F}\u{1F}Takes the next message from the current process's mailbox, waiting up to the given milliseconds. The flag is false on timeout.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Process.send(me, 7, 100)\ntrue\nalx> Process.receive(me, 100)\n{true, 7}\nalx> {arrived, _} = Process.receive(me, 10)\nalx> arrived\nfalse\u{1E}Process.register\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.register(pid[a], atom) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Gives a process a name by which this node, and the nodes connected to it, can find it. A name belongs to one living process at a time. The message type must be one that can be copied to another node: no functions, streams or other handles tied to this program.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Process.register(me, :doc_register)\ntrue\nalx> Process.register(me, :doc_register)\nfalse\nalx> Process.unregister(:doc_register)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.report\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.report(pid[t]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Where and why a process failed, or its exit reason; meaningful once it has ended.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.crash(\"bad input\") end, 1)\nalx> Process.await(worker, 1000)\nalx> String.contains?(Process.report(worker), \"bad input\")\ntrue\u{1E}Process.self\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.self(integer) -> pid[t]\u{1F}\u{1F}The current process, creating its mailbox with the given capacity on first use. The message type comes from the annotation.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(8)\nalx> Process.send(me, \"hello\", 100)\ntrue\nalx> Process.receive(me, 100)\n{true, \"hello\"}\u{1E}Process.send\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.send(pid[t], t, integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Queues a message for a process, waiting up to the given milliseconds for room. False when it timed out or the process has ended.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Process.send(me, 7, 100)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.send_after\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.send_after(atom, a, integer) -> integer\u{1D}Process.send_after(pid[a], a, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a message to a process after the given milliseconds and returns a timer reference for Process.cancel_timer. A message to a process that has ended by then is dropped. Given a name instead of a pid, the message goes to whatever holds the name when the time comes: a process registered with Process.register whose messages are of the message's type, or a server registered with Server.register whose requests are, as a cast; when nothing fitting holds it, the message is dropped.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> timer = Process.send_after(me, \"ping\", 10)\nalx> timer > 0\ntrue\nalx> Process.receive(me, 1000)\n{true, \"ping\"}\nalx> Process.register(me, :doc_inbox)\ntrue\nalx> Process.send_after(:doc_inbox, \"named\", 10) > 0\ntrue\nalx> Process.receive(me, 1000)\n{true, \"named\"}\nalx> Process.unregister(:doc_inbox)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.sleep\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.sleep(integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Pauses the current process for a number of milliseconds and returns 0. Process.sleep(0) yields to other processes.\u{1F}alx> Process.sleep(1)\n0\u{1E}Process.spawn\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.spawn(fn[pid[t],integer], integer) -> pid[t]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a process running the function, which receives its own pid and returns an exit status. The second argument is the mailbox capacity.\u{1F}alx> parent: pid[integer] = Process.self(8)\nalx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do\n...>   {_, n} = Process.receive(self, 1000)\n...>   Process.send(parent, n * 2, 1000)\n...>   0\n...> end, 8)\nalx> Process.send(worker, 21, 1000)\ntrue\nalx> Process.receive(parent, 1000)\n{true, 42}\u{1E}Process.stop\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.stop(pid[t]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Asks a process to stop; true when this request was the first.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end, 1)\nalx> Process.stop(worker)\ntrue\nalx> {ended, _} = Process.await(worker, 1000)\nalx> ended\ntrue\u{1E}Process.trap_exits\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.trap_exits(boolean) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Chooses whether linked failures arrive as events instead of stopping this process, returning the previous setting.\u{1F}alx> Process.trap_exits(true)\nalx> Process.trap_exits(false)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.unlink\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.unlink(pid[t]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes a link in both directions; true when there was one.\u{1F}alx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end, 1)\nalx> Process.unlink(worker)\nfalse\nalx> Process.stop(worker)\ntrue\u{1E}Process.unregister\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.unregister(atom) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Frees a name; false when it was not registered.\u{1F}alx> Process.unregister(:doc_nobody)\nfalse\u{1E}Process.whereis\u{1F}Process\u{1F}Process.whereis(atom) -> option[pid[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Finds a process of this node by name. The message type comes from the annotation and must be the one the name was registered with.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Process.register(me, :doc_whereis)\ntrue\nalx> found: option[pid[integer]] = Process.whereis(:doc_whereis)\nalx> case found do\n...>   some(named) -> Process.send(named, 7, 100)\n...>   none -> false\n...> end\ntrue\nalx> assert_receive(me, 7)\nalx> other: option[pid[string]] = Process.whereis(:doc_whereis)\nalx> case other do\n...>   some(_) -> \"found\"\n...>   none -> \"registered for another type\"\n...> end\n\"registered for another type\"\u{1E}Range.disjoint?\u{1F}Range\u{1F}Range.disjoint?(Range, Range) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether two ranges hold no integer in common.\u{1F}alx> Range.disjoint?(1..5, 6..9)\ntrue\nalx> Range.disjoint?(1..5, 5..9)\nfalse\u{1E}Range.new\u{1F}Range\u{1F}Range.new(integer, integer) -> Range\u{1D}Range.new(integer, integer, integer) -> Range\u{1F}\u{1F}The range from first to last, a step of 1 apart, or of a given step.\u{1F}alx> Range.new(1, 3)\n1..3\nalx> Range.new(0, 10, 5) |> Enum.to_list()\n[0, 5, 10]\u{1E}Range.shift\u{1F}Range\u{1F}Range.shift(Range, integer) -> Range\u{1F}\u{1F}Moves a range by a number of its steps.\u{1F}alx> Range.shift(1..3, 2)\n3..5\u{1E}Range.size\u{1F}Range\u{1F}Range.size(Range) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}How many integers a range holds.\u{1F}alx> Range.size(1..10)\n10\nalx> Range.size(1..10//4)\n3\nalx> Range.size(1..0//1)\n0\u{1E}Range.split\u{1F}Range\u{1F}Range.split(Range, integer) -> tuple[Range,Range]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits a range after a number of its integers, or before the last of them for a negative number.\u{1F}alx> Range.split(1..10, 3)\n{1..3, 4..10}\u{1E}Range.to_list\u{1F}Range\u{1F}Range.to_list(Range) -> list[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The integers of a range, in order.\u{1F}alx> Range.to_list(5..1//-2)\n[5, 3, 1]\u{1E}Regex.compile\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.compile(string) -> result[regex,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Compiles a pattern from text, returning an error for an invalid one. A ~r/.../ literal is checked at compile time instead.\u{1F}alx> case Regex.compile(\"a+\") do\n...>   ok(pattern) -> Regex.match?(pattern, \"caat\")\n...>   error(_) -> false\n...> end\ntrue\u{1E}Regex.compile!\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.compile!(string) -> regex\u{1F}\u{1F}Compiles a pattern from text; fails on an invalid one.\u{1F}alx> Regex.match?(Regex.compile!(\"^a\"), \"abc\")\ntrue\u{1E}Regex.match?\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.match?(regex, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the pattern matches anywhere in the string.\u{1F}alx> Regex.match?(~r/\\d+/, \"order 42\")\ntrue\nalx> Regex.match?(~r/^hello/i, \"HELLO there\")\ntrue\u{1E}Regex.named_captures\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.named_captures(regex, string) -> option[map[string,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The named groups of the first match, as a map.\u{1F}alx> Regex.named_captures(~r/(?P<key>\\w+)=(?P<value>\\w+)/, \"a=1\")\nsome(%{\"key\" => \"a\", \"value\" => \"1\"})\u{1E}Regex.replace\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.replace(regex, string, string) -> string\u{1D}Regex.replace(regex, string, fn[string,string]) -> string\u{1D}Regex.replace(regex, string, fn[list[string],string]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces every match; \\\\1 in the replacement inserts a group. The replacement may instead be a function of the match, or, when its parameter is declared list[string], of the match followed by its groups as Regex.run gives them.\u{1F}alx> Regex.replace(~r/\\d/, \"a1b2\", \"\#\")\n\"a\#b\#\"\nalx> Regex.replace(~r/\\d+/, \"a1b22\", fn digits -> \"<\" <> digits <> \">\" end)\n\"a<1>b<22>\"\nalx> Regex.replace(~r/(\\w)=(\\d)/, \"a=1 b=2\", fn(found: list[string]): string do Enum.at(found, 2, \"\") <> Enum.at(found, 1, \"\") end)\n\"1a 2b\"\u{1E}Regex.run\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.run(regex, string) -> option[list[string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The first match followed by its groups, or none.\u{1F}alx> Regex.run(~r/(\\w+)@(\\w+)/, \"mail joe@example now\")\nsome([\"joe@example\", \"joe\", \"example\"])\nalx> Regex.run(~r/x/, \"abc\")\nnone\u{1E}Regex.scan\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.scan(regex, string) -> list[list[string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Every match, each followed by its groups, as Regex.run gives the first.\u{1F}alx> Regex.scan(~r/\\d+/, \"1 and 22\")\n[[\"1\"], [\"22\"]]\nalx> Regex.scan(~r/(\\w)(\\d)/, \"a1 b2\")\n[[\"a1\", \"a\", \"1\"], [\"b2\", \"b\", \"2\"]]\u{1E}Regex.source\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.source(regex) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The pattern text.\u{1F}alx> Regex.source(~r/a+b/)\n\"a+b\"\u{1E}Regex.split\u{1F}Regex\u{1F}Regex.split(regex, string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits the string wherever the pattern matches.\u{1F}alx> Regex.split(~r/\\s*,\\s*/, \"a , b,c\")\n[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\u{1E}Registry.count\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.count(registry[k,v]) -> integer\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The number of entries held by living processes.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do\n...>   Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end, 1)\nalx> Process.sleep(50)\nalx> Registry.count(rooms)\n1\nalx> Process.send(worker, 0, 100)\ntrue\nalx> Process.await(worker, 1000)\n{true, \"normal\"}\nalx> Registry.count(rooms)\n0\u{1E}Registry.count_match\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.count_match(registry[k,v], k, fn[v,boolean]) -> integer\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}How many entries under a key have values that pass the test.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 7)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.count_match(rooms, \"lobby\", fn n -> n > 5 end)\n1\u{1E}Registry.dispatch\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.dispatch(registry[k,v], k, fn[list[tuple[integer,v]],b]) -> atom\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Calls the function with the entries under a key, as {process id, value}, when there are any, and returns :ok. Put a pid in the value to send to the processes.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, pid[string]] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"news\", me)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.dispatch(rooms, \"news\", fn entries -> Enum.each(entries, fn {_, inbox} -> Process.send(inbox, \"hello\", 100) end) end)\n:ok\nalx> Process.receive(me, 1000)\n{true, \"hello\"}\u{1E}Registry.entries\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.entries(registry[k,v], k) -> list[tuple[integer,v]]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The process ids and values registered under a key by living processes, in the order they registered, as Elixir's lookup gives pids and values. Process.id gives a pid's id.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Registry.entries(rooms, \"lobby\") == [{Process.id(me), 1}]\ntrue\u{1E}Registry.keys\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.keys(registry[k,v], pid[a]) -> list[k]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The keys a process holds.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Registry.keys(rooms, me)\n[\"lobby\"]\u{1E}Registry.lookup\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.lookup(registry[k,v], k) -> list[v]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The values registered under a key by living processes, in the order they registered. Elixir also returns each entry's pid; put the pid in the value when it is needed.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 2)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.lookup(rooms, \"lobby\")\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Registry.match\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.match(registry[k,v], k, fn[v,boolean]) -> list[tuple[integer,v]]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The entries under a key whose values pass the test, as {process id, value}.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 7)\nok({})\nalx> Enum.map(Registry.match(rooms, \"lobby\", fn n -> n > 5 end), fn {_, n} -> n end)\n[7]\u{1E}Registry.meta\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.meta(registry[k,v], atom) -> option[m]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}A value stored with put_meta. Its type comes from the annotation; a value of another type is not found.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.put_meta(rooms, :owner, \"chat\")\n:ok\nalx> owner: option[string] = Registry.meta(rooms, :owner)\nalx> owner\nsome(\"chat\")\u{1E}Registry.partitions\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.partitions(registry[k,v]) -> integer\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The number of partitions; start_link takes [partitions: n] after the keys option. Each partition has its own lock, so processes registering under different keys seldom wait for each other.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate], [partitions: 8])\nalx> Registry.partitions(rooms)\n8\u{1E}Registry.put_meta\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.put_meta(registry[k,v], atom, m) -> atom\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Stores a value under a name for the whole registry, and returns :ok.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.put_meta(rooms, :owner, \"chat\")\n:ok\u{1E}Registry.register\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.register(registry[k,v], k, v) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Registers the calling process under a key with a value, until it ends or unregisters. In a unique registry a key held by a living process gives error(\"already_registered\").\u{1F}alx> names: registry[atom, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :unique])\nalx> Registry.register(names, :alpha, 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.register(names, :alpha, 2)\nerror(\"already_registered\")\u{1E}Registry.select\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.select(registry[k,v], fn[k,integer,v,boolean]) -> list[tuple[k,integer,v]]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Every entry whose key, process id and value pass the test, as {key, process id, value}: what Elixir's select does with a match specification.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"hall\", 2)\nok({})\nalx> Enum.map(Registry.select(rooms, fn key, _, _ -> key == \"hall\" end), fn {key, _, value} -> {key, value} end)\n[{\"hall\", 2}]\u{1E}Registry.start_link\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.start_link(list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> registry[k,v]\u{1D}Registry.start_link(list[tuple[atom,atom]], list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> registry[k,v]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Starts a registry. keys: :unique allows one living entry per key; keys: :duplicate allows any number, from one process or several. The key and value types come from the annotation; keys are integers, strings, booleans or atoms.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, pid[string]] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.count(rooms)\n0\u{1E}Registry.unregister\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.unregister(registry[k,v], k) -> boolean\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Removes the calling process's entries under a key; false when it had none.\u{1F}alx> names: registry[atom, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :unique])\nalx> Registry.register(names, :alpha, 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.unregister(names, :alpha)\ntrue\nalx> Registry.lookup(names, :alpha)\n[]\u{1E}Registry.unregister_match\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.unregister_match(registry[k,v], k, fn[v,boolean]) -> integer\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Removes the calling process's entries under a key whose values pass the test, and returns how many it removed.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 7)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.unregister_match(rooms, \"lobby\", fn n -> n > 5 end)\n1\nalx> Registry.lookup(rooms, \"lobby\")\n[1]\u{1E}Registry.update_value\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.update_value(registry[k,v], k, fn[v,v]) -> result[tuple[v,v],string]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}Replaces the value of the calling process's entry under a key with the function's result: ok({new, old}), or error(\"not_registered\").\u{1F}alx> names: registry[atom, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :unique])\nalx> Registry.register(names, :hits, 1)\nok({})\nalx> Registry.update_value(names, :hits, fn n -> n + 1 end)\nok({2, 1})\u{1E}Registry.values\u{1F}Registry\u{1F}Registry.values(registry[k,v], k, pid[a]) -> list[v]\u{1F}k is a map key type\u{1F}The values one process registered under a key.\u{1F}alx> rooms: registry[string, integer] = Registry.start_link([keys: :duplicate])\nalx> Registry.register(rooms, \"lobby\", 1)\nok({})\nalx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Registry.values(rooms, \"lobby\", me)\n[1]\u{1E}Server.alive\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.alive(server[q,r]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the server is running.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.alive(counter)\ntrue\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.await\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.await(server[q,r], integer) -> tuple[boolean,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for the server to end, returning whether it did and its exit reason.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\nalx> {ended, _} = Server.await(counter, 1000)\nalx> ended\ntrue\u{1E}Server.call\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.call(server[q,r], q, integer) -> result[r,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a request and waits up to the given milliseconds for the reply: ok(reply), or error(\"timeout\"), error(\"server is not running\") or error(\"server stopped: reason\").\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 5, 1000)\nok(5)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.cast\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.cast(server[q,r], q) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Queues a request without waiting for the reply; false when the server has stopped or its queue is full.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.cast(counter, 5)\ntrue\nalx> Server.call(counter, 0, 1000)\nok(5)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.continue\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.continue(q) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Asks the running server to handle a request of its own as soon as it has answered the current one, ahead of any request already waiting: the work a handler defers until after its reply, as handle_continue does in Elixir. Replies to continued requests go nowhere.\u{1F}alx> loader = Server.start([], fn(steps: list[string], step: string): tuple[list[string], list[string]] do\n...>   if step == \"start\" do\n...>     Server.continue(\"load\")\n...>   end\n...>   {steps ++ [step], steps ++ [step]}\n...> end)\nalx> Server.call(loader, \"start\", 1000)\nok([\"start\"])\nalx> Server.call(loader, \"check\", 1000)\nok([\"start\", \"load\", \"check\"])\nalx> Server.stop(loader)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.mailbox\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.mailbox(server[q,r], fn[m,q]) -> pid[m]\u{1F}\u{1F}Gives a server a pid that any process, port or socket can send to. Each message becomes a request, made from the message inside the server by the function and queued like a cast: the messages Elixir delivers to handle_info. The mailbox ends with the server; for a supervised server it outlives restarts.\u{1F}alx> log = Server.start([], fn(seen: list[string], event: string): tuple[list[string], list[string]] do {seen ++ [event], seen ++ [event]} end)\nalx> inbox: pid[integer] = Server.mailbox(log, fn n -> \"got \" <> to_string(n) end)\nalx> Process.send(inbox, 7, 1000)\ntrue\nalx> Process.sleep(50)\nalx> Server.call(log, \"checked\", 1000)\nok([\"got 7\", \"checked\"])\nalx> Server.stop(log)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.monitor\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.monitor(server[q,r], pid[a], fn[string,q]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Turns a process's exit into a request to a server: when the process ends, the function makes a request from the exit reason, inside the server, and it is queued like a cast. This is how a server hears of the processes it depends on.\u{1F}alx> log = Server.start([], fn(seen: list[string], event: string): tuple[list[string], list[string]] do {[event] ++ seen, [event] ++ seen} end)\nalx> worker: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do 0 end, 1)\nalx> Server.monitor(log, worker, fn reason -> \"worker ended: \" <> reason end)\ntrue\nalx> Process.sleep(50)\nalx> Server.call(log, \"checked\", 1000)\nok([\"checked\", \"worker ended: normal\"])\nalx> Server.stop(log)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.register\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.register(server[q,r], atom) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Gives a server a name that Server.whereis finds. A name belongs to one living process or server at a time; a supervised server keeps its name across restarts. Process.unregister frees it.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.register(counter, :doc_counter)\ntrue\nalx> Server.register(counter, :doc_counter)\nfalse\nalx> Process.unregister(:doc_counter)\ntrue\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.restarts\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.restarts(server[q,r]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}How many times a supervised server has been restarted; 0 for an unsupervised one.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.restarts(counter)\n0\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.self\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.self() -> server[q,r]\u{1F}\u{1F}The server whose handler is running, for a handler that schedules requests for itself or gives its handle away. The request and reply types come from the annotation and must be the server's own.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do\n...>   me: server[integer, integer] = Server.self()\n...>   if amount > 1 do\n...>     Server.cast(me, amount - 1)\n...>   end\n...>   {total + amount, total + amount}\n...> end)\nalx> Server.cast(counter, 3)\ntrue\nalx> Process.sleep(50)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 0, 1000)\nok(6)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.send_after\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.send_after(server[q,r], q, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Casts a request to a server after the given milliseconds and returns a timer reference for Process.cancel_timer. A server can schedule work for itself with Server.self.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.send_after(counter, 5, 10) > 0\ntrue\nalx> Process.sleep(100)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 0, 1000)\nok(5)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.start\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.start(s, fn[s,q,tuple[s,r]]) -> server[q,r]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a process owning a state. The handler takes the state and a request and returns {next_state, reply}; requests are answered one at a time, in order.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 5, 1000)\nok(5)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 2, 1000)\nok(7)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.start_supervised\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.start_supervised(s, fn[s,q,tuple[s,r]], integer, integer) -> server[q,r]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like Server.start, restarting the server with its initial state when the handler fails, up to max_restarts within window_ms.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start_supervised(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end, 3, 1000)\nalx> Server.call(counter, 5, 1000)\nok(5)\nalx> Server.restarts(counter)\n0\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.stop\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.stop(server[q,r]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Asks the server to shut down.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Server.whereis\u{1F}Server\u{1F}Server.whereis(atom) -> option[server[q,r]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Finds a server by name. The request and reply types come from the annotation; a name registered for other types, or for a process, is not found.\u{1F}alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\nalx> Server.register(counter, :doc_named)\ntrue\nalx> found: option[server[integer, integer]] = Server.whereis(:doc_named)\nalx> case found do\n...>   some(named) -> Server.call(named, 4, 1000)\n...>   none -> error(\"missing\")\n...> end\nok(4)\nalx> other: option[server[string, string]] = Server.whereis(:doc_named)\nalx> case other do\n...>   some(_) -> \"found\"\n...>   none -> \"not found\"\n...> end\n\"not found\"\nalx> Server.stop(counter)\ntrue\u{1E}Stream.chunk_by\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.chunk_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> stream[list[a]]\u{1F}b supports equality\u{1F}Lazily splits into lists, starting a new one each time the function's result changes.\u{1F}alx> Stream.chunk_by([1, 1, 2, 3, 3], &(&1)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[[1, 1], [2], [3, 3]]\u{1E}Stream.chunk_every\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.chunk_every(enum[a], integer) -> stream[list[a]]\u{1D}Stream.chunk_every(enum[a], integer, integer) -> stream[list[a]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily splits into lists of count elements, optionally starting one every step elements.\u{1F}alx> Stream.chunk_every(11..18, 3) |> Enum.to_list()\n[[11, 12, 13], [14, 15, 16], [17, 18]]\nalx> Stream.from_index() |> Stream.chunk_every(2) |> Enum.take(2)\n[[0, 1], [2, 3]]\u{1E}Stream.chunk_while\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.chunk_while(enum[a], s, fn[a,s,tuple[option[c],s]], fn[s,option[c]]) -> stream[c]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily splits into chunks under the control of a state; see Enum.chunk_while.\u{1F}alx> Stream.chunk_while(1..7, 0, fn n, total ->\n...>   if total + n >= 6 do {some(total + n), 0} else {none, total + n} end\n...> end, fn total -> if total > 0 do some(total) else none end end) |> Enum.to_list()\n[6, 9, 6, 7]\u{1E}Stream.concat\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.concat(enum[list[a]]) -> stream[a]\u{1D}Stream.concat(enum[a], enum[a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily joins two enumerables, or a list of lists.\u{1F}alx> Stream.concat([7, 8], 9..10) |> Enum.to_list()\n[7, 8, 9, 10]\u{1E}Stream.cycle\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.cycle(enum[a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Repeats an enumerable forever.\u{1F}alx> Stream.cycle([1, 2]) |> Enum.take(5)\n[1, 2, 1, 2, 1]\u{1E}Stream.dedup\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.dedup(enum[a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Lazily removes consecutive duplicates.\u{1F}alx> Stream.dedup([5, 5, 6, 5]) |> Enum.to_list()\n[5, 6, 5]\u{1E}Stream.dedup_by\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.dedup_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}b supports equality\u{1F}Lazily removes consecutive elements with the same function result.\u{1F}alx> Stream.dedup_by([1, 3, 2], &rem(&1, 2)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Stream.drop\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.drop(enum[a], integer) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily drops the first count elements.\u{1F}alx> Stream.drop(1..5, 3) |> Enum.to_list()\n[4, 5]\u{1E}Stream.drop_every\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.drop_every(enum[a], integer) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily drops every nth element, starting with the first.\u{1F}alx> Stream.drop_every(1..6, 2) |> Enum.to_list()\n[2, 4, 6]\u{1E}Stream.drop_while\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.drop_while(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily drops elements while the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Stream.drop_while(1..6, &(&1 < 4)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[4, 5, 6]\u{1E}Stream.duplicate\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.duplicate(a, integer) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}A stream of the value repeated count times.\u{1F}alx> Stream.duplicate(\"ab\", 3) |> Enum.to_list()\n[\"ab\", \"ab\", \"ab\"]\u{1E}Stream.each\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.each(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Calls the function with each element as it passes through, leaving the elements unchanged.\u{1F}alx> Stream.each([1, 2], fn n -> IO.write(Integer.to_string(n)) end) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Stream.filter\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.filter(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily keeps elements for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Stream.filter(1..10, &(rem(&1, 3) == 0)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[3, 6, 9]\u{1E}Stream.flat_map\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.flat_map(enum[a], fn[a,list[b]]) -> stream[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily maps each element to a list and concatenates.\u{1F}alx> Stream.flat_map(1..2, fn n -> [n, -n] end) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, -1, 2, -2]\u{1E}Stream.from_index\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.from_index() -> stream[integer]\u{1D}Stream.from_index(integer) -> stream[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The integers from 0, or from the given start, without end.\u{1F}alx> Stream.from_index() |> Enum.take(3)\n[0, 1, 2]\nalx> Stream.from_index(5) |> Enum.take(2)\n[5, 6]\u{1E}Stream.intersperse\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.intersperse(enum[a], a) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily places the separator between elements.\u{1F}alx> Stream.intersperse(4..6, 0) |> Enum.to_list()\n[4, 0, 5, 0, 6]\u{1E}Stream.interval\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.interval(integer) -> stream[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}Emits 0, 1, 2, ... waiting the given milliseconds before each.\u{1F}alx> Stream.interval(1) |> Enum.take(3)\n[0, 1, 2]\u{1E}Stream.into\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.into(enum[string], file) -> stream[string]\u{1D}Stream.into(enum[a], file, fn[a,string]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes each element to a file opened with File.open as the stream is consumed, and passes the elements on; a function, when given, makes the text written for each.\u{1F}alx> path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), \"stream_into_doc.txt\")\nalx> ok(f) = File.open(path, [:write])\nalx> Stream.into([\"a\\n\", \"b\\n\"], f) |> Enum.count()\n2\nalx> File.close(f)\ntrue\nalx> File.read(path)\nok(\"a\\nb\\n\")\u{1E}Stream.iterate\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.iterate(a, fn[a,a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts with a value and keeps applying the function to the previous one.\u{1F}alx> Stream.iterate(3, &(&1 * 3)) |> Enum.take(4)\n[3, 9, 27, 81]\u{1E}Stream.map\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.map(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> stream[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily applies the function to each element.\u{1F}alx> Stream.map(1..1000000000, &(&1 * 2)) |> Enum.take(3)\n[2, 4, 6]\u{1E}Stream.map_every\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.map_every(enum[a], integer, fn[a,a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily applies the function to every nth element.\u{1F}alx> Stream.map_every(1..5, 2, &(&1 * 10)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[10, 2, 30, 4, 50]\u{1E}Stream.publish\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.publish(atom, fn[stream[a]]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Offers a stream to other nodes under a name. The function builds the stream, once for each reader, in a process of this node that advances it as readers ask; false when the name is taken. The stream stays here: only the elements a reader consumes are copied to it.\u{1F}alx> Stream.publish(:doc_squares, fn -> Stream.map(Stream.from_index(1), &(&1 * &1)) end)\ntrue\nalx> Stream.publish(:doc_squares, fn -> Stream.map(1..3, &(&1 * &1)) end)\nfalse\u{1E}Stream.reject\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.reject(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily drops elements for which the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Stream.reject(1..6, &(rem(&1, 2) == 0)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 3, 5]\u{1E}Stream.repeatedly\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.repeatedly(fn[a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Calls the function for each element, without end.\u{1F}alx> Stream.repeatedly(fn -> \"x\" end) |> Enum.take(2)\n[\"x\", \"x\"]\u{1E}Stream.resource\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.resource(fn[s], fn[s,option[tuple[list[a],s]]], fn[s,b]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds a stream from a start function, a next function returning some({items, state}) or none, and a cleanup function that always runs.\u{1F}alx> counter = Stream.resource(fn -> 3 end, fn n -> if n == 0 do none else some({[n], n - 1}) end end, fn _ -> 0 end)\nalx> Enum.to_list(counter)\n[3, 2, 1]\u{1E}Stream.run\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.run(enum[a])\u{1F}\u{1F}Consumes a stream for its effects.\u{1F}alx> Stream.run(Stream.each(1..2, fn n -> IO.write(Integer.to_string(n)) end))\u{1E}Stream.scan\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.scan(enum[a], fn[a,a,a]) -> stream[a]\u{1D}Stream.scan(enum[a], b, fn[a,b,b]) -> stream[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily emits every intermediate result of a fold.\u{1F}alx> Stream.scan(1..4, &(&1 + &2)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 3, 6, 10]\u{1E}Stream.take\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.take(enum[a], integer) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily takes the first count elements.\u{1F}alx> Stream.take(Stream.cycle([1]), 3) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 1, 1]\u{1E}Stream.take_every\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.take_every(enum[a], integer) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily takes every nth element, starting with the first.\u{1F}alx> Stream.take_every(1..10, 4) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 5, 9]\u{1E}Stream.take_while\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.take_while(enum[a], fn[a,boolean]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily takes elements while the function returns true.\u{1F}alx> Stream.take_while(Stream.from_index(), &(&1 < 3)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[0, 1, 2]\u{1E}Stream.timer\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.timer(integer) -> stream[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}Emits 0 once, after the given milliseconds.\u{1F}alx> Stream.timer(1) |> Enum.to_list()\n[0]\u{1E}Stream.transform\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.transform(enum[a], s, fn[a,s,option[tuple[list[b],s]]]) -> stream[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Maps with state: the function returns some({items, state}) to emit and continue, or none to stop.\u{1F}alx> Stream.transform(1..10, 0, fn n, total -> if total + n > 6 do none else some({[total + n], total + n}) end end) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 3, 6]\u{1E}Stream.unfold\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.unfold(s, fn[s,option[tuple[a,s]]]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Generates values from a state: the function returns some({value, next_state}), or none to stop.\u{1F}alx> Stream.unfold(1, fn n -> if n > 20 do none else some({n, n * 3}) end end) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 3, 9]\u{1E}Stream.uniq\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.uniq(enum[a]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}a supports equality\u{1F}Lazily removes elements seen before.\u{1F}alx> Stream.uniq(Stream.cycle([1, 2, 3])) |> Enum.take(3)\n[1, 2, 3]\u{1E}Stream.uniq_by\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.uniq_by(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> stream[a]\u{1F}b supports equality\u{1F}Lazily removes elements whose function result was seen before.\u{1F}alx> Stream.uniq_by([\"a\", \"b\", \"cc\"], &String.length(&1)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[\"a\", \"cc\"]\u{1E}Stream.with_index\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.with_index(enum[a]) -> stream[tuple[a,integer]]\u{1D}Stream.with_index(enum[a], integer) -> stream[tuple[a,integer]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily pairs each element with its index.\u{1F}alx> Stream.with_index([\"a\", \"b\"]) |> Enum.to_list()\n[{\"a\", 0}, {\"b\", 1}]\u{1E}Stream.zip\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.zip(enum[list[a]]) -> stream[list[a]]\u{1D}Stream.zip(enum[a], enum[b]) -> stream[tuple[a,b]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily pairs elements of two enumerables.\u{1F}alx> Stream.zip(Stream.from_index(), [\"a\", \"b\"]) |> Enum.to_list()\n[{0, \"a\"}, {1, \"b\"}]\u{1E}Stream.zip_with\u{1F}Stream\u{1F}Stream.zip_with(enum[a], enum[b], fn[a,b,c]) -> stream[c]\u{1F}\u{1F}Lazily combines paired elements with the function.\u{1F}alx> Stream.zip_with(1..3, Stream.cycle([10]), &(&1 + &2)) |> Enum.to_list()\n[11, 12, 13]\u{1E}String.at\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.at(string, integer) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The character at a zero-based position as an option; negative positions count from the end.\u{1F}alx> String.at(\"héllo\", 1)\nsome(\"é\")\nalx> String.at(\"abc\", 9)\nnone\u{1E}String.capitalize\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.capitalize(string) -> string\u{1D}String.capitalize(string, atom) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Upper-cases the first character and lower-cases the rest.\u{1F}alx> String.capitalize(\"hELLO\")\n\"Hello\"\u{1E}String.codepoints\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.codepoints(string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The Unicode code points as one-character strings.\u{1F}alx> String.codepoints(\"héy\")\n[\"h\", \"é\", \"y\"]\u{1E}String.contains\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.contains(string, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string contains the text. Same as String.contains?.\u{1F}alx> String.contains(\"alchemy\", \"chem\")\ntrue\u{1E}String.contains?\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.contains?(string, string) -> boolean\u{1D}String.contains?(string, list[string]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string contains the text, or any of a list of texts.\u{1F}alx> String.contains?(\"alchemy\", \"chem\")\ntrue\nalx> String.contains?(\"alchemy\", [\"gold\", \"lead\", \"my\"])\ntrue\nalx> String.contains?(\"alchemy\", [])\nfalse\u{1E}String.count\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.count(string, string) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Counts non-overlapping occurrences of the text.\u{1F}alx> String.count(\"banana\", \"an\")\n2\u{1E}String.downcase\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.downcase(string) -> string\u{1D}String.downcase(string, atom) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Lower-cases the string.\u{1F}alx> String.downcase(\"HeLLo\")\n\"hello\"\u{1E}String.duplicate\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.duplicate(string, integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Repeats the string count times.\u{1F}alx> String.duplicate(\"ab\", 3)\n\"ababab\"\u{1E}String.ends_with\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.ends_with(string, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string ends with the suffix. Same as String.ends_with?.\u{1F}alx> String.ends_with(\"main.alc\", \".alc\")\ntrue\u{1E}String.ends_with?\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.ends_with?(string, string) -> boolean\u{1D}String.ends_with?(string, list[string]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string ends with the suffix, or with any of a list of suffixes.\u{1F}alx> String.ends_with?(\"main.alc\", \".alc\")\ntrue\nalx> String.ends_with?(\"main.alc\", [\".ex\", \".exs\"])\nfalse\u{1E}String.first\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.first(string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The first character as an option.\u{1F}alx> String.first(\"abc\")\nsome(\"a\")\nalx> String.first(\"\")\nnone\u{1E}String.graphemes\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.graphemes(string) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The characters of the string as a list.\u{1F}alx> String.graphemes(\"abc\")\n[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\u{1E}String.jaro_distance\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.jaro_distance(string, string) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}How alike two strings are, from 0.0 for nothing in common to 1.0 for equal, by the Jaro measure over grapheme clusters.\u{1F}alx> String.jaro_distance(\"Dwayne\", \"Duane\")\n0.8222222222222223\u{1E}String.join\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.join(list[string], string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Joins a list of strings with a separator. Enum.join also accepts numbers and any enumerable.\u{1F}alx> String.join([\"a\", \"b\"], \"-\")\n\"a-b\"\u{1E}String.last\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.last(string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The last character as an option.\u{1F}alx> String.last(\"abc\")\nsome(\"c\")\u{1E}String.length\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.length(string) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of characters (code points).\u{1F}alx> String.length(\"héllo\")\n5\u{1E}String.match?\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.match?(string, regex) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the regular expression matches anywhere in the string.\u{1F}alx> String.match?(\"order-42\", ~r/\\d+/)\ntrue\u{1E}String.myers_difference\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.myers_difference(string, string) -> list[tuple[atom,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}The edits that turn one string into another, as runs of {:eq | :del | :ins, text}.\u{1F}alx> String.myers_difference(\"abc\", \"adc\")\n[eq: \"a\", del: \"b\", ins: \"d\", eq: \"c\"]\u{1E}String.next_codepoint\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.next_codepoint(string) -> option[tuple[string,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits off the first character, or none for an empty string.\u{1F}alx> String.next_codepoint(\"abc\")\nsome({\"a\", \"bc\"})\u{1E}String.next_grapheme\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.next_grapheme(string) -> option[tuple[string,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits off the first grapheme cluster, or none for an empty string.\u{1F}alx> String.next_grapheme(\"e\\u0301x\")\nsome({\"e\\u0301\", \"x\"})\u{1E}String.normalize\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.normalize(string, atom) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The string in Unicode normalization form :nfc, :nfd, :nfkc or :nfkd.\u{1F}alx> String.normalize(\"e\\u0301\", :nfc)\n\"é\"\nalx> String.length(String.normalize(\"é\", :nfd))\n1\nalx> String.normalize(\"ﬁ\", :nfkc)\n\"fi\"\u{1E}String.pad_leading\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.pad_leading(string, integer) -> string\u{1D}String.pad_leading(string, integer, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Pads on the left to a length, with spaces or the given text.\u{1F}alx> String.pad_leading(\"7\", 3, \"0\")\n\"007\"\nalx> String.pad_leading(\"ab\", 4)\n\"  ab\"\u{1E}String.pad_trailing\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.pad_trailing(string, integer) -> string\u{1D}String.pad_trailing(string, integer, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Pads on the right to a length, with spaces or the given text.\u{1F}alx> String.pad_trailing(\"ab\", 4, \".\")\n\"ab..\"\u{1E}String.printable?\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.printable?(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether every character is printable.\u{1F}alx> String.printable?(\"abc\")\ntrue\u{1E}String.replace\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.replace(string, string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces every occurrence of a text, or every match of a regular expression.\u{1F}alx> String.replace(\"a-b-c\", \"-\", \"+\")\n\"a+b+c\"\nalx> String.replace(\"a1b22c\", ~r/\\d+/, \"\#\")\n\"a\#b\#c\"\u{1E}String.replace_leading\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.replace_leading(string, string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces every repetition of a text at the start.\u{1F}alx> String.replace_leading(\"aaab\", \"a\", \"x\")\n\"xxxb\"\u{1E}String.replace_prefix\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.replace_prefix(string, string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces a prefix once, when present.\u{1F}alx> String.replace_prefix(\"unhappy\", \"un\", \"\")\n\"happy\"\u{1E}String.replace_suffix\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.replace_suffix(string, string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces a suffix once, when present.\u{1F}alx> String.replace_suffix(\"main.alc\", \".alc\", \".go\")\n\"main.go\"\u{1E}String.replace_trailing\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.replace_trailing(string, string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Replaces every repetition of a text at the end.\u{1F}alx> String.replace_trailing(\"abbb\", \"b\", \"x\")\n\"axxx\"\u{1E}String.reverse\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.reverse(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Reverses the characters.\u{1F}alx> String.reverse(\"abc\")\n\"cba\"\u{1E}String.slice\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.slice(string, Range) -> string\u{1D}String.slice(string, integer, integer) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The substring of a length starting at a zero-based position, or at a range of positions, counted in characters; a negative position counts from the end.\u{1F}alx> String.slice(\"hello world\", 6..-1)\n\"world\"\nalx> String.slice(\"alchemy\", 2, 4)\n\"chem\"\nalx> String.slice(\"héllo\", 1, 9)\n\"éllo\"\u{1E}String.split\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.split(string) -> list[string]\u{1D}String.split(string, string) -> list[string]\u{1D}String.split(string, regex, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> list[string]\u{1D}String.split(string, regex, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> list[string]\u{1D}String.split(string, string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> list[string]\u{1D}String.split(string, string, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits on whitespace, on a separator, or wherever a regular expression matches. [parts: n] stops after n pieces, the last holding the rest; [trim: true] leaves out empty pieces.\u{1F}alx> String.split(\"k=v=w\", \"=\", [parts: 2])\n[\"k\", \"v=w\"]\nalx> String.split(\",a,,b\", \",\", [trim: true])\n[\"a\", \"b\"]\nalx> String.split(\" a  b \")\n[\"a\", \"b\"]\nalx> String.split(\"a,b,,c\", \",\")\n[\"a\", \"b\", \"\", \"c\"]\nalx> String.split(\"a, b;c\", ~r/[,;]\\s*/)\n[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\u{1E}String.split_at\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.split_at(string, integer) -> tuple[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits into two at a position.\u{1F}alx> String.split_at(\"alchemy\", 2)\n{\"al\", \"chemy\"}\u{1E}String.splitter\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.splitter(string, string) -> stream[string]\u{1D}String.splitter(string, enum[string]) -> stream[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits lazily at a pattern, or at any of a list of patterns, giving a stream.\u{1F}alx> String.splitter(\"red,green blue\", [\",\", \" \"]) |> Enum.to_list()\n[\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]\u{1E}String.starts_with\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.starts_with(string, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string starts with the prefix. Same as String.starts_with?.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with(\"alchemy\", \"al\")\ntrue\u{1E}String.starts_with?\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.starts_with?(string, string) -> boolean\u{1D}String.starts_with?(string, list[string]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string starts with the prefix, or with any of a list of prefixes.\u{1F}alx> String.starts_with?(\"alchemy\", \"al\")\ntrue\nalx> String.starts_with?(\"alchemy\", [\"gold\", \"alc\"])\ntrue\u{1E}String.to_atom\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.to_atom(string) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}The atom with the given name.\u{1F}alx> String.to_atom(\"ok\") == :ok\ntrue\u{1E}String.to_float\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.to_float(string) -> float\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses a float; fails when the text is not one. Float.parse returns a result instead.\u{1F}alx> String.to_float(\"2.5\")\n2.5\u{1E}String.to_integer\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.to_integer(string) -> integer\u{1D}String.to_integer(string, integer) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses an integer, optionally in a base; fails when the text is not one. Integer.parse returns a result instead.\u{1F}alx> String.to_integer(\"42\")\n42\nalx> String.to_integer(\"ff\", 16)\n255\u{1E}String.trim\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.trim(string) -> string\u{1D}String.trim(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes whitespace, or repetitions of a text, from both ends.\u{1F}alx> String.trim(\"  hi \\n\")\n\"hi\"\nalx> String.trim(\"xxhixx\", \"x\")\n\"hi\"\u{1E}String.trim_leading\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.trim_leading(string) -> string\u{1D}String.trim_leading(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes whitespace, or repetitions of a text, from the start.\u{1F}alx> String.trim_leading(\"  hi \")\n\"hi \"\u{1E}String.trim_trailing\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.trim_trailing(string) -> string\u{1D}String.trim_trailing(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Removes whitespace, or repetitions of a text, from the end.\u{1F}alx> String.trim_trailing(\"  hi \")\n\"  hi\"\u{1E}String.upcase\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.upcase(string) -> string\u{1D}String.upcase(string, atom) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Upper-cases the string.\u{1F}alx> String.upcase(\"hello\")\n\"HELLO\"\u{1E}String.valid?\u{1F}String\u{1F}String.valid?(string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the string is valid UTF-8.\u{1F}alx> String.valid?(\"héllo\")\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.alive\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.alive(supervisor[t]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the supervisor is running.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\nalx> Supervisor.alive(group)\nfalse\u{1E}Supervisor.await\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.await(supervisor[t], integer) -> tuple[boolean,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for the supervisor to end, returning whether it did and why.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\nalx> {ended, _} = Supervisor.await(group, 1000)\nalx> ended\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.child\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.child(supervisor[t], string) -> pid[t]\u{1F}\u{1F}The current pid of a named child, for sending it messages.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Process.alive(Supervisor.child(group, \"worker\"))\ntrue\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.children\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.children(supervisor[t]) -> list[tuple[string,integer]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Each child's name with its current process id, in declaration order.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Enum.map(Supervisor.children(group), fn {name, _} -> name end)\n[\"worker\"]\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.restarts\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.restarts(supervisor[t]) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}How many restarts the supervisor has performed.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Supervisor.restarts(group)\n0\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.server\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.server(atom, string, s, fn[s,q,tuple[s,r]]) -> tuple[string,string,fn[pid[tuple[]],integer]]\u{1F}\u{1F}A child for Supervisor.start that runs a server under a name: the child registers the server, Supervisor.start returns once it has, and Server.whereis finds whichever server is running. A failing server makes its child fail, so the strategy and the policy (\"permanent\", \"transient\" or \"temporary\") decide the restart, which begins from the initial state. Servers of different types can share one supervisor.\u{1F}alx> tree = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [Supervisor.server(:doc_total, \"permanent\", 0, fn(total: integer, n: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + n, total + n} end)], 1)\nalx> found: option[server[integer, integer]] = Server.whereis(:doc_total)\nalx> case found do\n...>   some(total) -> Server.call(total, 5, 1000)\n...>   none -> error(\"not found\")\n...> end\nok(5)\nalx> Supervisor.stop(tree, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.start\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.start(string, integer, integer, list[tuple[string,string,fn[pid[t],integer]]], integer) -> supervisor[t]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts children under a strategy (\"one_for_one\", \"one_for_all\" or \"rest_for_one\"), giving up after max_restarts within window_ms. Each child is {name, restart_policy, function}, with policy \"permanent\", \"transient\" or \"temporary\"; the last argument is each child's mailbox capacity.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Supervisor.alive(group)\ntrue\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.start_link\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.start_link(string, integer, integer, list[tuple[string,string,fn[pid[t],integer]]], integer) -> supervisor[t]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like Supervisor.start, and also links the supervisor to the process starting it.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start_link(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}Supervisor.stop\u{1F}Supervisor\u{1F}Supervisor.stop(supervisor[t], integer) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops the supervisor and its children, waiting up to the given milliseconds; false when the wait ran out.\u{1F}alx> group = Supervisor.start(\"one_for_one\", 3, 1000, [{\"worker\", \"permanent\", fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do Process.sleep(60000) end}], 4)\nalx> Supervisor.stop(group, 1000)\ntrue\u{1E}System.argv\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.argv() -> list[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The command-line arguments after the program name.\u{1F}alx> length(System.argv()) >= 0\ntrue\u{1E}System.cmd\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.cmd(string, enum[string]) -> tuple[string,integer]\u{1D}System.cmd(string, enum[string], list[tuple[atom,string]]) -> tuple[string,integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs a program with arguments and returns its combined output with its exit status.\u{1F}alx> System.cmd(\"echo\", [\"hello\"])\n{\"hello\\n\", 0}\u{1E}System.collect_garbage\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.collect_garbage()\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs a full collection now and waits for it, so that System.memory then counts only what the program still refers to. Programs do not need it to run well; it is for measuring.\u{1F}alx> before = Map.fetch!(System.memory(), :collections)\nalx> System.collect_garbage()\nalx> Map.fetch!(System.memory(), :collections) > before\ntrue\u{1E}System.convert_time_unit\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.convert_time_unit(integer, atom, atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Converts an amount of time between units, rounding down.\u{1F}alx> System.convert_time_unit(2, :second, :millisecond)\n2000\nalx> System.convert_time_unit(1500, :millisecond, :second)\n1\u{1E}System.cwd!\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.cwd!() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The working directory.\u{1F}alx> System.cwd!() == File.cwd!()\ntrue\u{1E}System.env\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.env(string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The value of an environment variable. Same as System.get_env.\u{1F}alx> System.env(\"ALCHEM_NO_SUCH_VARIABLE\")\nnone\u{1E}System.find_executable\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.find_executable(string) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The full path of a program on PATH.\u{1F}alx> System.find_executable(\"alchem-no-such-program\")\nnone\u{1E}System.get_env\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.get_env(string) -> option[string]\u{1D}System.get_env(string, string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The value of an environment variable as an option, or the default when it is unset.\u{1F}alx> System.put_env(\"ALCHEM_DOC_GET\", \"1\")\nalx> System.get_env(\"ALCHEM_DOC_GET\")\nsome(\"1\")\nalx> System.get_env(\"ALCHEM_NO_SUCH_VARIABLE\", \"fallback\")\n\"fallback\"\u{1E}System.halt\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.halt(integer)\u{1F}\u{1F}Ends the program immediately with an exit status.\u{1F}\u{1E}System.memory\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.memory() -> map[atom,integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The program's memory in bytes, as the runtime accounts for it: in_use is what its values occupy, including garbage not yet collected; held is what it has from the operating system and has not handed back; idle is the part of held that is free; stacks is what process stacks use; collections counts the collections run so far. in_use that keeps rising after System.collect_garbage is a leak; held far above in_use is an idle heap.\u{1F}alx> memory = System.memory()\nalx> Map.fetch!(memory, :in_use) > 0 and Map.fetch!(memory, :held) >= Map.fetch!(memory, :idle)\ntrue\nalx> Enum.sort(Map.keys(memory))\n[:collections, :held, :idle, :in_use, :stacks]\u{1E}System.monotonic_time\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.monotonic_time() -> integer\u{1D}System.monotonic_time(atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}A clock that only moves forward, for measuring: in nanoseconds, or in a unit, :second, :millisecond, :microsecond or :nanosecond.\u{1F}alx> System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) >= 0\ntrue\u{1E}System.os_time\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.os_time() -> integer\u{1D}System.os_time(atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The operating system's clock, as for system_time.\u{1F}alx> System.os_time(:second) > 1700000000\ntrue\u{1E}System.pid\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.pid() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The operating system's process identifier, as text.\u{1F}alx> String.length(System.pid()) > 0\ntrue\u{1E}System.put_env\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.put_env(string, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets an environment variable for this program and the commands it runs.\u{1F}alx> System.put_env(\"ALCHEM_DOC_PUT\", \"on\")\nalx> System.get_env(\"ALCHEM_DOC_PUT\", \"\")\n\"on\"\u{1E}System.stop\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.stop(integer)\u{1F}\u{1F}Ends the program with a status after an orderly shutdown: the main process stops as if it had returned, and the other processes are then stopped in the usual way. System.halt ends it at once.\u{1F}alx> stop = fn(code: integer): boolean do\n...>   System.stop(code)\n...>   true\n...> end\u{1E}System.system_time\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.system_time() -> integer\u{1D}System.system_time(atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The time since 1970 in nanoseconds, or in a unit as for monotonic_time.\u{1F}alx> System.system_time(:second) > 1700000000\ntrue\u{1E}System.tmp_dir\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.tmp_dir() -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}A directory for temporary files.\u{1F}alx> System.tmp_dir() != none\ntrue\u{1E}System.tmp_dir!\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.tmp_dir!() -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}A directory for temporary files; fails when there is none.\u{1F}alx> System.tmp_dir!() != \"\"\ntrue\u{1E}System.user_home\u{1F}System\u{1F}System.user_home() -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The user's home directory.\u{1F}alx> System.user_home() != none\ntrue\u{1E}TCP.accept\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.accept(socket, integer) -> result[socket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for a connection to a listening socket.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> case TCP.accept(listener, 10) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"connected\"\n...>   error(reason) -> reason\n...> end\n\"timeout\"\u{1E}TCP.address\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.address(socket) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The local address of a socket, host:port.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> String.contains?(TCP.address(listener), \":\")\ntrue\u{1E}TCP.close\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.close(socket) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes a listener or connection; false when it was already closed. Sockets are also closed when the program ends.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> TCP.close(listener)\ntrue\nalx> TCP.close(listener)\nfalse\u{1E}TCP.connect\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.connect(string, integer) -> result[socket,string]\u{1D}TCP.connect(string, integer, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> result[socket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Connects to an address, waiting up to the given milliseconds.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> server = Task.async(fn ->\n...>   ok(client) = TCP.accept(listener, 1000)\n...>   ok(line) = TCP.recv_line(client, 1000)\n...>   TCP.send(client, String.upcase(line) <> \"\\n\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(conn) = TCP.connect(TCP.address(listener), 1000)\nalx> TCP.send(conn, \"hi\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> TCP.recv_line(conn, 1000)\nok(\"HI\")\u{1E}TCP.connect_tls\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.connect_tls(string, integer) -> result[socket,string]\u{1D}TCP.connect_tls(string, integer, list[tuple[atom,string]]) -> result[socket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Connects to an address and makes the TLS handshake, both within the given milliseconds; the socket is then used as any other, and what it sends and receives is encrypted. The certificate must be for the host of the address and from an authority the system trusts. Options, as text: ca_file: a PEM file of certificates to trust instead, insecure: \"true\" to trust any, and server_name: the name the certificate must be for.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn _ -> HTTP.text(200, \"plain\") end)\nalx> case TCP.connect_tls(HTTP.address(server), 1000) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"encrypted\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"not a TLS server\"\n...> end\n\"not a TLS server\"\u{1E}TCP.controlling_process\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.controlling_process(socket, pid[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Hands a socket to another process, whose end then closes it instead of the opener's.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> keeper: pid[integer] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[integer]): integer do\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end, 1)\nalx> TCP.controlling_process(listener, keeper)\ntrue\u{1E}TCP.listen\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.listen(string) -> result[socket,string]\u{1D}TCP.listen(string, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> result[socket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Listens on an address, host:port; port 0 picks a free one, which TCP.address reports.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> String.starts_with?(TCP.address(listener), \"127.0.0.1:\")\ntrue\u{1E}TCP.peer\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.peer(socket) -> option[string]\u{1F}\u{1F}The address of the other end of a connection; none for a listener.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> TCP.peer(listener)\nnone\u{1E}TCP.recv\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.recv(socket, integer, integer) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads exactly the given number of bytes, or with 0 whatever has arrived once anything has, waiting up to the given milliseconds. error(\"closed\") once the other end has closed.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> server = Task.async(fn ->\n...>   ok(client) = TCP.accept(listener, 1000)\n...>   ok(line) = TCP.recv_line(client, 1000)\n...>   TCP.send(client, String.upcase(line) <> \"\\n\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(conn) = TCP.connect(TCP.address(listener), 1000)\nalx> TCP.send(conn, \"xy\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> TCP.recv(conn, 0, 1000)\nok(\"XY\\n\")\nalx> TCP.recv(conn, 0, 1000)\nerror(\"closed\")\u{1E}TCP.recv_line\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.recv_line(socket, integer) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Reads a line, without its newline or a carriage return before it, waiting up to the given milliseconds.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> server = Task.async(fn ->\n...>   ok(client) = TCP.accept(listener, 1000)\n...>   ok(line) = TCP.recv_line(client, 1000)\n...>   TCP.send(client, String.upcase(line) <> \"\\n\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(conn) = TCP.connect(TCP.address(listener), 1000)\nalx> TCP.send(conn, \"line\\r\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> TCP.recv_line(conn, 1000)\nok(\"LINE\")\u{1E}TCP.send\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.send(socket, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes text to a connection.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> server = Task.async(fn ->\n...>   ok(client) = TCP.accept(listener, 1000)\n...>   ok(line) = TCP.recv_line(client, 1000)\n...>   TCP.send(client, String.upcase(line) <> \"\\n\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(conn) = TCP.connect(TCP.address(listener), 1000)\nalx> TCP.send(conn, \"abc\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> TCP.recv(conn, 4, 1000)\nok(\"ABC\\n\")\u{1E}TCP.setopts\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.setopts(socket, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1D}TCP.setopts(socket, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sets socket options. active: true sends every packet to the controlling process as TCP.Event.data(text), then TCP.Event.closed or TCP.Event.error(reason); active: :once sends one and makes the socket passive again; active: n sends n; active: false stops. The controlling process is the one given to controlling_process, or the opener, and its messages must be TCP.Event. packet: :raw (the default) delivers what has arrived, packet: :line a line with its newline, and packet: 1, 2 or 4 a packet with a length header of that many bytes, which TCP.send adds. TCP.recv with length 0 reads one packet.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> server = Task.async(fn ->\n...>   ok(client) = TCP.accept(listener, 1000)\n...>   ok(line) = TCP.recv_line(client, 1000)\n...>   TCP.send(client, String.upcase(line) <> \"\\n\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(conn) = TCP.connect(TCP.address(listener), 1000)\nalx> me: pid[TCP.Event] = Process.self(8)\nalx> TCP.setopts(conn, [active: :once])\nok({})\nalx> TCP.send(conn, \"hi\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> Process.receive(me, 1000)\n{true, TCP.Event.data(\"HI\\n\")}\nalx> TCP.setopts(conn, [packet: 4])\nok({})\u{1E}TCP.shutdown\u{1F}TCP\u{1F}TCP.shutdown(socket, atom) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes one direction of a connection: :write tells the other end there is no more to read, :read stops reading, :read_write closes it.\u{1F}alx> ok(listener) = TCP.listen(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> server = Task.async(fn ->\n...>   ok(client) = TCP.accept(listener, 1000)\n...>   ok(line) = TCP.recv_line(client, 1000)\n...>   TCP.send(client, String.upcase(line) <> \"\\n\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(conn) = TCP.connect(TCP.address(listener), 1000)\nalx> TCP.send(conn, \"bye\\n\")\nok({})\nalx> TCP.shutdown(conn, :write)\nok({})\nalx> TCP.recv_line(conn, 1000)\nok(\"BYE\")\u{1E}Task.async\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.async(fn[a]) -> task[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function in its own process, linked to the caller, and returns a task to await. A task that fails takes its caller with it, unless the caller traps exits; Task.Supervisor.async_nolink starts one that does not.\u{1F}alx> task = Task.async(fn -> 6 * 7 end)\nalx> Task.await(task)\n42\u{1E}Task.async_stream\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.async_stream(enum[a], fn[a,b]) -> stream[b]\u{1D}Task.async_stream(enum[a], fn[a,b], integer) -> stream[b]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function over each element concurrently, at most one task per processor (or the given number) at a time, yielding results in input order.\u{1F}alx> Task.async_stream([1, 2, 3], fn n -> n * n end) |> Enum.to_list()\n[1, 4, 9]\nalx> Task.async_stream(1..4, fn n -> n + 1 end, 2) |> Enum.sum()\n14\u{1E}Task.await\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.await(task[a]) -> a\u{1D}Task.await(task[a], integer) -> a\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits for a task's result, for 5 seconds or the given milliseconds. Fails the caller when the task failed or the time ran out.\u{1F}alx> Task.await(Task.async(fn -> \"done\" end), 1000)\n\"done\"\u{1E}Task.await_many\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.await_many(enum[task[a]]) -> list[a]\u{1D}Task.await_many(enum[task[a]], integer) -> list[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits for every task, returning the results in order.\u{1F}alx> Task.await_many([Task.async(fn -> 1 end), Task.async(fn -> 2 end)])\n[1, 2]\u{1E}Task.shutdown\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.shutdown(task[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops a task whose result is no longer wanted.\u{1F}alx> task = Task.async(fn -> Process.sleep(60000) end)\nalx> Task.shutdown(task)\ntrue\u{1E}Task.start\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.start(fn[a]) -> task[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function in its own process, not linked to the caller, for work nobody needs to wait for; the task can still be awaited or shut down.\u{1F}alx> task = Task.start(fn -> 5 end)\nalx> Task.await(task)\n5\u{1E}Task.start_link\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.start_link(fn[a]) -> task[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like Task.start, linked to the caller: a task that fails takes the caller with it.\u{1F}alx> task = Task.start_link(fn -> 8 end)\nalx> Task.await(task, 1000)\n8\u{1E}Task.yield\u{1F}Task\u{1F}Task.yield(task[a], integer) -> option[result[a,string]]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds: some(ok(value)) when the task finished, some(error(reason)) when it failed, none when it is still running.\u{1F}alx> Task.yield(Task.async(fn -> 42 end), 1000)\nsome(ok(42))\nalx> slow = Task.async(fn -> Process.sleep(60000) end)\nalx> Task.yield(slow, 1)\nnone\nalx> Task.shutdown(slow)\ntrue\u{1E}Task.Supervisor.async\u{1F}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Task.Supervisor.async(dynamic_supervisor, fn[a]) -> task[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function as a task under the supervisor, linked to the caller, and returns it to await.\u{1F}alx> tasks = Task.Supervisor.start_link()\nalx> Task.await(Task.Supervisor.async(tasks, fn -> 6 * 7 end))\n42\u{1E}Task.Supervisor.async_nolink\u{1F}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Task.Supervisor.async_nolink(dynamic_supervisor, fn[a]) -> task[a]\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function as a task under the supervisor, not linked to the caller: a failure is seen only by awaiting or yielding the task.\u{1F}alx> tasks = Task.Supervisor.start_link()\nalx> task = Task.Supervisor.async_nolink(tasks, fn -> div(1, 0) end)\nalx> case Task.yield(task, 1000) do\n...>   some(error(_)) -> \"failed\"\n...>   _ -> \"other\"\n...> end\n\"failed\"\u{1E}Task.Supervisor.children\u{1F}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Task.Supervisor.children(dynamic_supervisor) -> list[integer]\u{1F}\u{1F}The process ids of the supervisor's running tasks.\u{1F}alx> tasks = Task.Supervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(_) = Task.Supervisor.start_child(tasks, fn -> Process.sleep(60000) end)\nalx> length(Task.Supervisor.children(tasks))\n1\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.stop(tasks)\ntrue\u{1E}Task.Supervisor.start_child\u{1F}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Task.Supervisor.start_child(dynamic_supervisor, fn[a]) -> result[task[a],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a task under the supervisor that nobody needs to await, unlinked: ok(task), or error(\"max_children\") or error(\"supervisor is not running\").\u{1F}alx> tasks = Task.Supervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(task) = Task.Supervisor.start_child(tasks, fn -> 1 end)\nalx> Task.await(task)\n1\u{1E}Task.Supervisor.start_link\u{1F}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Task.Supervisor.start_link() -> dynamic_supervisor\u{1D}Task.Supervisor.start_link(list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> dynamic_supervisor\u{1F}\u{1F}Starts a task supervisor, with DynamicSupervisor's options such as max_children:.\u{1F}alx> tasks = Task.Supervisor.start_link()\nalx> Task.Supervisor.children(tasks)\n[]\nalx> limited = Task.Supervisor.start_link([max_children: 4])\nalx> DynamicSupervisor.stop(limited)\ntrue\u{1E}Task.Supervisor.terminate_child\u{1F}Task.Supervisor\u{1F}Task.Supervisor.terminate_child(dynamic_supervisor, task[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Stops one of the supervisor's tasks; false when it is not running there.\u{1F}alx> tasks = Task.Supervisor.start_link()\nalx> ok(task) = Task.Supervisor.start_child(tasks, fn -> Process.sleep(60000) end)\nalx> Task.Supervisor.terminate_child(tasks, task)\ntrue\nalx> Task.Supervisor.terminate_child(tasks, task)\nfalse\u{1E}assert\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}assert(boolean)\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails the test, or the program, when the condition is false. A failed comparison reports both sides.\u{1F}alx> assert(1 + 1 == 2)\nalx> assert(\"a\" in [\"a\", \"b\"])\u{1E}assert_in_delta\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}assert_in_delta(float, float, float)\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails unless two floats differ by at most the delta.\u{1F}alx> assert_in_delta(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3, 0.0001)\u{1E}assert_raise\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}assert_raise(string, fn[a])\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails unless the function raises with a message containing the text.\u{1F}alx> assert_raise(\"division by zero\", fn -> div(1, 0) end)\u{1E}assert_receive\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}assert_receive(pid[t], pattern)\u{1D}assert_receive(pid[t], pattern, integer)\u{1D}assert_receive(pid[t], pattern, integer, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}A statement that takes the first message in the current process's mailbox matching a pattern, waiting for one to arrive: 100 milliseconds unless --receive-timeout or a third argument says otherwise. Other messages stay in the mailbox in their order. Names in the pattern are bound for the rest of the test, ^name compares with an existing binding, and when adds a guard. A failure shows the pattern, the values of pinned names and the last ten messages in the mailbox; a fourth argument replaces its first line.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[tuple[string, integer]] = Process.self(8)\nalx> Process.send(me, {\"started\", 1}, 100)\ntrue\nalx> Process.send(me, {\"finished\", 42}, 100)\ntrue\nalx> assert_receive(me, {\"finished\", result})\nalx> result\n42\nalx> assert_receive(me, {_, count} when count < 10, 500, \"the start was never reported\")\nalx> count\n1\u{1E}assert_received\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}assert_received(pid[t], pattern)\u{1D}assert_received(pid[t], pattern, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Like assert_receive, but looks only at messages that have already arrived and does not wait. An argument after the pattern replaces the first line of the failure.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Process.send(me, 7, 100)\ntrue\nalx> assert_received(me, n when n > 5)\nalx> n\n7\u{1E}capture_io\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}capture_io(fn[a]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Runs the function and returns what was written to standard output meanwhile.\u{1F}alx> capture_io(fn -> IO.puts(\"hello\") end)\n\"hello\\n\"\u{1E}flunk\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}flunk(string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails the test with a message.\u{1F}alx> assert_raise(\"not reached\", fn -> flunk(\"not reached\") end)\u{1E}on_exit\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}on_exit(fn[a])\u{1F}\u{1F}Registers a function to run when the current test ends, whether it passed or failed; several run last first. A failure in one fails the test.\u{1F}alx> File.write!(\"doc_on_exit.txt\", \"temporary\")\nalx> on_exit(fn -> File.rm!(\"doc_on_exit.txt\") end)\nalx> File.exists?(\"doc_on_exit.txt\")\ntrue\u{1E}refute\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}refute(boolean)\u{1F}\u{1F}Fails when the condition is true.\u{1F}alx> refute(1 > 2)\u{1E}refute_receive\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}refute_receive(pid[t], pattern)\u{1D}refute_receive(pid[t], pattern, integer)\u{1D}refute_receive(pid[t], pattern, integer, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}A statement that fails if a message matching the pattern is in the current process's mailbox, or arrives within the wait. Use _ for any message. The arguments after the pattern are those of assert_receive.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[string] = Process.self(4)\nalx> Process.send(me, \"ok\", 100)\ntrue\nalx> refute_receive(me, \"error\", 10)\nalx> assert_receive(me, \"ok\")\nalx> refute_receive(me, _, 10)\u{1E}refute_received\u{1F}Testing\u{1F}refute_received(pid[t], pattern)\u{1D}refute_received(pid[t], pattern, string)\u{1F}\u{1F}Like refute_receive, but looks only at messages that have already arrived and does not wait.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[integer] = Process.self(4)\nalx> refute_received(me, _)\nalx> Process.send(me, 7, 100)\ntrue\nalx> refute_received(me, 8, \"an eight arrived\")\u{1E}Time.add\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.add(Time, integer) -> Time\u{1D}Time.add(Time, integer, atom) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a number of seconds, wrapping around midnight.\u{1F}alx> Time.add(~T[23:59:30], 45)\n~T[00:00:15]\u{1E}Time.after?\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.after?(Time, Time) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first time is later than the second.\u{1F}alx> Time.after?(~T[10:00:00], ~T[09:00:00])\ntrue\u{1E}Time.before?\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.before?(Time, Time) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the first time is earlier than the second.\u{1F}alx> Time.before?(~T[10:00:00], ~T[09:00:00])\nfalse\u{1E}Time.compare\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.compare(Time, Time) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns :lt, :eq or :gt as the first time is earlier than, equal to or later than the second.\u{1F}alx> Time.compare(~T[10:00:00], ~T[10:00:00])\n:eq\u{1E}Time.diff\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.diff(Time, Time) -> integer\u{1D}Time.diff(Time, Time, atom) -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}The number of seconds from the second time to the first.\u{1F}alx> Time.diff(~T[10:01:00], ~T[10:00:00])\n60\u{1E}Time.from_iso8601\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.from_iso8601(string) -> result[Time,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses HH:MM:SS text, returning ok(time) or error(reason).\u{1F}alx> Time.from_iso8601(\"13:04:05\")\nok(~T[13:04:05])\u{1E}Time.from_iso8601!\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.from_iso8601!(string) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses HH:MM:SS text; fails when it is invalid.\u{1F}alx> Time.from_iso8601!(\"13:04:05\")\n~T[13:04:05]\u{1E}Time.monotonic\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.monotonic() -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Milliseconds from a clock that never goes backwards, for measuring durations.\u{1F}alx> started = Time.monotonic()\nalx> Time.monotonic() >= started\ntrue\u{1E}Time.new\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.new(integer, integer, integer) -> result[Time,string]\u{1D}Time.new(integer, integer, integer, integer) -> result[Time,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds a time from hour, minute, second and optionally microsecond, returning an error when out of range.\u{1F}alx> Time.new(13, 4, 5)\nok(~T[13:04:05])\u{1E}Time.new!\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.new!(integer, integer, integer) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}Builds a time from hour, minute and second; fails when out of range.\u{1F}alx> Time.new!(13, 4, 5)\n~T[13:04:05]\u{1E}Time.now\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.now() -> integer\u{1F}\u{1F}Milliseconds since the Unix epoch.\u{1F}alx> Time.now() > 1600000000000\ntrue\u{1E}Time.shift\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.shift(Time, Duration) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}Moves a time of day by a duration of hours, minutes, seconds and microseconds, wrapping around midnight. A duration with date units fails.\u{1F}alx> Time.shift(~T[23:30:00], Duration.minutes(45))\n~T[00:15:00]\u{1E}Time.to_iso8601\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.to_iso8601(Time) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The time as HH:MM:SS text.\u{1F}alx> Time.to_iso8601(~T[13:04:05])\n\"13:04:05\"\u{1E}Time.to_string\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.to_string(Time) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The time as HH:MM:SS text.\u{1F}alx> Time.to_string(~T[13:04:05])\n\"13:04:05\"\u{1E}Time.truncate\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.truncate(Time, atom) -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}Drops precision: :second keeps no fraction, :millisecond thousandths.\u{1F}alx> Time.truncate(~T[10:00:00.123456], :millisecond)\n~T[10:00:00.123000]\u{1E}Time.utc_now\u{1F}Time\u{1F}Time.utc_now() -> Time\u{1F}\u{1F}The current time of day in UTC.\u{1F}alx> now = Time.utc_now()\nalx> Time.compare(now, now)\n:eq\u{1E}UDP.address\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.address(socket) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The local address of a UDP socket, host:port.\u{1F}alx> ok(socket) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> String.contains?(UDP.address(socket), \":\")\ntrue\u{1E}UDP.close\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.close(socket) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes a UDP socket; false when it was already closed.\u{1F}alx> ok(socket) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> UDP.close(socket)\ntrue\nalx> UDP.close(socket)\nfalse\u{1E}UDP.controlling_process\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.controlling_process(socket, pid[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Hands a UDP socket to another process, which then receives its events and whose end closes it.\u{1F}alx> ok(socket) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> keeper: pid[UDP.Event] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[UDP.Event]): integer do\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end, 1)\nalx> UDP.controlling_process(socket, keeper)\ntrue\u{1E}UDP.open\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.open(string) -> result[socket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Opens a UDP socket on an address, host:port; port 0 picks a free one, which UDP.address reports.\u{1F}alx> ok(socket) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> String.starts_with?(UDP.address(socket), \"127.0.0.1:\")\ntrue\u{1E}UDP.recv\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.recv(socket, integer) -> result[tuple[string,string],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for a datagram: ok({from, text}), or error(\"timeout\").\u{1F}alx> ok(socket) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> UDP.recv(socket, 10)\nerror(\"timeout\")\u{1E}UDP.send\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.send(socket, string, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a datagram to an address.\u{1F}alx> ok(a) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> ok(b) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> UDP.send(a, UDP.address(b), \"ping\")\nok({})\nalx> case UDP.recv(b, 1000) do\n...>   ok({_, text}) -> text\n...>   error(reason) -> reason\n...> end\n\"ping\"\u{1E}UDP.setopts\u{1F}UDP\u{1F}UDP.setopts(socket, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1D}UDP.setopts(socket, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}active: true, :once, false or a number sends datagrams to the controlling process as UDP.Event.datagram(from, text), as TCP.setopts does for TCP.\u{1F}alx> me: pid[UDP.Event] = Process.self(8)\nalx> ok(socket) = UDP.open(\"127.0.0.1:0\")\nalx> UDP.setopts(socket, [active: :once])\nok({})\nalx> UDP.send(socket, UDP.address(socket), \"self\")\nok({})\nalx> case Process.receive(me, 1000) do\n...>   {true, UDP.Event.datagram(_, text)} -> text\n...>   _ -> \"nothing\"\n...> end\n\"self\"\u{1E}URI.append_path\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.append_path(URI, string) -> URI\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a path, which starts with /, to the URI's path.\u{1F}alx> URI.to_string(URI.append_path(URI.parse(\"http://a.com/api\"), \"/v1\"))\n\"http://a.com/api/v1\"\u{1E}URI.append_query\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.append_query(URI, string) -> URI\u{1F}\u{1F}Adds a query to the URI's query, joined with &.\u{1F}alx> URI.to_string(URI.append_query(URI.parse(\"http://a.com/?x=1\"), \"y=2\"))\n\"http://a.com/?x=1&y=2\"\u{1E}URI.decode\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.decode(string) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes percent-encoding, returning an error for a malformed escape.\u{1F}alx> URI.decode(\"a%20b\")\nok(\"a b\")\nalx> case URI.decode(\"%zz\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"malformed\"\n...> end\n\"malformed\"\u{1E}URI.decode_query\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.decode_query(string) -> map[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes a query string into a map; a later pair replaces an earlier one with the same key.\u{1F}alx> URI.decode_query(\"q=a+b&page=2\")\n%{\"page\" => \"2\", \"q\" => \"a b\"}\u{1E}URI.decode_www_form\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.decode_www_form(string) -> result[string,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Decodes a form value, where + is a space.\u{1F}alx> URI.decode_www_form(\"a+b%26c\")\nok(\"a b&c\")\u{1E}URI.encode\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.encode(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Percent-encodes every character that is neither reserved nor unreserved in a URI, so an address keeps its structure.\u{1F}alx> URI.encode(\"https://example.com/a b?q=é\")\n\"https://example.com/a%20b?q=%C3%A9\"\u{1E}URI.encode_query\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.encode_query(map[string,string]) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes a map as a query string, keys in ascending order.\u{1F}alx> URI.encode_query(%{\"q\" => \"a b\", \"page\" => \"2\"})\n\"page=2&q=a+b\"\u{1E}URI.encode_www_form\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.encode_www_form(string) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Encodes text as a form value: everything but letters, digits and -_.~ is escaped, and a space becomes +.\u{1F}alx> URI.encode_www_form(\"a b&c=d\")\n\"a+b%26c%3Dd\"\u{1E}URI.merge\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.merge(URI, string) -> URI\u{1D}URI.merge(string, string) -> URI\u{1F}\u{1F}Resolves a reference against a base URI, as a browser resolves a link.\u{1F}alx> URI.to_string(URI.merge(\"http://a.com/x/y\", \"../z?q=1\"))\n\"http://a.com/z?q=1\"\u{1E}URI.new\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.new(string) -> result[URI,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Like URI.parse, returning an error for text that is not a well-formed address.\u{1F}alx> case URI.new(\"http://example.com/%zz\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"malformed\"\n...> end\n\"malformed\"\u{1E}URI.parse\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.parse(string) -> URI\u{1F}\u{1F}Splits an address into a URI struct: scheme, userinfo, host, port, path, query and fragment. Parts the address lacks are none; http, https, ws, wss and ftp get their usual port. Text that is not an address becomes a URI with only a path.\u{1F}alx> uri = URI.parse(\"https://ada@example.com:8443/a/b?q=1\#top\")\nalx> {uri.scheme, uri.userinfo, uri.host, uri.port}\n{some(\"https\"), some(\"ada\"), some(\"example.com\"), some(8443)}\nalx> {uri.path, uri.query, uri.fragment}\n{\"/a/b\", some(\"q=1\"), some(\"top\")}\nalx> URI.parse(\"http://example.com\").port\nsome(80)\nalx> URI.parse(\"/only/a/path\").host\nnone\u{1E}URI.to_string\u{1F}URI\u{1F}URI.to_string(URI) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a URI struct as text, leaving out the usual port of its scheme.\u{1F}alx> URI.to_string(URI.parse(\"https://example.com:443/a?b=1\"))\n\"https://example.com/a?b=1\"\nalx> uri = URI.parse(\"http://example.com/a\")\nalx> URI.to_string(%URI{uri | path: \"/b\", query: some(\"page=2\")})\n\"http://example.com/b?page=2\"\u{1E}Version.compare\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.compare(string, string) -> atom\u{1D}Version.compare(Version, Version) -> atom\u{1F}\u{1F}Returns :lt, :eq or :gt as the first version is lower than, equal to or higher than the second. A pre-release is lower than its release. Fails on an invalid version.\u{1F}alx> Version.compare(\"1.9.0\", \"1.10.0\")\n:lt\nalx> Version.compare(\"2.0.0-rc.1\", \"2.0.0\")\n:lt\nalx> Version.compare(\"1.0.0-alpha.2\", \"1.0.0-alpha.10\")\n:lt\nalx> Version.compare(\"3.1.4\", \"3.1.4\")\n:eq\u{1E}Version.match?\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.match?(string, string) -> boolean\u{1D}Version.match?(Version, string) -> boolean\u{1D}Version.match?(string, Version.Requirement) -> boolean\u{1D}Version.match?(Version, Version.Requirement) -> boolean\u{1D}Version.match?(string, string, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether a version meets a requirement: comparisons with ==, !=, >, >=, <, <= and ~>, joined by and and or. ~> 2.1 allows 2.1 and later within 2; ~> 2.1.3 allows 2.1.3 and later within 2.1. Fails on an invalid version or requirement.\u{1F}alx> Version.match?(\"2.3.1\", \"~> 2.1\")\ntrue\nalx> Version.match?(\"3.0.0\", \"~> 2.1\")\nfalse\nalx> Version.match?(\"2.1.9\", \"~> 2.1.3\")\ntrue\nalx> Version.match?(\"1.5.0\", \">= 1.0.0 and < 2.0.0\")\ntrue\nalx> Version.match?(\"0.9.0\", \">= 1.0.0 or == 0.9.0\")\ntrue\nalx> Version.match?(\"2.1.0-rc.1\", \"~> 2.0\", [allow_pre: false])\nfalse\nalx> Version.match?(Version.parse!(\"2.3.0\"), \"~> 2.1\")\ntrue\u{1E}Version.parse\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.parse(string) -> result[Version,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses a semantic version into a Version struct, with its pre-release identifiers and build metadata.\u{1F}alx> ok(v) = Version.parse(\"2.10.3\")\nalx> {v.major, v.minor, v.patch}\n{2, 10, 3}\nalx> ok(pre) = Version.parse(\"1.0.0-rc.1+build5\")\nalx> {pre.pre, pre.build}\n{[\"rc\", \"1\"], some(\"build5\")}\nalx> case Version.parse(\"1.2\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(reason) -> reason\n...> end\n\"invalid version \\\"1.2\\\"\"\u{1E}Version.parse!\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.parse!(string) -> Version\u{1F}\u{1F}Parses a semantic version into a Version struct, failing when it is not one.\u{1F}alx> Version.parse!(\"1.2.3\").minor\n2\u{1E}Version.parse_requirement\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.parse_requirement(string) -> result[Version.Requirement,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Checks a requirement once, giving a Version.Requirement to match versions against.\u{1F}alx> ok(req) = Version.parse_requirement(\"~> 2.0\")\nalx> Version.match?(\"2.4.0\", req)\ntrue\nalx> case Version.parse_requirement(\"bogus\") do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"valid\"\n...>   error(_) -> \"invalid\"\n...> end\n\"invalid\"\u{1E}Version.parse_requirement!\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.parse_requirement!(string) -> Version.Requirement\u{1F}\u{1F}Like Version.parse_requirement, failing on an invalid requirement.\u{1F}alx> Version.match?(\"1.0.0\", Version.parse_requirement!(\">= 1.0.0\"))\ntrue\u{1E}Version.to_string\u{1F}Version\u{1F}Version.to_string(Version) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}Writes a Version back as text.\u{1F}alx> Version.to_string(%Version{major: 2, minor: 0, patch: 1, pre: [\"beta\", \"2\"]})\n\"2.0.1-beta.2\"\u{1E}WebSocket.close\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.close(websocket) -> boolean\u{1D}WebSocket.close(websocket, integer, string) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Closes with code 1000, or with a code from 3000 to 4999 and a reason of at most 123 bytes, waiting up to a second for the other end to answer. false when the socket was already closed or another process is closing it: of several processes closing a socket at once, one gets true.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.close(ws)\ntrue\nalx> WebSocket.close(ws)\nfalse\nalx> WebSocket.send(ws, \"late\")\nerror(\"closed\")\u{1E}WebSocket.compressed?\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.compressed?(websocket) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Reports whether the handshake agreed to compress messages: the server upgraded with compress: and the client offered it.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   ok(socket) = WebSocket.upgrade(request, [compress: true])\n...>   WebSocket.recv(socket, 1000)\n...>   HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server), 1000, [compress: \"true\"])\nalx> WebSocket.compressed?(ws)\ntrue\nalx> ok(plain) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server), 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.compressed?(plain)\nfalse\u{1E}WebSocket.connect\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.connect(string, integer) -> result[websocket,string]\u{1D}WebSocket.connect(string, integer, list[tuple[atom,string]]) -> result[websocket,string]\u{1D}WebSocket.connect(string, integer, list[tuple[atom,string]], list[tuple[string,string]]) -> result[websocket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Opens a WebSocket to a ws:// or wss:// address, waiting up to the given milliseconds for the connection and the handshake. Options, as text: ca_file: a PEM file of certificates to trust, insecure: \"true\" to trust any, origin: the Origin header to send, and compress: \"true\" to offer permessage-deflate, which is used when the server accepts it. A list of {name, value} headers after the options is sent with the handshake, such as a Cookie or Authorization; the headers the handshake sets itself are refused.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.send(ws, \"hi\")\nok({})\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nok(WebSocket.Event.text(\"HI\"))\nalx> case WebSocket.connect(\"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server), 1000) do\n...>   ok(_) -> \"connected\"\n...>   error(reason) -> reason\n...> end\n\"a WebSocket address starts with ws:// or wss://\"\u{1E}WebSocket.controlling_process\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.controlling_process(websocket, pid[a]) -> boolean\u{1F}\u{1F}Hands a socket to another process, whose end then closes it and to which an active socket sends its events.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> keeper: pid[WebSocket.Event] = Process.spawn(fn(self: pid[WebSocket.Event]): integer do\n...>   Process.receive(self, 60000)\n...>   0\n...> end, 1)\nalx> WebSocket.controlling_process(ws, keeper)\ntrue\u{1E}WebSocket.peer\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.peer(websocket) -> string\u{1F}\u{1F}The address of the other end, host:port.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> String.starts_with?(WebSocket.peer(ws), \"127.0.0.1:\")\ntrue\u{1E}WebSocket.recv\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.recv(websocket, integer) -> result[WebSocket.Event,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Waits up to the given milliseconds for the next event: a text or binary message, WebSocket.Event.closed(code, reason) when the other end closes, or WebSocket.Event.error(reason) when the connection fails. After the last event it is error(\"closed\").\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 10)\nerror(\"timeout\")\nalx> WebSocket.send(ws, \"x\")\nok({})\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nok(WebSocket.Event.text(\"X\"))\nalx> WebSocket.close(ws)\ntrue\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nok(WebSocket.Event.closed(1000, \"\"))\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nerror(\"closed\")\u{1E}WebSocket.send\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.send(websocket, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a text message.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.send(ws, \"abc\")\nok({})\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nok(WebSocket.Event.text(\"ABC\"))\u{1E}WebSocket.send_binary\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.send_binary(websocket, string) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Sends a binary message: any bytes, which arrive as WebSocket.Event.binary.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, \"\\x01\\xff\")\nok({})\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nok(WebSocket.Event.binary(\"\\x01\\xff\"))\u{1E}WebSocket.setopts\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.setopts(websocket, list[tuple[atom,atom]]) -> result[tuple[],string]\u{1F}\u{1F}active: true sends every event to the controlling process as a WebSocket.Event message, active: :once sends one, and active: false stops. The controlling process is the one given to controlling_process, or the socket's maker, and its messages must be WebSocket.Event.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> me: pid[WebSocket.Event] = Process.self(8)\nalx> WebSocket.setopts(ws, [active: :once])\nok({})\nalx> WebSocket.send(ws, \"hi\")\nok({})\nalx> Process.receive(me, 1000)\n{true, WebSocket.Event.text(\"HI\")}\u{1E}WebSocket.upgrade\u{1F}WebSocket\u{1F}WebSocket.upgrade(HTTP.Request) -> result[websocket,string]\u{1D}WebSocket.upgrade(HTTP.Request, list[tuple[atom,boolean]]) -> result[websocket,string]\u{1D}WebSocket.upgrade(HTTP.Request, list[tuple[atom,integer]]) -> result[websocket,string]\u{1F}\u{1F}Called by an HTTP.serve handler, answers its request with the WebSocket handshake and returns the socket. The response the handler returns afterwards is not sent. A request that is not a WebSocket handshake is error(\"not a WebSocket request: ...\"), and the handler answers it as it likes. [max_message_bytes: n] bounds incoming messages (16 MB by default); a larger one closes the socket with code 1009. [compress: true], or compress: 1 beside max_message_bytes:, accepts permessage-deflate (RFC 7692) from a client that offers it, as browsers do: messages of 256 bytes or more are then sent compressed, each on its own, and the limit of a message applies to what it expands to. Check HTTP.header(request, \"origin\") before upgrading when only your own pages should connect.\u{1F}alx> ok(server) = HTTP.serve(\"127.0.0.1:0\", fn request ->\n...>   case WebSocket.upgrade(request) do\n...>     ok(ws) ->\n...>       answered: result[tuple[], string] = case WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000) do\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.text(text)) -> WebSocket.send(ws, String.upcase(text))\n...>         ok(WebSocket.Event.binary(data)) -> WebSocket.send_binary(ws, data)\n...>         _ -> ok({})\n...>       end\n...>       if answered == ok({}) do\n...>         WebSocket.recv(ws, 5000)\n...>       end\n...>       HTTP.text(200, \"\")\n...>     error(reason) -> HTTP.text(400, reason)\n...>   end\n...> end)\nalx> ok(ws) = WebSocket.connect(\"ws://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\", 1000)\nalx> WebSocket.send(ws, \"hello\")\nok({})\nalx> WebSocket.recv(ws, 1000)\nok(WebSocket.Event.text(\"HELLO\"))\nalx> ok(plain) = HTTP.get(\"http://\" <> HTTP.address(server) <> \"/echo\")\nalx> {plain.status, plain.body}\n{400, \"not a WebSocket request: no upgrade to websocket\"}"
  end

  def examples(): string do
    "Core\u{1F}    alx> inspect([some(1), none])\n    \"[some(1), none]\"\n    alx> 5 |> then(fn n -> n * 2 end)\n    10\n\u{1E}Collections\u{1F}    alx> Enum.map([1, 2, 3], fn n -> n * 2 end)\n    [2, 4, 6]\n    alx> Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, fn n, acc -> acc + n end)\n    6\n    alx> Enum.group_by([1, 2, 3, 4], &rem(&1, 2))\n    %{0 => [2, 4], 1 => [1, 3]}\n    alx> Stream.chunk_every(11..18, 3) |> Enum.to_list()\n    [[11, 12, 13], [14, 15, 16], [17, 18]]\n    alx> Map.update(%{\"a\" => 1}, \"a\", 0, &(&1 + 1))\n    %{\"a\" => 2}\n    alx> MapSet.new([3, 1, 3]) |> MapSet.to_list()\n    [1, 3]\n\u{1E}Text\u{1F}    alx> String.split(\"k=v=w\", \"=\", [parts: 2])\n    [\"k\", \"v=w\"]\n    alx> Regex.run(~r/(\\w+)@(\\w+)/, \"mail joe@example now\")\n    some([\"joe@example\", \"joe\", \"example\"])\n    alx> Integer.parse(\"42\")\n    ok(42)\n    alx> JSON.decode(\"[1, true]\")\n    ok(JSON.list([JSON.integer(1), JSON.boolean(true)]))\n\u{1E}Dates and times\u{1F}    alx> Date.add(~D[2026-02-27], 2)\n    ~D[2026-03-01]\n    alx> DateTime.from_iso8601(\"2026-01-05T13:04:05+02:00\")\n    ok({~U[2026-01-05T11:04:05Z], 7200})\n\u{1E}Files and system\u{1F}    alx> File.write!(\"doc_read.txt\", \"hello\")\n    alx> File.read(\"doc_read.txt\")\n    ok(\"hello\")\n    alx> File.write!(\"doc_stream.txt\", \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\")\n    alx> File.stream!(\"doc_stream.txt\") |> Stream.map(&String.trim(&1)) |> Enum.take(2)\n    [\"one\", \"two\"]\n    alx> Path.join(\"src\", \"main.alc\")\n    \"src/main.alc\"\n    alx> System.cmd(\"echo\", [\"hello\"])\n    {\"hello\\n\", 0}\n\u{1E}Concurrency\u{1F}    alx> Task.async_stream([1, 2, 3], fn n -> n * n end) |> Enum.to_list()\n    [1, 4, 9]\n    alx> counter = Server.start(0, fn(total: integer, amount: integer): tuple[integer, integer] do {total + amount, total + amount} end)\n    alx> Server.call(counter, 5, 1000)\n    ok(5)\n    alx> counter = Agent.start(fn -> 10 end)\n    alx> Agent.get_and_update(counter, fn n -> {n, n + 1} end)\n    10\n\u{1E}Testing\u{1F}    alx> assert(1 + 1 == 2)\n    alx> assert(\"a\" in [\"a\", \"b\"])\n    alx> assert_raise(\"division by zero\", fn -> div(1, 0) end)\n\u{1E}Network\u{1F}"
  end

  def targets(): string do
    "claude\u{1F}Claude Code skill\u{1F}.claude/skills/alchem/SKILL.md\u{1F}.claude/skills/alchem/SKILL.md\u{1F}\u{1F}---\nname: alchem\ndescription: Write, check, test and document Alchem (.alc) programs. Use whenever a task touches .alc files or the alchem command.\n---\n\n\u{1E}cursor\u{1F}Cursor rule\u{1F}.cursor/rules/alchem.mdc\u{1F}\u{1F}\u{1F}---\ndescription: Write, check, test and document Alchem (.alc) programs. Use whenever a task touches .alc files or the alchem command.\nglobs: \"**/*.alc\"\nalwaysApply: false\n---\n\n\u{1E}windsurf\u{1F}Windsurf rule\u{1F}.windsurf/rules/alchem.md\u{1F}\u{1F}\u{1F}\u{1E}cline\u{1F}Cline rule\u{1F}.clinerules/alchem.md\u{1F}\u{1F}\u{1F}\u{1E}copilot\u{1F}GitHub Copilot instructions (managed block)\u{1F}.github/copilot-instructions.md\u{1F}\u{1F}shared\u{1F}\u{1E}agents\u{1F}AGENTS.md, read by many agents (managed block)\u{1F}AGENTS.md\u{1F}AGENTS.md\u{1F}shared\u{1F}"
  end

  def skill(): string do
    "\# Alchem\n\nAlchem is a statically typed, Elixir-inspired language that compiles to native executables. Source files end in `.alc`. Its syntax resembles Elixir but it is a different language: types are checked at compile time, there is no `nil`, macros are templates rather than code run by the compiler, and the standard library is its own. Do not assume Elixir or Hex behaviour; look it up.\n\nThe `alchem` command documents itself, and what it prints always matches the installed compiler. Before writing or changing Alchem code, run:\n\n```\nalchem agent-guide\n```\n\nIt lists the commands, the syntax topics and the library modules, and how to ask for more:\n\n- `alchem syntax <topic>`: one construct with its rules and a working example (`alchem syntax` lists the topics).\n- `alchem doc <Module>`: the functions of a library module; `alchem doc <Module.function>`: signatures and examples.\n- `alchem doc --group <group>` and `alchem syntax --group <group>`: one group of modules or topics, with what each is for.\n- `alchem doc --search <text>`: find functions and topics by name or description.\n- `alchem doc <file.alc>`: the documentation written in a project file.\n\nWhile working:\n\n1. `alchem check --json <entry.alc>` after each change; fix every diagnostic before going on.\n2. `alchem fmt <path>` to format.\n3. `alchem test <path>` to run `*_test.alc` files; add `--seed N` to repeat a failing order.\n4. Document public functions with `@doc` and `alx>` examples, and run them with `doctest Module` in a test file.\n"
  end

  def generated(): string do
    "<!-- Generated by `alchem docs` from the compiler's own tables. Do not edit: change the documentation in the compiler and run `alchem docs` again. -->\n\n"
  end

  def hello(): string do
    "defmodule Hello do\n  def greeting(name: string): string do\n    \"hello, \#{name}\"\n  end\n\n  def main() do\n    IO.puts(greeting(\"world\"))\n  end\nend\n"
  end
